      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <p>I</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER</p>
        <p>MT Dun NELLIIII. Although it must be confessed that the weather Is far from wintry with regard to frost and snow, at one of the rinks the other day I could not help but notice what I considered an excellent skating costume. There is no doubt that it would also make an admirable walking dress if cut to the proper length. Surely it would not be too much to expect that a person would object to wearing it for both of these exercises. The skirt was plain, of black peau-de-soie lined with red glace silk, and it had a frill of the glace inside the hem. The short, well-fitting coat was of red cloth, fragged with black and piped with black satin. This lady's collar V7'.3 a high black astrachan one, which, perhaps, looked too dull had it not had a jabot of cream lace. The toque was black astrachan with the wings of a red bird. As if in keeping with the rest of the dress, the astrachan muff had two red camellias on it with two beautiful green leaves and a fan arrangement of cream lace.</p>
        <p>A friend of mine was wearing a fine toque last tight of mink fur, the brim of which was lined with old-rose velvet. Round the fur crown there were light folds of brown tulle, which also forms a full rosette in front between rosettes of the rose- coloured velvet, which have little bow-shaped paste brooches in the centre. Above these there is a long white paradise osprey. Another ha el a lovely hat of violet felt, trimmed with miroir velvet of the same delicate shade, on which were sewn narrow plaited trimmings of suede leather in a conventional design, and at the edge of the velvet there were accordion-pleated frills of chiffon. The folds of the velvet were caught in front with a -choice silver buckle, set with paste diamonds and under the brim at the back was a large bunch of velvet violets of a darker shade.</p>
        <p>There is not quite as much variety just now I "think in children's coats. They do not now seem to be made for ornamental purposes, but for their 'quality of wear. In these wintry days when the weather alternates between a great closeness and a storm of sleet, it is advisable that the coat should be of such a material that it can resist both. One that by giving sufficient protection from the cold and the other so that it can be opened easily and at the same time fit comfortably and well. The child I saw yesterday afternoon had a double- breasted coat, reaching down to her knees. It had but three buttons down on either side. The coat was of cloth with velvet lapels and collars which could when desired to keep away the cold, be turned up so as to make the wearer feel comfort- able and warm. The little girl had on a large full hat with two large grey ostrich feathers protruding from the top. I should say she was from 10 to 12 years of age.</p>
        <p>Unless very dirty, it is a mistake to scub oil- ' cloth, as it very soon destroys the pattern, especially if this is at all raised. It should be well rubbed with a flannel wrung out in warm until clean, and then polished dry with a soft linen cloth. To restore the shiny appearance, use a little pure linseed oil—it does not evaporate like paraffin, besides which it preserves the oilcloth and prevents it from cracking. Another good thing for this purpose is an ounce of beeswax shredded into a saucer, covered with turpentine, and melted. Rub the surface of the floorcloth with this after cleaning, and the result is most satis- iactory. Many a housewife may be glad of this excellent hint regarding flies. Take a piece of flannel satur- ated with paraffin, rub it lightly over mirrors, picture frames, and overmantles, and let it dry of its own accord. You will not be even troubled cleaning these, for flies will not go near paraffin. This will bring my letter to a conclusion this week.</p>
        <p>Your loving JENNIE.</p>
        <p>TO COOK AN OLD FOWL.—If a fowl is old and tough it may be mad.e.ten i r'er by wrapping it care- fully n a double thickness of wrapping paper, pinning it so that it cannot come undone and roasting it for a full hour; then take it out of the oven, remove the paper, and roast until is is per- fectly tender, basting it and turning it frequently. CUSTARD PUDDING WITH CRUST.—Required: A pint of milk, two eggs, sugar, nutmeg, a little pastry. Method: Line a small pie-dish with short crust or puff paste; beat up the eggs, strain them to the milk, add sugar to taste, and a grating of nutmeg. Pour into the pie-dish and bake in a slow Oven for three-quarters of an hour.</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS,</p>
