      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <p>C</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT.'</p>
        <p>Many gowns for the first Drawing Room which will be held on the. 24th of next month, at Buckinghatn Palaee, are in course of construction, the wise ones realising that they can command far more attention now than a little later on, when the rush and drive will, asusual,be tremendous. Some of the wraps en evidence, are indescribably lovely. These are all most cosily lined, of course, with a view to protecting the wearers from chills during the long weary wait in the Mall. Every year, some amount of discontent is given ex- pression to, ty dissatisfied women, attending the Draw- ing Rooms, concerning the long wait in their carriages in the Mall, in a conditien of full dress. They seem to overlook the fact that there is no necessity for any wcman to remain one single instant in the Mall. The only object for doing so, is to get to the Palace earlier than would otherwise be possible, except for those who possess the co serted privilege of the entree. And as to the uncovered neck and shboulders, in which they have to appear befere the Queen, there is no law, written or unwritten, which forbids women to shroud the neck and shoulders with shawls or even Vaikets, if they choose, before they enter the Palace, though it is true they have to leave all such in the Bow Library, before proceeding to the Throne Room. The Throne Room is upholstered in crimson silk, and the ceiling decorated to represent the war of the Roses. The Empress Frederick was christened in this apart- ment, and durifig the married life of the Queen, the , members of the Privy Council assembled there, for their I meetings. Since the death of the Prince Consort, Her Majesty has held the Drawing Rooms there. Before a, function of this description, the Royal Family assemble in an apartment known as the Sovereign's Closet, the walls of which are covered with valuable and interest- ing miniatures, and at the proper time they walk in procession to the Throne Room, conducted by the Great Officers of the Household. Her Majesty never now joins in the Royal procession, but is wheeled in a cnair, direct from her own apartments, and she remains seated throughout the Drawing Room. The •tenants ball, at Arundel Castle, prepared on so lavish a scale, lends colour to the run,our that the Duke of Norfolk is about to emerge from the gloom with which be has surrounded himself, since the death of the Duchess. The Duke was devoted to his wife, but lost her shortly after the birth of tbeir only child. This child was born dumb and blind, another life long grief to the Duke. Be is however deveted to the young Earl, ' who in return adores his father, and the two have alwayn been inseparable. The late Duchess of Norfolk was perhaps the plainest woman in society, but so kind and charitable that everyone liked her. Madame Lemens Sherrington, the once famous singer, who ir,e; now permanently taken up her abode in Brus- sels, wilt be much missed by her many friends and acquaintances in this country. Her sister, Miss Grace Sherrington, ;a still in London, and her daughter Mary remains at Manchester, but her youngest daughter has just married a young Belgian lawyer, and will settle in Brus els,near her mother. Madame Lemens Sherrington is sixty years of age, but in appearance would easily pass for quite ten years younger. Among society women, amateur vocalists are few and far batween at the present time. Mrs. William Dun- eombe has a lovely voice, but it is understood she has now joined the ranks of professionals, and Mrs. Ronald's voice,once second to few, seems to be a thing of the past. A few good piano players though are still in our midst. Lady Randolph Cnurchill, and the Hon. Blanche Tollemache are both first class performers. An excellent amateur violin performer is Miss Maud Graves, while the finest woman bagpipe player we have is Miss Espeth Campbell, a niece of the Princess Louise, Marchioness of Lorne.</p>
        <p>The most up-to-date millinery is adorned (?) with the head and wings of the owl, which is indeed a matter for regret. The bird is unquestionably one of the farmer's most valuable friends. It will kill more rats in one night than a terrier in a weet,, and if Dame Fashion demands the plumage of the bird for hats and toques, the country districts will soon be depopulated of their b-,st protect'on against vermin, which do great damage to the crops. To remonstrate is useless as was clearly ahown in the cas of the osprey.—What is needed is a good example in high quarters. If the various leaders of fashion would refuse to allow their headgear to be ornamented(?) with</p>
        <p>the owl, all the rest wourd follow suit. But while those who lead the way patronise the mode,cost what it may, others will imitate them.</p>
        <p>Why it_is_we are content to ray the original price o'</p>
        <p>the model, for wearing the fiftieth or bundredth copy of it, ever will be a deep impenetrable mystery. We all know that a copy of a great master, is practically worthkss by comparison, and who is the collector that sets any store by imitation china ? Yet women are happy to be gowned and hatted like scores of others, with whom they come in contact. They will willingly pay thirty or forty guineas for a gown so long as it is of a cut and color similar to half a hundred that they meet with every day, but would look askance at an artistic confection tbat they bad not admirel on scores of figures, the very antithesis of their own. Fancy jewels, as those are called which are of little instrinsic value, are worn in great profusion. There never was such an age of glitter. A ball gown rowa- da.,"S is often a sheet of god or silver, and would en- chant the barbaric taste cf an African negress, or a. North American Indian squaw. For the coming draw- ing rooms, wh.le gowns are being sewn with the artifi- chi glitter of mock diamonds, emeralds, and rubies, and a bride of a week ago had her train fastened on with enormo,,s buckles of glass diamonds. Ererythiog is ejewelled, frnm hat crowns to shoe buckles, and the</p>
        <p>indtkry if mock jewels is likely to flourish long. Agate is enjoying a wide vogue at the moment, and is much seen in the long chains which four' a part of afternoon and evening dress. There is such a great variety in this charming stone, that most people can select a special kind for their own use.' The large geld and eramelled hearts that were so fashionable a short time ago, have put in an appearance at.;ain, fashioned in agate. They are occassionally mountdd in diamonds when they are really mcst fascinating little items. Amber is eceasionally seen on the well dressed, but by no means frequently. It was loved dearly by renhetics but since they vanished, it has suffered eclipse. Onyx is in favor for decorating umbrella handles, and other humble uses. The moonstone, the cernelian, and the chrysoprase, hover about the bwders of the fashionable world, occasionally entering it,one by one,being adopted for a season or EO, and ti,en discarded, no cue knows why, unless it is that they are forced to the front for</p>
        <p>THE MARVELLOUS POLYPHON MUSICAL ,AY BY THEMSELVES Ike ordinary musical boxes, but much au- ' perior tone. HUNDREDS or TUNES can be piayed. You can choose your own tunes. GEISHA AND UP-TO-DATE TUNES. Vices from 128.6 d. to £25.</p>
        <p>A. RCHER &amp; SONS, 43 TO 49, LORD-STREET, LIVERPOOL.</p>
