        <p>the purpose of trade. In agates, the variations, both of translucency,and of tint in the same stone,are eften bO gteat as to give much richness of appearance, and tais combined with the high polish, which they are capable of receiving, imparts great value to some specimens. These stonea generally occur in the form of detached rouaded nodules, in a variety of the trap-rocks called Amygdaloid. It is found in large quantities in Scotland and Germany, and is olso imported from Brazil and India. Frequently beeusiful markings, like drawings of moss, trees, or small landscapes occur in agates. Coloured centres have returned to favour somewhat, for the dinner table, which is a matter for congratula- tion, as it is far easier to make the festive board fascina- ting in appearance if one is permitted this little addi- tion. Experience tends to show that the most effective colour is pale yellow. Take a length of pale yellow silken fabric, allowing half as much again as the actual space to be covered, and puff it lightly and gracefully down the centre of the table. Upon this place the bon- bon dishes, flower bowls, filled with violets for prefer- ence, and candlesticks or lamps, the latter filled with yellow silk shades. Trails of smilax or asparagus fern, shoufd edge the puffed silk centre. CHESTNUT SOUP.—Take half a small head of celery, two onions, and a carrot, chop fine, and fry in butter till b. own. Add a quart of white stock, three or four cloves, and salt to taste. Simmer together one hour. Meantime take three dozen chestnut peel off the outer shell, and place the nuts in hot water on the fire,stirring until sufficiently soft to permit the inner, shell to come off easily. Stew them half an hour in the soup. After- wards set aside a third, leave them whole, chop the re- mainder, and strain them with the liquor through a sieve. Make hot again, then place the whole chestnuts in the bottom of the tureen and pour the soup over them. It should be of the consistency of very thick cream.</p>
