      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>rtiE EXAMINER FRIDAY, JANUARY 20 1899</head>
        <p>rtiE EXAMINER FRIDAY, JANUARY 20 1899</p>
        <p>(('opyright.)</p>
        <p>ant( I worked early anti late at tne aiggings, ana very likely 'a wife is just the sort of ballast he needs. Now I wonder where :I could find one to 'suit him? I think I'll go and ask Jane's advice 'about it. Three minutes later, already having forgotten that his wife's serenity had recently been ruffled, •he was knocking at the door of the showily- -furnished boudoir, which was supposed to have been very much improved in appearance since it was the sanctum-sanctorum of a Duchess. "Can I come in, Jane ? " he called out. "To be sure, dear," was the pleasant answer, which shewed that Jane was also a stranger to sulks. "I was just wanting to speak to you, for I have got such a wonderful idea about what to do with Polly and Teddy that it has very nearly taken my breath away." , "And what's it all about, eh? " "Well, sit down beside me, on this lounge, and I will tell you all about it. No, sit further back. I always think it spoils upholstered furniture to sit on the edge of it." "Good gracious, Jane, it wouldn't matter if we spoilt a dozen settees in a week. We have money enough to keep on buying more if they were wanted. But I don't blame you for being careful. If you hadn't always been careful in our younger days we should never have been where we are now. I remember-"</p>
        <p>"Now, look here, Joe, if you start any more remembrances I'll not speak to you again for a week. Either you will hold your tongue, or I will hold mine." "There, there, don't get into a stew, Jane. It would never do for you to have to hold your tongue for a whole week, so fire away. Only, if I'm not to talk myself I must smoke. "What! in heie ? " _ _ _</p>
        <p>"To be sure. You never wanted to hinder me from smoking in our own parlour in the days gone by, before you got a lot of new-fangled notions. Why, I remember-" "Wait a minute, Joe, and I'll get you a match. Have you got a cigar in your pocket?" Joe had more than one cigar in his pocket, and his wife, mentally hoping that the smell of tobacco smoke would have had time to disappear before any of her fashionable friends entered the room again, helped him to light it, and presently had the satisfaction of seeing him so wrapped up in the enjoyment of his smoke that he was content to adopt the role of a passive listener. "I have it all cut and dried," she resumed,</p>
        <p>"and I don't think there will be much difficulty in carrying out my plan. If our Polly can marry a prince, and if Teddy can marry a princess, my ambition will be satisfied."</p>
        <p>But for the cigar at which he was puffing away Mr. Quicke would have quietly made a remark to the effect that his spouse's ambition was of a some- what courageous order. As it was, he refrained from comment, and the good lady's mind was able to disburthe, itself of the bold projects by which it had become haunted. "Teddy isn't exactly handsome," she continued. "But he's a lot better looking than one or two of the young lords I've seen lately; and he has the extra advantage of being sure of inheriting a hand- some fortune. The same may be said of Polly, except that she's a lot brighter than Teddy, and is a real beauty, and it seems to me that there's both a prince and a princess ready waiting for them. Prince Carlos was very much taken with Polly at our own dance, and he has called here every day since upon some excuse or other. It's quite plain that he's in love with her; and the proper continuation of love is matrimony. They say that Prince Carlos belongs to one of the oldest families in the world, for some of his forerunners • were Kings of jerusalem. But he seems to have a great deal more blue blood than money, and he would have to go a long way before Ile could find another wife with such a fortune as our Polly could take to a husband. Then there's that beautiful Princess Delilah of Courland. What's to stop her from marrying our Teddy, if the thing is properly managed? Nothing at all, in my opinion, and there isn't anything out of the common in such a thing. Most of our English dukes and lords go to America for wives who have no more to recommend them than our Polly has, and if a Silver King can marry his daughter to a prince of an ancient family, why shouldn't a Gold King be able to do the same? And take the other side of the picture. Now then, Joe, if the son of a French actress could get a princess to marry him, is there any reason why our Teddy shouldn't do the same ? " Mr. Quicke puffed another cloud of smoke into the room, and then answered slowly,, after the manner of one who is dealing with a huge joke:</p>
        <p>"You are the best scholar ever I cm e across, Janet You beat these fine London ladies on their own ground, and you will make your record as a matchmaker, if you can pull off both events." "Don't you think it would be a good thing if I could ? "</p>
        <p>"I am not quite so sure as I might be about that. I should be as proud as you would be of , having a Prince for a son-in-law, and a Princess for a daughter-in-law. But my pride would turn into sorrow if I thought that our children were not happy with all their grandeur; and we don't want to persuade them to be married except to people who will like them for themselves, and not for their money." "I think you may trust them to form their own opinions about that. What we have got to do is to arrange as many meetings as we can between the young people, so that they will have a chance of finding out whether they care for one another or not." _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Quicke heartily endorsed this remark. Theta he recollected that he wanted to see his friend, John Mills, and after affectionately kissing his wife, he left her to meditate upon the dazzling possibilities of the future, while he went to an expensive club of which he and Mr. Mills were both members. "Is Mr. Mills here ? " he inquired of one of the club waiters, receiving for answer: "No, sir, he isn't here, and I don't believe that he has been here for three or four days." It was such a regular custom for John Mills to go to his club every morning and every evening, that his failure to attend for several days in succes- sion argued something wrong. "I hope he isn't ill," muttered Mr. Quicke. "I will go to his flat and inquire." He had driven to the club in a hansom, and he also adopted that mode of journeying to Mr. Mills's residence. On asking the hall porter if Mr. Mills was at home, he was informed that that gentleman had not been seen here for three days. "He has stayed with you sometimes, sir," remarked the porter, "and we thought that he was visiting you again." "I have not seen him for nearly a week," Mr. Quicke replied. "I hope nothing has happened to him, but it isn't a bit like him to go away any- where without letting me know that he intended to do so. Are you sure that he has left no instruc- tions ? " "Quite certain, sir. Let me see now, this is Friday, and the last time I saw Mr. Mills was on Tuesday morning, about half-past ten o'clock. He was going out, and I noticed a small yellow mark . on the sleeve of his coat, and asked him if he would wait half a minute while I brushed it off for him. He laughed when I told him about it, and said: I think I shall have to keep a valet, after all. I knew last night that I had brushed my sleeve against a bouquet which my hostess held in her hand. When I apologised for my clumsi- ness, she behaved very nicely, saying that as I had taken some of the pollen from her favourite flowers, the least I could do was to accept a few of the flowers themselves, and they are upstairs, Carter. You shall put some fresh water to them when I come back. lam going to the Bank now, and I have an appointment after that. Mit the whole business won't take me long, and I shall be in to lunch unless Igoon to see Mr. Quiche before I come back.' Those were just his words, sir, and, of course, when he didn't come back, I thought nothing about it, for I imagined that he was stopping with you. "He was going to the Bank, was lie? " mused Mr. Quicke, beginning to feel slightly uneasy about his friend. "That would very likely be to pay in his half-year's receipts from the Golden Placer. My cheque, the largest I have had yet, reached me cvi Monday. As his would come by the same post, fhat would naturally take him to the Bank. I think I'll go there and inquire." Mr. Quicke did go to the Bank. But he did not hear anything there to reassure him. Mr. Mills had called about eleven o'clock on the Monday morning, and had placed to his credit the sum of fifteen thousand pounds. "And then," was the information volunteered by the assistant-cashier, who knew that intimate busi- ness relations existed between the two eX-gold , miners, "he did a very curious thing. He with- drew four thousand pounds.' "But that is not a very curious thing," opined Mr. Quicke. "It would not have been curious if he had not insisted upon the whole of the sum in gold." "What 1 Four thousand sovereigns? " "Yes, four thousand sovereigns, and a fairly stiff load they made, too," -</p>
        <p>Mr. Wicke received this information in a manner which was indicative of the consternation it inspired in him. "After John Mills left the Bank on Monday, morning," he exclaimed, "he must have met with tovi play, for he has never been home since. lther has he been seen by his friends or at hie club, and I mean to drive straight from here to Scotland Yard, for the police must investigate this matter at once."</p>
        <p>CHAPTER IV.</p>
        <p>A DANGEROUS TEST.</p>
        <p>Hundreds of people disappear from London every year without stating their destination to any- body, and without leaving the slightest clue thereto behind them. Yet, by reason of many things,, their disappearance gives no anxiety, provokes no comment, and has no more effect upon the unruffled current of everyday lfe than the falling of a rose-leaf would have upon the ocean. They come as strangers, and they depart as they came, for they are so poor and friendless that not a soul takes the slightest interest in them. But when a man of the social status of John Mills vanishes without apparent cause or reason, it is natural that the sphere in which he has moved shall be constrained to discuss the smallest item of information which could 'throw light upon so unlooked-for an event.</p>
        <p>Mr. Quicke was ready to move heaven and earth, as the saying goes, to solve the mystery. But it remained insoluble, although it had to baffle the vigilance of intelligent and energetic detectives, who were employed to spend money ad libitum in the working out of what was believed to be a criminal case of exceptional cunning and im- portance. Day followed day with the same dispiriting result. Nothing could be heard of John Mills, subsequent to his departure from the Bank. No one had noticed the mode or direction of that departure, and if he had never existed, he apparently could not have been less in evidence. Of course, the usual number of sceptics had their little word to say about the matter. Some thought John Mills was eccentric, and that he had gone away for a holiday to some little known corner of the world, without thinking it necessary to dis- close his intentions to anyone. Others opined that he had had some secret love affair on the carpet, which would not bear holding up too strictly to the light of observant morality. Others again thought it quite possible that some discreditable deed in his past life might have caused his sudden flight from London. The fact that he had provided himself with four thousand pounds in gold helped partially to support the latter theory, and even the police thought there might be something in it, although the popular millionaire, Mr. Rizzen-Quicke, strenuously affirmed that Mr. Mills had never done a dishonest or shady action in his life, much less one that need cause him to volun- tarily exile himself from civilisation. But time passed on, and the disappearance of John Mills gave place to newer sensations, and, even while the subject was still warm, the interest in him and his affairs was not permitted to inter- fere with the plans of many people. It was, how- ever, Mr. Quicke's firm belief that his friend had been decoyed somewhere and made away with, and his desire to put an end to his doubts and fears became nothing short of morbid, for it constantly occupied his thoughts. With Mrs. Quicke it was different. She had felt very sorry on account of her husband's friend and partner, but she was so busily engaged in promoting the innocent little schemes which had for their magnum opus the further aggrandisement of the Rizzen-Quicke family, that she could not spare much time for futile regrets, and success seemed determined to shower its most coveted favours upon her, for certainly, to all outward appearance, nothing could be more encouraging than the progress of events so far as they concerned the social future of Edward Rizzen-Quicke, alias "Teddy," and of Mary Ann Ditto, alias "Polly." No one could be more assiduous in his attentions than Prince Carlos, unless, indeed, it was Teddy himself. He was never happy out of the presence of the charming Princess, who invariably treated him to her most fascinating smiles, but who, nevertheless, had a knack of keeping him at such a distance that, whenever he tried to nerve himself to the point of proposing, his courage seemed to ooze out at his fingers' ends. "All the same, he told himself, "I have only got to 'say the word and I know what the answer will be, for of all the men who surround her there is no one whom she treats exactly as she treats me." This opinion had a substratum of truth for its foundation, for the Princess Delilah's partiality for Teddy Quicke had been noticed and commented upon to such an extent that, mysterious hints of a probable fashionable alliance had already found their way into one or two of the less reliable society papers. Yet, after all, it never does to take things too much for granted, and although Teddy plumed himself a great deal upon the special treatment which he was supposed to receive, he had not the slightest guarantee that the Princess did not smile with equal kindness upon each of her other admirers, wherever they were en tete-a-tete with her.</p>
        <p>In these days it was Teddy's principal recreation to lavish costly presents upon the lady who had charmed his heart from him, and he regarded it, as a specially happy augury that she was good enough to accept everything that he bought for her. Indeed, she went even further than that, for she expressed an overpowering anxiety to become the owner of a star-sapphire, similar to the one which was now in Mrs. ' Quicke's 'possession. "I would not be like your mother," she laughingly observed, "if I had such a wonderful jewel as that in my collection. It would be -my pride and delight to sport it on all possible occa- sions, and to excite the wonder and envy of other women." "You do that already," was Teddy's gallant</p>
        <p>response. "There you are mistaken. Other women don't envy me for any personal gifts I may posse-Ss; for very few of them will admit, even to themselves, that I have a greater share of good looks than they have, and I know that, by many of them, my taste in dress is considered too outlandish and demode to be worth imitating. No, it is not what I am that will ever excite their envy, but what I own. Oh, if any man could but procure me a gem of such unearthly beauty as the star-sapphire. every othe sentiment in me would be swallowed up in grati- tude, and I would give him--" "What would von give him ? "inquired Teddy, almost agasp at his own boldness, but unable to resist the question. "What would I give him? Ah, Mr. Quicke, I would give him my heart, my hand, and—my love," she added softly. "I wonder if I could persuade the mater to part with her sapphire," muttered Teddy, not so softly but that the Princess Delilah caught the import of his mutterings, and if he could have seen the covert smile which flitted over her face as she buried it among the gorgeous cattleyas which- ghe held in her hand, it might have struck simple- hearted Teddy that there was something more than a half child-like desire to out-shine 'her fellow- women behind her longing to become the owner of a star-sapphire. • Unfortunately, there was an element of rustieity in Teddy Quieke's nature which made him an 'easy prey to the beguilings .of a woman who knew how to subjugate comparatively level-headed men who were a long way Teddy's superiors, both in ace and experience, and it was, therefore, an in, concl usi on— thult,---81., w.auld conquer hi-m - her- blandishments. Reclining gracefully against the softly-cushioned depths of a gessip-lounge, whose bronze upholstery threw up the dazzling fairness of her skin to perfection, the bewitching Princess knew that The Rizzen-Quicke's only son and heir was on the we:a of surrendering himself, body and soul, to ter influence, and the bright smiles with which elle set his senses aflame were all the moee potent because they were tinCtaired by triumph. Shti wore an exquisitely-made evening-gown of `pale-blue velvet, and her languorous blonde beauty was heightened by a quantity of gems which scintillated in. her hair, on her neck and arnis, iition her dress, and wherever it was possible to introduce them effectively. "See, Mr. Quiche," she said, with a tender glance at the young votary of love, who was drink- ing in the nectar of bliss while he leaned towards her from the other half of the gossip-lounge. "See, I have plenty of jewels, for they are a passion with me. But none of them can compare with the star-sapphire which I saw at your house the other week, when it was so gallantly presented to your mother. That jewel fairly haunts me, and I shall never be happy again until I possess either it or its counter-part, though I doubt Whether the world contains anotherlike it.- But, of 'course, it is sheer folly to allow myself to be carried away like this by what some_ people would call mere covetousness, so I don't want ••you to mention a single word to anybody about the matter, Rave I your promise?" .</p>
        <p>ovitabie</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>PRINCESS DELILAH.</head>
        <p>PRINCESS DELILAH.</p>
        <p>By MRS. GEORGE CORBETT, Author of "When the Sea Gives up its Dead,* "The Prince Physician," "The Vindication of Olive Trevethick," "The Star of Yukon," "The Adventures of an Ugly Girl," "Deb o' Melly's," "A Fair Blockade Runner," &amp;e.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: John Mills, part owner or a gold mine and of a most valuable star-sapphire, roses all to the millionaire, Rizzen-Quicke, at gambling. Rizzen-Quicke gives it him back on the condition that he never gambles again. In gratitude Mills gives the star- sapphire. to his benefactor's wife. Meanwhile two aristocratic scoundrels, Prince Carlos of Mityleno and the Duke of Solltesein, are plotting with a stockbroker named Mosenthal to float a bogus gold-Makers company, and incidentally they hope to capture some of the wealth of Rizzen-Quicke and Mills.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER 111,</p>
        <p>TIFF. IJSAPP[AANCI OF JOIIN MILLS.</p>
        <p>";r00," said Mrs. Rizzen-Quicke about a week Later, "I have had an invitation. Guess who it's iuii ? "</p>
        <p>"From anybody particular ? " "Yes, • from somebody vary particular. You •eally couldn't guess who. "Tlie.n it's not a bit of. use me trying, so you can't ttar than put me Out of • my misery by telling re at ono? who its from.</p>
        <p>s from the Princess Delilah of Courland. She grand hall at Willis's Itomns, and is • ing all the most swellish people in London." 'You shouldn't say 'most swellish,' my dear. it i'sot sound exactly up to the mark." "lOw do you mean that it doesn't sound up to the inark ? "</p>
        <p>htvin</p>
        <p>-"Weil, it has a bit of an orkard sound, and there's no need to let folks know that we haven't always belonged to the upper circles." "But they'll soon find it out, Joey, if ever you use that 'orkard ' word again, for I'm sure it soinid,i worse than 'most swellish! ' " Being thus convinced that his own social armour was somewhat defective, Mr. Rizzen -Quick° laughed good-humouredly,and replied: "To tell the truth, Jane, I am not sure that it isn't a mistake for you and me to be playing at lords and ladies like this. If is all very fine to be looked up to, and to have a lot of hard-up titled forks ready to toady You to your heart's content, but I don't believe we are a bit happier now than we used to be fifteen years since. Who would have tliought that you were -going to make our fort ane when you were so kind to that poor old lodge; of ours, lonely John, as we ,Used to call- him? " -</p>
        <p>Mrs. Quicke began to fidget a good dea: when her husband commenced to indulge in reminiscences of 'former days, for she knew from past experience that his flow of -recollection was, at times, incon- veniently vivid. "Look here, Joe," she said, in a tone of gentle remonstrance, "you are going away from the subject altogether. 1 was talking about Princess Delilah."</p>
        <p>"Yes, and / was talking about lonely John. We both used to be sorry for him because he didn't seem to have a relation' or a friend in the world, and you gave him many a little snack of, dinner that he didn't pay for, just because you couldn't bear the thought of anybody in ,the house being miserable and lonely and uncared for. And then, when. he died, it turned out that he had five hundred pounds in- the bank, and had left every Denny of it to you, because you deserved it more than anybody else he had ever met. And you did deserve it, too, my girl, for nobody ever tried harder to make an honest living for us all than you did when I was laid up with that long spell of rheumatic fever."</p>
        <p>"Yes, I know all about that, Joe. But what's the use of harping back on it now, especially as it's such a long time since ? " "It's not long enough to make me forget the beginning Of our good tim s. Things ,lind been going bad with us before I was took ill, and nearly everything that you could raise money on had gone to Mosenthal's pawnshop. But when you came into your fortune, we got our things home, had a new rig-out all round, went down to Margate for a fortnight, and, came back well and strong ~again. Then you lent me some of your money to go into business with, and things have gone from good to better with us ever since." 4'</p>
        <p>"Do be reasonable, Joe, and pay attention to what I want to say about Princess Delilah's ball." "My dear Jane, I have been listening all the time, and I have been waiting to hear your plans." When once Mr. Rizzen-Quicke got into a talking vein it was difficult to put in a word edgeways, and he was always so anxious to unburthen his mind of all its thoughts that he was unconsciously in the habit of being decidedly - rude. He would ruth- lesssly interrupt the most intereitiiik rerriarks rather than be silent himself before he had said all he wished to say, and in order to cover his numerous lapses of courtesy and disrespect, it 'was a current fiction in the Quicke family that its masculine head was troubled by.a defective sense• of hearing. "It is such a pity my husband is deaf," was an apologetic remark which Mrs. Quicke often had occasion to make to others, and in some 'cases the apology was accepted with good-humoured credence. But there were others who would certainly have voted him boorish and overbearing if he had been a man with neither wealth nor influence, and the fact that he did not intend to be rude would have had small weight with them. "You are waiting to hear my, plans, are you ? " repeated Mrs. Quicke, after she had apparently diverted her husband's thoughts from things past to things present. "I am glad to hear that, for they are very important ones. You know very well why we are so particular about getting into as grand society as we can. We want to-- " There is only one thing I regret about those times, Jane. We weren't able to redeem my father's old seal. It would never have been pawned if we hadn't been about as hard up as anybody could be. And if only your fortune, had come a week or so sooner I shouldn't have had to feel sorry at losing the only bit of jewellery my father had to leave me."</p>
        <p>"Polly is grown up now, and she is a very pretty girl, so there is no reason why she shouldn't make a "splendid match. I think I would be content to.-" _ _ _</p>
        <p>"I always thought Mosenthal was a bit hard on me when he wouldn't let me have that seal back, for it was only a day or two over the time that the law gives for the interest to be paid, and when he swore he had sold it directly it was forfeited, I didn't believe him."</p>
        <p>"I wish to goodness you would drop all this talk about what cannot be helped now. Surely your daughter's future ought to be of some interest to you if the son--" "It is my belief he admired that seal, and didn't like to part with it." "Upon my word, you're the most aggravating man I know!" exclaimed Mrs. Quicke, rising from her seat with such unmistakable signs of temper that her husband looked at her in astonishment.</p>
        <p>"Why, good gracious, Jane, what's the matter ? " he inquired. "Oh, don't ask me," was the answer. "I'm sick of trying to teach you manners." With these words Mrs. Quicke flounced out of the room, followed by the much amazed glances of her husband.</p>
        <p>"Well," he muttered to himself," I have often heard that there's no understanding m omen, and Jane can be as orkard as anybody when she likes. Just fancy her getting up in a tantrum and going off like that when I had never said a cross word to her. She must be a bit out of sorts, that's what's the matter. The worst of it is, one doesn't know how to manage a woman who has got everything she wants, and that's one of the disadvantages of being so rich that you don't know what to do with your money. I have seen the day when Jane would have been lifted into the seventh heaven of delight by the prospect of a new black silk dress, and you couldn't have ruffled her temper if you tried. But now, even the ownership of the star-sapphire, the loveliest thing in all London, isn't able to keep her good-humoured." By all of which it will be seen that, in spite of his wife's remonstrances, Mr. Quicke was still per- fectly unconscious of his own shortcomings. Perhaps this is scarcely to be wondered at, seeing that he was innocent of any intention to offend. "Talking of the star-sapphire," he continued, "just reminds me that I haven't seen John Mills to-day, and I would like to have a talk with him. He doesn't seem to me to be half as happy as he ought to be considering all things,- and lam inclined to fancy that it is because he isn't`rnarried. I don't think I am cut out for a matchmaker, but it maybe wouldn't ben bad idea to find a wife for my old friend. He doesn't seem near as steady and kensikle fis he used to Jo in the days wlign</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <p>t miii. co anything that you com. "</p>
        <p>nv, Si more closely t this necklace, Mr. Quicke. . What do you think of it? "Iti6 very beautiful, but not so beautiful as the ,vhich adorns it."</p>
        <p>"Thank you, that was prettily said. But I want you to examine the central stone in the penJant very particularly. Take it in ipur fingers, and compare it with the star-sapphire. Almost delirious with happiness Teddy bent over his fascinating enchantress; drank in her bf.auty with wild delight, inhaled the odour of the subtle perfume which characterised her presence, mis- interpreted the light which shone from her eves, touched the warm flesh of her perfectly moulded throat with his trembling fingers—and was lost! "Don't you think the star-sapphire would look much more beautiful here than this brilliant does ? " softly whispered the temptress. "Nothing could help reflecting beauty when placed near you," was the reply of the intoxicated worshipper at the Shrine of Venus. "But if it would make you happy to wear the star-sapphire you shall have it." "There spoke the true lover! A man who will not dare anything for his lady-love could never hope to win my regard, and I will marry no man who has not shewn that he is willing to risk much for my sake." "Delilah, I worship you! And you shall not acc4e me of want of courage." "Remember—the test is the star-sapphire." - "I have already said that you shall have it." "Then let me implore you to be cautions. Ido not presume to dictate the manner in which you get possession of it, but I forbid you absolutely to mention my name in the matter." "That is already understood. And my reward ? " "My hand in marriage. But you must go now, for I do not want any one but my servants to see you here again until our engagement is publicly announced.</p>
        <p>And that is how Teddy Quicke's doom was' sealed!</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
