      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>NEW SEWER.</head>
        <p>A NEW SEWER.</p>
        <p>- - The Surveyor Eubmitted a plan he had received from Mezers. John White, Mclver and Co., on behalf of Messrs Greenall, Whitley and Co., Limited, for a sewer to be connected in Fairfield-road, and continue as far as Latchford Without. It was decided to leave the matter in the hands of Mr. Swinton to see that a proper con. nection is made.—Mr. Piling said he would recommend the Rural Council to accept the plan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>MORE LAMPS- TO BE PROVIDED.</head>
        <p>MORE LAMPS- TO BE PROVIDED.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Pemberton in accordance with notice of motion given mcved that a lamp be fixed in Bedford-3treet, and another in Cawdor-street.</p>
        <p>- Mr. Pilling seconded. Mr. Pearson thought it would be a waste of public money to erect a lamp in Cawdor-street, where there were only four houses. He proposed an amendment that a lamp be fixed in Bedford-street only. Mr. Mclntyre seconded the amendment. Mr. Clarke said he wa,4 only in favour of the lamps being fixed when the obstructions leading to the streets were removed. So long as the obstructions remained the streets were considered as private property. The committee ought to withhold the lamps and endeavour to get the obstructions removed. If they held out a little longer he thought the obstructions would be re. moved. They were told at the last meeting by Mr. Pemberton that if these two streets were lighted be should insist upon his private streets being lighted. The result would be that the committee would be illuminat- ing everybody's private property and there would be no public streets. Mr. Pemberton said the committee had already under. taken to light a lamp at Mitchell-street where an obstruc• tion remained. He did not think they should make fish of one and flesh of another. The committee had broken the principle of not lighting private streets by lighting the one at the corner of Mitchell-street. Mr. Pilling : The committee came to the conclusion that it was an open street. The Chairman said the owners were not likely to remove the stumps. They had not only to consider the p,ople who live I there but the public who used the streets. He was of the opinion that the private streetS ought never to have been omitted from the lighting scheme. Mr. Pearson said any impartial man who visited Mitchell-street would agree that the Committee did right in fixing a lamp there. It lam not a street which was completely blocked to the public use. On a vote being taken it was decided to erect the two lamps nuntioned in Mr. Pemberton's resolution. „A. fuither amendment by Mr. Clarke that the lamps be -ot fixed until the ob3eructions were removed was also lost. Mr. Pearson then moved teat a lamp be fixes at the tep cad of Gaskell-street. - Mr. Ciarke seconded and it was agreed to. It was also decided to erect a lamp at the corner of Mill-lane, Grappenhall road, and another in Victoria* road. This was all the business.</p>
