      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>GALE AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE GALE AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>On Thursday,last week Warrington experienced a tremendous gale, beside which all others previous sink into insignificance. The wind began to rise in the morning and increased in violence until about six o'clock, when its full fury was felt for three or four hours. A posting station on Wilderspool- road was blown down, and falling across the road, 'might have caused an accident to the 'bus which passed a minute or two after, but for prompt information. Numerous slates from the housetops were thrown into the streets and chimneys dislodged. The lights in a large number of street lamps were extinguished, and the town,particularly in the suburbs, presented a very dark and desolate I appearance. The tides were very high, and the fields at Arpley presented tne appearance of a lake— indeed it is tbe general opinion that but for the construction of the Ship Canal, which receives a large quantity of water, serious floods, and a possible loss of life, would have occurred. The bedroom window of the house occapied by Mr. Stelfox, in Arthur.street, was blown in during the gale and the kitchen window damaged, bath rooms being saturated with water. A large number ef slates were blownoff the roof of Mr. Connor's stables behind the Queen's Hotel,and they fat into the pas- sage in which a horse, attached to a cab was stand- - ing. The animal becoming frightened b the noise, rushed off in the direction of Saukerstreet,but was fortunately stopped at once by one of the stable- men. A couple of small trees in the Park were up- rooted, and numerous branches were broken off other trees. Shortly before five o'clock part of the roof was destroyed at the Csstle Rubber Works and some of the employes were sent home. A con- servatory at Fir Grove, Latchford, formerly the resi- dence of Mr. Percy Davies, and now occupied by Mr. Monro, was also damaged. A corrugated roof was lifted off Cunningham's bottling stores in Owea's Fields, Winwick-road. The telephone wires did not escape the violence of the gale and were much damaged in several parts of the town The roofs of houses in , Warrington have perhaps never been very soverly tested, any many were damaged on this occasion. Many bay-windows were also stripped of their lead covering. Padgate also bad its fair share of the gale. Hedges and trees were uprooted, and a window of a house was blown in. A Mr. William Birchall,of Irlam, left his horse and cart in the road at Hollinsgreen while he was sheltering from the storm, when a slate fell off a house and staitlecithe animal, and caused it t 3 run away. The driver caught it and brought, it back. A curious incident then happened. No sooner had he got the horse to a standstill then another slate struck it, and it again started off,knocking Birchall down. The vehicle passed over him. He was taken back to Irlam as soon as the horse was secured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>fl Case in Point. A Substitute nearly Killed this Lady.</p>
        <p>Dr. Williams' Pink Pills Cured her pain.</p>
        <p>MRS. M. J. MANNING, 2, Quarry-cottages, Honick- nowle, Plymouth, told the Western Daily Mercury what happened through taking a substitute. "I had had," she said, "influenza each year, for three or four years, and it left me very weak indeed. I had to be attended like a child. First of all I would be taken with cold shivers, then I would burn and sweat, and ache in every joint. I sent into Plymouth to buy a box of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, but, carelessly enough, received three-pennyworth of common pink pills in mistake, and THEY NEARLY KILLED ME. I rolled on the floor in pain after taking them. I was worse than before, and had pains in .my neck, head, back, stomach—everywhere. My joints ached as they never had before, and I was perfectly wretched. Then I wrote direct for Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, so that I should be sure to get the genuine pills. They were sent to me promptly, and after the first box I could feel myself better and stronger, and free from nearly all the pain. I've picked up over a stone in weight, and I'm a different woman altogether.</p>
        <p>Substitutors scent to Prison. THE Dr. Williams' Medicine, Company will, without regard to expense, exhaust the resources of the law in defence of the trade mark. Substitutors are sometimes sufficiently crafty to keep outside the law. But there. are cases all over the world where they hnve been made amenable to _punishment.</p>
        <p>Some months ago, particulars were published of a case -:n Canada, where a man was sent to ---;,son for selling a counterfeit of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. Another case (reported in the Adelaide, South Australia, Advertiser) shows that even in that far- away country the Dr. Williams' Medicine Company is active in defending its rights, and protecting the public against substitution and counterfeit. in this trial two men in partnership, offering a substitute for Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, claiming that the sub- stitute was "the same" as that medicine, were con- victed of obtaining money under false pretences, and sentenced to three months' hard labour.</p>
        <p>In a large number of cases tradesmen selling substitutes in England have been stopped by Her Majesty's Judges, and the law will be set in motion in all cases where the Company's right is infringed.</p>
        <p>. ...,%„..v...i,„... , ...."-' . ~. 7...,r Mrs. M. `l. Manning. Mannz - itt‘</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <p>Vain Attmpt to Pusb a substitute.</p>
        <p>A Crimean Veteran not to be deceived!</p>
        <p>JOHN FINNIGAN, artilleryman, fought at Sebastopol, where he received two bayonet wounds. He was through the Indian Mutiny, and when he retired from the Army, he was the proud possessor of the Crimean medal, the Sebastopol clasp, and the Turkish medal. A representative of the Sussex Evening Times paid a visit to No, 78, Shirley-street, West Brighton, where he now resides, and enjoyed a chat with the veteran, now a well-built man, sixty-four years of age. In answer to ques- tions he said, "Yes, I left the Army finally in 1876, and I seemed to be in good health until twelve years ago, when I first came to Brighton. I had been in the town for about a year when I was seized with epi- lepsy, had a terrible time with it, and eventually I went into the Sussex County Hospital, but after remaining there for about six weeks I was discharged, the doctors telling me that they had no hope of my being cured. After I left the hospital I continued to have the fits, sometimes three ar four a day. Sometimes for six or seven nights I ha ti not a wink of sleep, and though I tried all kinds of remedies I could get no relief." "But you seem to have at last found the cure."</p>
        <p>"Yes 1" answered Mr. Finnigan. "I heard of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and at first I took no heed of them, as I had tried so many pre- parations. But some nine or ten months ago I wrote to Holborn Viaduct and gave Dr. Williams' Company particulars of my case. In their reply I was advised to try the Pills, and I did so, ttnd I shall be ever thankful for doing so." _</p>
        <p>"Did you soon feel any effect " queried the Times reporter. "Oh, yes," he replied. "Fourteen days after I started taking them ... felt my sight better, the fits were not so frequent, and the noisim I had had in my head cease,. I went on taking them until at last the fits entirely ceased, and now, thank God, I am a com- paratively healthy man. I only hope that my case will induce others to try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. I can, with a clear conscience, tell the world of the benefit I received. When I first went to buy the pills the assistant in the shop argued with me over them. He wanted me to buy others which, he said, would be just as efficacious, but I stuck to having Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, feeling no confidence in imitations of the genuine pills, and now you can see what they have done for me."</p>
