      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>TIE IN BEWSEY WARD.</head>
        <p>THE TIE IN BEWSEY WARD.</p>
        <p>DATE OF ELECTION FIXED.</p>
        <p>On Friday an application was made in the Divi- sional Court of the Queen's Bench, London. on behalf of the Warrington Corp nation for a manda- mus emp,wering. them to hold another election in Bewsey ward owing to the tie which to3lc place on the Ist November. The Court fixed Mon-lay, January 30th, for the new election. It will be re- membered that the two candidates who tied were Mr. Thomas Broomhall Malkin (Liberal and Labour) and Mr. William Rogers (Independent). Each can- didate received 319 votes. There are 751 electors in the ward so that is clear that. a number did not record their votes. As the election will take place in a little over a fortnight, there is little time to i lose in entering upon the contest n earnest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>IS DEAFNESS CURABLE?</head>
        <p>IS DEAFNESS CURABLE?</p>
        <p>Very many still consider deafness an incurable com- plaint. and this opinion seems to receiv^, corroboration when we find so many deaf people resigned .to their sad lot, and making no effort to obtain the slightest re- lief. However, we car positively reply in she affirmi- tive to the above question. Yes, deafness is curable, anti, what is nrirn, vary sprwirtf nr.hie svh Ih!,vPr may be the nature of the auricular affection which is the cause of the d.afness, and however long stanaing the dea fnegq may have been, As we are grim to show. The Rev. W. Heel, of 47, Chetwynd Road, Darmouth Park. London, for some thirty years had suffered from deaf- ness, caused by chronic catarrhal inflammation of the middle (ar, which appeared quite int:tit:able, having re- sisted the treatment of the many specialists whom the reverend gentle,mac had consulted. Hs alsi suffered from ennst tnt unbearable noises in the ears. The Rev. Mr. Heel has now been cotnp'etely cured, as the follow- ing letter testifies, it havinr beeen addressed to the Dronet Institut,„ 72, Regens Park R )ad, London, NW.. a medical establishment famed for its succestdal treat- m.nt of ear, throat, an I nose comp'aints</p>
        <p>" After three wmeks treatment I ;Liu giad t ?ay that I can now hear weil ; the use of your prescription has Droved a decided success. _ _</p>
        <p>"It as with great pleasure I inform Yen of this. To reclver one's hearing spenlily is a ?Jett b!essing. Wish. ing you all succeis p3ssible io your work, for it is ow- sible to cure deafneis by your excellent manner of treatment and I hope that the InAitute will flourish in this land jor the belefit of all afflicted as I t.ave been. I shall recmmend any cases brought to my notice to avail themselves of your remedies." We may remind our readers that the Drooet Institute gives advice, by letter. free o charge, while " The Newspan•yr for the Deaf," a -cieriiitic review full of in- teresting information, is for ward:d frPe to a'l on anon- cation to the Manager, 72, Reg3nt's Park Road, I4m. on, N.W.</p>
      </div>
