      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>TEE PARR HALL SMOKING CONCERTS.</head>
        <p>TEE PARR HALL SMOKING CONCERTS.</p>
        <p>Though not quite so large as that of the previous I Saturday, the audience at the smoking concert in I Parr Hall on Saturday elt ning was of very respect- I able dimensions. And the excellence of the pro- I gramme fully repaid those who had thought it worth their while to patronise the concert. All this season there has been a noticeable advance in the quality of the artistes engaged by the Corpora- I tion over those of previous seasons,and on Saturday I evening each and all of the performers were highly accomplished. In contrast to the concert of the week before, there were three vocalists, Miss Jessie Young, Mr. Walter Lawley, and Mr. Stanley Cook- son. Miss Young's sweet contralto was heard to advantage in the songs down to ,her name, but she was not fortunate enough to secure an encore. Mr. Walter Lawley is a popular tenor in Warrington, and it was no surprise therefore that he was recalled for each of his songs "The Sailor's Grave," and "Let me like a Soldier Fall." As en- cores he gave "When other Lips," and "Take a pair of Sparkling Eyes." One of the secrets of Mr. Law- ley's success is his clear enunciation, and his vigor- ous vocalisation. In Mr. Stanley Cookson the audi- ence found a bass singer considerably above the average. His voice is sonorous and tuneful, and was beard most effectively in "What care I,"for the finished rendering of which Mr. Cookson was encored. The humourist was Mr. Harrison Hill, a gentleman with a large store of originality and unusual skill as a pianist. Unfortunately for Mr. Hill be had had the misfortune during the day to sprain an ankle, which, it may readily be imagined, did not render his task of provoking mirth any the easier. Though labouring under such great physical disadvantages. Mr. Hill con- trived to entertain his audience most satisfactorily. With his imitations of Mr. Chamberlain, and appropriate songs such as " If I were only Joseph," and" How I deputised for Joe," he roused the resibilities of his hearers to a large extent; and succeeded fairly well with "The voyage of the Potted Shrimp," and "A Shakesperian Coon Song." His triumph, however, came at the close when he satirised the recent aimtroversy about the leadership of the Hall 6 concehs with a musical sketch entitled "A Cowen-Richter-Wagner-Cycle." The piece was humorously conceived, and at the same time afforded Mr. Hill full scope for his mu-ical abilities. At the termination of the concert he was heartily applauded—both for his pluck and his ability. Last, but not least, came the novelty of seine magnificent acting by Mr. I and Mrs. G. F. Black. This accomplished couple, who come from the " Lycesim," first appeared in a scene from "Hamlet," Mrs. Black taking the part of Queen Gertrude and her husband that of Hamlet. The dramatic force of Shakespere's play was fully brought out by the two artistes,who acted with all the intensity ot feeling and fiery eloquence that is associated with the scene. Later they took a scene from " The school for Scandal," appearing as Sir Peter and Lady Teazle. The differ- ences of the elderly knight and his charming young wife were spiritedly pourtrayed, and Mr. and Mrs. Black fully deserved the hearty applause which fol- lowed their efforts. In response Mr. Black recited Tennyson's well-worn, but always popular "Charge of the Light Brigade "—and recited it too with an imprsssiveness which few present had heard eaualled. During the evening the 'uties of nec papist were capably performed by Mr. Alfred Ains- worth.</p>
        <p>LECTURE ON " EGYPT " AT THE CONSERVATIVE CLUB. - -</p>
        <p>On Monday evening- —the second of a series of political lectures, illuttratei by lime-light views, in connection with the Warrington Junior Conserva- tive Association, was delivered by Mr. W. L. Wil- liams, chairman of the association, in the Cowerva- tive Club Assembly-room, before a large audience. The subject was " Egypt : Why we are t her -, and what we are doing." Mr. Roger Parr presided over the meeting.. Mr. J. H. Smethurst proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. WUlisms for his lecture. Mr. Hampson seconded the resolution, which was carried.</p>
      </div>
