      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>EX A MIN Ell,</head>
        <p>THE EX A MIN Ell,</p>
        <p>JANUARY 20:1), 1899.</p>
        <p>MR. MORLEY could hardly have Patriotism expected for his observations at and Party. Brechin any other reception than has been accorded to them. Some Liberal journals think he has not sufficiently explained the cross-currents to which he referred, and some Ministerial critics endeavour to convict him of bad logic, inconsistency, and pessimism. We need not trouble ourselves to decide between them. The spectacle to which we draw attention is lost sight of in these comments. It is that of the patriotism of the man who dares to detach himself from his own party, as far as its formal counsels and its, to him, dominant policy are concerned, because he does not honestly believe he can do any good by remahing responsible for the former and by not protesting against the latter. There is not so much austere and vigorous principle in our Imperi- al politics that we can afford to pour scorn on the head of the politician who stands aside, makes his objections,and leaves facts to work out their own lessons. Mr. MORLEY'S attitude recalls that of Mr. JOHN BRIGHT in protesting against the Crimean war when the nation was mad for it, to be at once ostracised and then reviled for years, only to find that the country had come round to his view of the affair. Who ventures to ridicule Mr. BRIGHT now? Yet there are some persons in exactly the same position, wanting to break the power of the Russian Colossus, and intent on questioning the patriotism of everybody who, if not entitled to be regarded as a Jingo, is still claiming to be an Im- perialist and a fighting patriot. Mr. MORLEY can defend himself. But it is worth our while to say that he is not a patriot because he will neither be a slavish party man nor an opportunist of the most slavish type? We say, no. It is good for us, as a nation, to sometimes have an Athanasius in politics, and to be driven back to first prin- ciples and sound reason when so much plausible sentiment prevails. We need not stone our prophets and then build altars to them. Mr. MORLEY reads us a lesson, and it was wanted. A national mood is bringing us along we hardly know whither. The mystics used to say that we were never so near the divine as when we could not see our way. The idea does not hold good in politics. The policy of plunge is seldom safe, and is often disastrous. Mr. MORLEY does not think it safe just now. Empire and militarism and ever-growing expenditure all hang together. It is patriotic to tell us this, and with the honesty of obvious conviction. Mr. MORLEY has nothing to gain by diminishing his party position to be, as he thinks, more of a free patriot. He loves his country; he has an ideal, and he thinks and acts up to it. The immediate rewards of this logical severity are seldom great. They may never come at all. But the example is always a fine one, and it flashes light into dark places,and upon sordid motives, and we see things better by its aid. Ten years hence Mr. MORLEY'S example will be even higher than it is to-day.</p>
        <p>- Labour IN the urban contests in inlre- Ireland. land a strange thinghas happened, A new party,apparently, has made its appearance—the Labour party. There can be no doubt of the fact. There will be six aldermen in Dublin connected with the Labour party. The poll-headers become aldermen under the recent Act. There will be working-men aldermen in Limerick and Cork. It, is being said that the Labour party has had a disturbing effect on the Nationalists, and little effect on the Unionists. But that is a mistake, requiring to be explained. The working-men representatives will all be sound on the financial relations question and on the Home Rule question. In Dublin the revolt has had the effect, so the" Times "correspondent admits, of giving the publican interest, "its woi st blow." It was Parnellite, and the Parnellites have been reduced by one-half. The Cork result is that the Parnellites have fallen from 36 to 19, or just one more than the Dillonites. The results are not yet all in, but the facts seem very nuic.ll tho same in all boroughs. There has been the rise of a new party, and we shall be interested in ascertaining what it means to do and what it will be capable of doing. Probably the women electors have in , many cases voted for the Labour candidates. We hope they have, because the fear was that they would be likely to vote on the high social ticket, whatever it was. The working-men of the Irish boroughs have never had very much local power before. Many of them never had a vote, and where, as in Limerick, there was trade friction at work,and the authorities had given, as was alleged, undue help to the masters in a recent industrial dispute, there was solidarity which enabled tbe workmen to win, by large majorities, twenty-five of the forty seats. The rush was tremendous, for Ithe "Times "local correspondent remarks: " Old favourites, men some of whom have been in the corporation for years back, were rejected in favour of the Latour candidates, nearly all of whom are in humble walks of life." The up- rising has thus been most remarkable. It was fore- seen in some quarters long ago, but it is quite</p>
        <p>clear the Unionists thought it would help theati, and have found it very much to the contrary.' When the new Councils get to work we shall have a better chance of seeing the value of the move. ment. If a good use he made of the victory, and practical sense I4e shown in administration, and wrangling bc ruthlessly checked, Ireland is enter- ing upon a new education, a serious training for self-government. As it is, there must be a feeling of consternation in many quarters. Always the unexpected comes in Ireland. There is a full. fledged Labour party in sxistenca as the result of the urban elections. What will happen in the counties ? We shall have to wait some weeks for an answer to the question.</p>
        <p>In another column we publish what we venture to consider a very sensible letter from Sir J. T. Brunner, Bart., M.P., who, it is quite evident, does not accept the verdict delivered at the recent inquiry by the managers of the church schools as a full refutation and disproval of the charges made. Canon Wood has taken it for granted that no one will dare, or feel inclined, to question the justice of the ver-, diet, and'comments complacently upon it in the Parish Magazine. Those who may not be in com- plete accord with the Canon's sentiments will find Sir John Brunner's letter interesting reading. As is well known Sir John favours the policy of with.. drawing Nonconformist children from the religious instruction given in Church schools, and his views in this direction have our full sympathy. It is very desirable in these days that the immunity of the children of Nonconformist parents from the inculcation of doctrines which seem to differ very little from those of the Roman Catholic Church, , should be secured.</p>
        <p>The Society of Arts course of lectures now beire delivered by Dr. S. Rideal, on the bacterial treat- ment of sewage, recalls the fact that a Royal Com.. mission is endeavouring to clear up one of the mos' important questions of public health. D:. Rides% in his recent works, ha been a warm advocate = the treatment of sewage by bacteria, and he mai/ tains that in this way the difficulty cf obtaining land in the neighbourhood of large towns for the purification of the matter is avoided. The subject reached an acute stage about two years ago, when the city of Exeter applied to the Local Govern- ment Board for a loan of £40,000 to deal with the whole of the sewage in their area by what is knowrt as the septic tank method. After some delay, and the formation of the Royal Commission, the Loco/ Government Board granted the application. Last week the Manchester Corporation put before the Local Government Board a similmr scheme for dealing with the whole of the Manchet = ter sewage, amounting to 25 million gallons daily, and discharging it into the Ship Canal. Experts were of opinion that if a bacterial method were adopted the effluent discharged would be superior ,in purity to the water in the canal. There seem Ito be two distinct methods by which bacterial 'treatment can be applied to sewage. The first is to allow the sewage to stay or a more or less long period in a closed tank, from which light and air have been excluded, or to allow it to pars slowly up through a tank containing flints. under these conditions the aerobic bacteria are enabled to dissolve the organic matter in suspension by resolving it into simpler compounds, which are subsequently oxidised in filters well supplied with air, before being finally discharged into the river. The other system relies upon the aerobic bacteria for effecting the preliminary process; but at Leeds where this latter plan has been tried for the last eighteen menths,screens and settling tanks have had to be used in order to prevent the sludging up of the aerobic preliminary filter. Manchester hes decided to adopt the seco-2d of the schemes, and Exeter is going on with the first.</p>
        <p>The aver age 'bus horse is a much to be pitied animal, though we fear that people's thoughts seldom dwell upon the hardness of his lot. Omni- buses are an invaluable convenience—especially in towns where trams are only in futuro—and we should feel their want keenly were they to cease their periodical perambulation from point to point. Users of the penny 'bus—and the two-penny one too, it is not yet extinct—ought to have some con- sideration for the more or less dejected looking quadrupeds between the shafts. In some of the London omnibuses at present there is appearing the following little message to the public. "The Horses' appeal to Riders. Old adage: Lame Leg—no horse.' If you love us do not strain our legs by needless re-startingsv but kindly alight when we are stopped near your destination." A similarly plaintive ap., peal ought to be made on behalf of the Warring.. ton 'bus-horses. There is ample need for it. Some passengers—and they are mostly of the gentler sex—never dream of alighting from a 'bus unless it has stopped at the one spot mathematically nearest their home, and think nothing of bringing the vehicle to a standstia three times in 5 0 yards: Conduct of this kind is inconsiderate, bad for the horses, aggravating to the drivers, and calculated at the same time to imbue the mildest of male passengers with a wicked desire to be fluently prol fane.</p>
        <p>If the members and officials of the War.; rington Corporation connected with the Payt ing and Sewerage Committee are not (heartily ashamed of the condition of the roads in the :town especially those which are mai serted to be macadam—it is about time :they were. For the past two months they have been a standing disgrace to all concerned, and no steps whatever seem to have been taken to remedy them. During the Christmas and New Year's holidays there were many visitors to the town and we have heard of some very unfavourable comm ments reflecting on the authority responsible fot the state of the Warrington highways. Eve= some of the parapets have been ankle-deep in slush and have been sadly in need of a brushing which they have not received. It cannot be said that the ratepayers do not do their share whert they are paying rates to the extent of 6s. in the £5 and still without a school board. But the Paving and Sewerage Committee seem to be impervious to all criticism and the difficulty is to devise a means of arousing them from their present lethargic state.</p>
        <p>Wd feel that it is our duty to warn improvident man of what a scientific paper calls his "fearful fireless fate." An article based on calculations by Lord Kelvin says "The end of human life is at hand. Within the comparatively brief period of 400 years, the cold waves of an unsailed sea will wash deserted shores. The wayward wind wil sweep over unpeopled plains, where never human voice was raised. As to the sun, that will shines more faintly far, upon lifeless cities crumblinginto ruins, upon rusting engines and rotting hulks, and upon the green, moulderiw skeletons of the un. buried rearguard of the vanished re.ca of man." Cheerful, is it not? It seems that the enormous tax upon the world's supply of fuel will inevitably nsult in its exhaustion within 400 years. At least the scienti-. fic paper quoted says so. It therefore devolves upon man to set to work and devise some radical method of producing heat other than those im vogua. Otherwise this "fearful fireless fate" ratist certainly overtake him. This is the reason why we take it upon ourselves to warn man,though we fear that he will selfishly content himself with a comfortable fire-side soliloquy on the approach.. ing end of all things animate, and leave the post- ponement of the dread day to unborn posterity.</p>
        <p>No MEDICINE FOR THE CURE OF ASTHMA, CONSIIMPrION, CouGus, AND 13Ru:cairns was ever attended with such speedy and unfailing success as Do. Lococs's PULmortIC WAFERS. In every newspaper and periodical may be seers testimonials of their wonderful efficacy. Nothing else gives such a sound, refreshing night's rest. In hysterical and heart complaints they are unfailing, in rheumatic and ner- vous pains "tt ey act like a charm." To singers and publier speakers they are invaluable for clearing and strengthening the voice. They taste pleasantly. Price is. lid., 2s. 9d., 4s. 6d. and its, per box. 6old by all medicine vendors.— Advt. (6).</p>
        <p>"LINSEED COMPOU D " for Cou hs and Colds, allays irritation. (lives immediate relief.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Co AG-ULINE —Transparent Cema . oz broken articies.-.. Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>SCALDING ACCIDENTS AT WESTON. Emily Bowers. 37, Merses -read,Widnes, was admitted to the Accident Hospital on Friday,suffering from severe scalds on the face, arms, and legs, caused by an outlet of steam, owing te the taps of a boiler not being turned off.—On Saturday John Fox, 12, Beaumont-street, aged six years, was admitted to the hospital suffering from scalds on the face, neck, chest, and arms, due to the child having pulled on himself a pan of boiling water. INQUEST AT WIDNES. On Monday afternoen at the Widnes Police Station,Mr Brighouse, coroner, held an inquest on the body of the child of Jane Twambley, singTo woman, Eleanor-street. —Dr. Hutchingon stated that he had made a post-mortera examination of the body,' and found that it was a fully developed male child. There were no external marks of volenee, except a few scratches on the face. There was no evidence that it had had a seperate existence.—A verdict in accordance with the medical testimony was returned. THE PHILHARMONIC SOCIETY'S CONCERT. The ituncorn Philharmouic Society are giving their second concert for this season at the Public Hall,Ruricorn on Wedneslay next. The celebrated tenor Mr. William Green, and Miss Hubi Newcombe (soprano), will appear as principals in Mendiessohn's "Lereley" and will doubt- less sustain their alrsaiy high reputation as vocalists. The programme will include ballads, nate songs, and instrumental pieces by the Runcern Orchestral Band, and the numbers should prove good ones as no pains have been spared by the:Society. A CHILD KILLED AT• THE LOCKS, On Saturday morning, Mr. J. E. Worsley (coroner) held an inquest, at Runcorn Police Court, on the body of a little child, Harriet Bland, aged]. year and 10 months, who was crushed to death between the ioetboard of the lock gates and the hatches of her father's narrow boat" Gothic" on Friday noon.—Harry Bland, boat- man, Wheelock, near San.dbach, gave evidence of iden- tification. His little daughter was playing on the boat by the hatches. When entering No. 9 Lock of the Bridgewater Canal, the tide, caused by the other traffic going in the opposite direction, gave his boat a swirl and the little child's head was trapped between the foot- board of the lock gates and the cabin hatches of his boat. She was carried off to the doctor's but died on the way. He had two other children on board aged 2e- years and -6 months respectively. At the time of the ac- cident his wife was with him on the bank.—Thoneas Dickson Cooke, 27, Albert-street, Runcorn, deposed to seeing the sad occurrence. The child's head was crushed as stated by the last witness. It was purely accidental. The boat was being handled in the usual way and no extra risks were being incursed.—The jury returned a verdict of " accidental death." AFSAULTING A CONSTABLE. At Widnes Police Court on Monday, James Hyland was charged with assaulting P.C. Tomlinson on the 14th January by kicking him on the head with his clogs. He was also charged with being drnnk and disorderly.— Prisoner pleaded guilty to being drunk, but said that he knew nothing about the other.—P.C. Tomlinson state,' that about.lo.ls a.m. on Saturday prisoner was making a disturbance in Marsh-street. Witness went to him and told him to go home, which he refused to do, but put himself in a fighting attitude and struck at witness, who closed with him. They hid a scuffle on the floor, and whilst leaning over prisoner, and holding.him with one hand, and endeavouring with the °Vier to get the " clasps " out of his pocket, prisoner kicked witness on the head and forehead. The wound bled profusely. With the assistance of two men prisoner was put on a lurry and taken to the station.—Two witnesses were called, who gave corroborative evidence.—Dr. McLen- nan said that he examined P.C. Tomlinson, and found two incised wounds, one on the head, and the other on the left side of the forehead. It would be about a week before he would be able to return to his duty.—The charge for being drunk was withdrawn.—The magis- trates said they.considered it to be a very bad case.— Prisoner would have to go to gaol for three months with lizard labour. PARISH CHURCH JUBILEE AT RUNCORN. Commencing on Wednesday last week, the church- people of Runcorn have been celebrating the Jubilee of the Runcorn Parish Church,by the holding of a series of services which ,cencluded on Wednesday night with an organ recital by Mr. H. Macbeth Locker, organist of Withington Parish Church, Manchester. On the first day there was a grand choral service of united choirs, and a sermon by the Very Rev. Dean of Christ Church. Thursday evening was devoted to a lecture illustrated with lime-light views by the Rev. H. B. Blogg, Vicar of Frodsham. 'The subject of the lecture was "Early Church history up to the time of the building of Run- corn Parish Church, about .1.000 years ago.' Ou Fri- day the preacher was the Rev. G. Harford Battersby, Vicar of Mosley :Hill.A grand conversazione took place in' the Parish School on the Saturday when the Lord Bishop of Chester was present,and met about 400 ehurchworkere. On Sunday the Right Rev. Lord Bishop of Chester occupied the pulpit meraing and evening and delivered excellent sermons to large congregations. In the after- noon a United Scholars Service was held when the Vicar (Canon Wood) preached. Monday and Tuesday were taken up with the annual congregational teeparty and a children's evening, and,as stated, a most success- ful celebration was brought to a close on Wednesday evening. The history of the Church has of late been sO often before the public that we refrain from giving it. THE EFFECTS OF THE GALE Doi RUNCORN. On Thursday a fi..rce gale swept over Runcorn and District causing considerable damage to property, and injury to many people. Chimney pots, ridge-tiles.slates and bricks were dislodged from almost every building. The roof of the Police Buildings was considerably damaged. A ehirnney pot of the Victoria-road Board School fell upon a roof causing general havoc. In Duke's' fields many houses had large panes of their windows blown in, the house occupied by Captain Heasley sure fered to a large extent. the gable end being blown in- wards with the resalt that several articles of furnituree will need replacing, luckily no one was injured. Pedes-, trians are not likely to forget the roueh handling they; received. At the old Quay a portion of the Manure Works, in process of construction there, was blown down and a plank struck a workman named John Handley, on the head, causing a severe wound. In an unconscious -condition Handley was conveyed to the office of -the works where every 'possible ail was rendered. Considerable time was lost owing to the inability of working the swing bridge across the canal, and it would be about two hours after- wards-when the sufferer was cenveyei to his borne in • Stanley-street. Another accident of a serious kind occurred at Caetner-Bellner WorkseWeston Point, when man named Price had his shoulder dislocated by a portion of shedding falling upon him. At Weston scarcely a house escaped the fury of the gale, and chine- , ney stacks were" minus "in a geed many places. In ,addition to having its chimney demolished, the house occupied by Mr. George People, in Sweetinatifs-row, hid its gable end destroyed, and the inmates were consider- Ably inconvenienced. At Norton several large trees were uprooted and fell across the road, completely blocking comtrunic atien betweenituncern and Warring- lee. The fenciag of the fieath-road Tennis Club fell an easy prey to toe fury of the gale, .and Leinster Gar- dens on the Friday morning presented an unusual and disorderly appearaace.--Enquiry down at the docks die- Close e teat little or no damage has occurred to :he shipping there, though the head en the water was nomething unuseal. On Monday afternoon and -evening another storm swept over the district, hut no ?serious damage wet reported. RUNCOR,N FREE LIBRA.RY COMAITTEE.</p>
        <p>The Runeern Free Library Committee held their imonthly meeting at the Town Hall, guncorn, on Tues- day evening, there, trice, present. :—Messrs D Bisbrown (chairman), W Han lley, W Hankinson. '1: Riley, and tf D Jones (librarian). The report of the Libratian'for the past month stiewel that t'4o.e Library had been opened 2D sys. ;Darin?, that time 2,132 volumes had been lent coniptising : Theolo 411. ; philosophy, 6 ; science, BO; history etc. 108; etry. 7; fiction, 1,521 ; vaiseellanems 2443 Ecench, 3.; music, 17; the figures showed an in- crease of 320 on the corresponding peried last year. Library rgpnrts were received fr3rn Belfast and Baillies institute Glasgow.—The Chairman said they all deeply regretted that since the last meeting of the Committee, Mr. JosKila Pritchard, their friend and col- league had Nossed away. Mt.. Prl,chard °rag aleian with a kind and genial heart. Re had been a member of the Library Committee for about two years and dur- ing that time had disclurged his duties with proficiency, He was a man ef wile views and very tolerant In ex- pressing his opinion, yetielways had the courage of his convictions. Mr. Pritchard took an active part in public work and had held a great number of positions in the town, notably as a member of the Halton Parish Council. He had been an (official of various associations, and may , be said to have been cue of ?the leaders of , friendly, societies. It Was rather a remarkable iact, but it was the fag time they had lest a member by ' .death (excludine members of the Council) since the death of Canon Barcla9. It would be recalled that about tar) years ago Mr. Pritchard gave a lecture on Halton, vrhich had been listened to with great intenst. Mr. Pritchard was a member of one of the oldeet fimilies of the town ; a family that bad always been 'closely con- nected with the tr ide and commerce of the town. He propoged that a vote of condolence ha sent to the be- reaved family, in the following form That we ex- press mar aphreciation of his service. 3 whilst a member of this catilMittee ; and convey to the family our. deep symeathy in their bereavement."—Mr. IV.Handley said It was with Very sad feelings he seconded. Mr. Pritchard had taken a great interest in th 3 work of the Committee and he recalled the time when he propose I him as a mem- ber of the committee. Mr. Pritchard was chen overjoyed at finliag some congenial work to do—(hear,hear).—Mr. Hankinson and Mr. Riley also testified to the loss the committee sustailled through the death of Mr. Pritchard. —The vote w is passed in silence.—The only other busi- ness was the passing of accounts for payment up to £5 53 8 I,and a res.oltiljen asking f,'r the transfer of £5O, to the Library Fund. LAMPS, (el stoves, Coke Stoves. Brags Fenders. Largest relection.—J. 3. cf,tELAND, Ironmonger, Bridge- etroot, Warrington.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT]</p>
        <p>The Balance Sheet of the Runcorn Savings Bint illustrates the return of prosperity to the town of Runcorn in a marked degree; also the appreciation of a valuable institution, both of which facts are highly gratifying. A perusal of the report of the annual meeting should be interesting to our readers. It will be found in another column.</p>
        <p>At the Rural District Council meeting on Tues- day the important question of tuberculosis in cattle was brought up by reports from the County analyst who had examined a quantity of dangerously infec- ted milk which had come from Sutton Weaver and Frodsham. Nothing was done in the matter, as there appears to be a doubt as to what can be done, but the Medical Officer was ordered to prepare a report as a basis of operancli. A warning nce-,e has been sounded, the importance of which should not be lost sight of by the public, and they must take steps for their own safety whilst considering the many side issues that are being and will be before them relating to this question for some little time to come.</p>
        <p>As a means for the spreading of infection,milk is decidedly dangerous, and to it on many occasions have serious outbreaks of disease been traced. Hence the need for milkshop regulations, etc., and the pre3eat agitation by the Manchester Corpora- tion for legislature respecting tuberculosis in cattle. It is therefore highly important to all to take cog- nisance of what is being done in the neighbourhood affecting their milk supply.</p>
        <p>The Board of Guardians has decided to borrow a fourth sum of money for the alterations to the Board Room, a step that will call forth much com- ment. The money, however, has so far been well spent, and the Board room appurtenances should fulfil all requirements for many years to came. On the whole the Guardians can be congratulated on securing good value, and as the expense will be spread over a period of twenty years the amount will hardly be felt.</p>
        <p>(BY ANOTHER CORRESPONDENT.) Continuing my remarks concerning the School Board Election I want it to be thoroughly under- stood—especially by those who say " we are taking our defeat badly;"--that it is not so. We give all credit to the victors; but still would not like to change places with them. They have won their last fight, depend upon it.</p>
        <p>Not only has the School Board Election been de- cided, but a good many other elections have been decided in the minds of the Liberal and Noncon- formist population. Hitherto, there has been too much credence given to the cry of" the best man with a broad mind, and broad views, etc.," In future it wAll be, to a great measure" what politics do you hold?" "He that is not for us is against us." And not only must the School Board Election be re- trieved, but the County Council, the Town Council, and every available public post must be filled with someone possessing the full confidence of the "majority," No! we are not taking our defeat badly; we are preparing for our turn!</p>
        <p>The whole of the School Board campaign has been an object lesson, and wa will not be the worse for taking that lesson well to heart. The better we learn it, the better will be the fruit of the future. It is all very well to say we have emerged out of it with" clean hands "—and it is certainly creditable to have done so—but there must be no more moral victories ;no mote half measures. And with a united front against the COMM)11 foe, we must and shall win.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RUNCORN RUaiku DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RUaiku DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting of the above Council was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday, there being present: Messrs. Joseph KnoWlo4 (chairman), S Wood- ward, T Wright, I B Chadwick, C E Linaksr, J M P Gleave, J Spruce, 0 Roylance, .1 Whitlow. E Clarke. E Bratherton, J W Bangley, G Pilling. T An- twiss.W Bradford,T Darlington,H Mawdsley,J Mclntyre T Lewis. Admiral Jones Parry, J Ashton (clerk), G F •Ashton'(deputy clerk), J Swinton (surveyor), J Farring- ton (inspector)and Dr. Adams(medical officer of health).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>TIIBEECCYLOSIS AMONGST CATTLE.</head>
        <p>TIIBEECCYLOSIS AMONGST CATTLE.</p>
        <p>Dr:Vacher, the County Medical Officer of Health, wrote under date Jahuary 1311 stating that he had re- ceived a lett!r from Dr. Hope, the Medical Officer of, Health of Liverpool, informing him that on November 10th last a sample of milk which came from the shippon of Mr. E. Davies of Sutton Weaver, was taken at Lime- street Station, and that it had been found to be infected with tuberculosis to a most dangerous extent. Dr. Hope also informed him that at the same time a sample of milk was taken which came from the shippon of Mr.H.Faulk- ner,of Frodshana,and that this was found to be similarly contaminated. He was sure the Council would at once take such steps as they might be empowered to take to prevent contaminated milk being sold. Some discussion ensued during which</p>
        <p>Mr., Woodward said it would 6e unfair to take pro- ceedings until they had decided what the basis of their common action should b 3. It was a very big question. Dr. Adams said It raised a question of policy. The matter was referred to the Parliamentary Com- mittee, and Dr. Adams promised to prepare a report for their information.</p>
        <p>SANDFIELDS-FLO&amp;D, FRODSELAIII. At a meeting of the committoe appointed to consider the advisability of paving. Sandfields-road in the Parish of•Frodsham, held on the 13th inst., the surveyor ex- plained that the cost of paving, flagging, metalling,and channelling the road would be about M. par running foot. After inspecting the road the committee resolved to recommend the Council to apply to the Local Government Board. to issue an order under the Private Street Works Act, 1892, to put in force the provisions of such act as regards this road, and that-the surveyor be instructed to prepare a spe3ifioasioo and estimate for the necessary work. Mr. 'Darlington moved the 'adoption of the recom- mmdation. fie thought they ought to encourage build- ing Wh;ch would contribute towards the rates. The road was In a shoeking condition. _ _ _ _ . _</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward-seconded and the motion was-carried e</p>
        <p>CANAL BOATS ACT.</p>
        <p>The 'inspector subin'ttei his annual teport under th- Canal Boats Lac% in which it was stated that 56 boat had beeninspected during the past year, which contained 91. men and 33 women. and 11 children t”tween 5 and 12 years of age, 14 of 5 years of age and under, making the total number of children 25. The general conditions of the cabins both as to cleanliness and repair were found toioe good, and there had besn no necessity to resort to legal proceedings, and it was only kist and fair to the boat-neople to state that there appeared to be a growing tendency among,it them to endeavour to comply with the regulations of the Acts. The majority of the boats which pass through the Council's District belong to four large firms•of carriers who t -iok a lively intr,r,st in the comfort anl welfare of tieir ern ileyers ; • it was also pleasing ts find that they were always willing to remedy and suppress any breach of the regu- lations when it was pointed out to them.</p>
        <p>The report was received and adopted. THE ACCOUHODATION OF THE PRZSS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker asked to bs allowed to suggest that the reporters should have accomodation provided for them at one end of the room in a position where they could hew. and see, PS they complained that the seats occupied by them were in such a position as to prevent their cor- rectly reporting what took place, and he thought it very desirable that they sh4uld be enabled to take notes of what transpired. Mr. Wood ward thought that the convenience all con- fort of the members was of more importance than tut of_the reporters.</p>
        <p>-Mr, Clarke moved that they pe v tlt nlxt</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker coisidored that the Friveltili, whilh was for the converdenee of the press, V 7,11 tr 3ate3 with scant eourtesy.</p>
        <p>Thle was all the business of importance.</p>
