      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>311ITCRESTER AND LIVERPOOL DISTRICT BASKING CO.</head>
        <p>311ITCRESTER AND LIVERPOOL DISTRICT BASKING CO.</p>
        <p>he half-yearly meeting of the Manche;ter and and Liverpool District Banking; Company, Llmited, was held in the bank oa Wednesday. Mr. Abfl Buckley, chairman of the company. presiied. A dividend of 154. per share and a bonus of 53. per shareware declarel,pay- able on Thursday. The retiring dirreors, M'ss. G3orge Barbonr, Abel Buckley, Frank Gemmill Crowther. El- ward Donner, andlames Halliday, were re-elected. Tee Chairman intimated with regret the retirement of Mr. James Flalliday from the office' of managing, director, which he had for a period of unwa,di of 21 years so worthily filled. He was glad to say, however, that Mr. Halliday would still remain a member of the b,ard. and retain the position of tleputy chairman. Mr. William Tipping, who had been associated with Mr. Halliday for the last 16 years, continues as manag,ing director, Mr. Andrew Bennie, manager at the head office, has been elected to a seat on the bootrd and appointel maaaging director and colleague to M. Tinning.</p>
        <p>SILTZ I‘TO.V Pito(l4ll4DlN'q : " LeptionAl onp3r- tunity." Jaelts. Mantl,s, lilliriery, Dressei Sze., 86e., at less than half price.—W. T. Wir.J.Lims, 74, Sankey- street. Warrington.____</p>
        <p>ASh •;„:,:cuaNitiaiiA.Nv murartlL Vir4THlo.—Adur •</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above was held at Dutton Workhouse, on Tuesday, when there were pre- aunt :—Messrs. J. Mosley Williams (chairman), J. Meadowcraft, Satruel Taylor, A. R. Norma.n, Dr. Gas- taldi, with the Rural District C3uncillore„ Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), and Mr. George F. Ashton (deputy clerk). THE VACCINATION OFFICERS' FEES. A letter was read from the Local Goverament Bard in re3pect of the fees proposed to be paid by the Guar- dians to their Vaccination Officers,and pointing out that the oropertion between the proposed fees was not in ac- cord ince with par. 7 of the Memorandum which accom- panied the circular letter of the Llca-VGDyerninent B3ard of thel2th December and rrquesting the Guardians to reconsider the fees in connection with paragraph 7 and 8 of the Memorandum. _ .</p>
        <p>- Mr. J. Mclntyre as chairman of the committee who hal had the matter under consideration, suggested that the fees be altered as follows : Mr. Cooke t receive 4a for the Birth Lis:, and is for the other cases, and the other two officers, Mr; Riley and Mr. D 3 Prez, 51 and 13 31. Tne fees they received before were out of pro - portion, that was all. It was done to comply with the requirements of the Local Government Board, and the va..:cination officers had agreed to those terms. Toe suggestion was unanimously agreed to, and was ordered to be forwarded t 3 the Local Government Board for confirmation. DUTTON SCHOOL BOARD AND THE WORKHOUSZ CHILDREN. A letter was real from the E iucation Department agreeing unler Section 4 (1) of the Elementary Educa- tion Act, 1891, that an inclusive fee not exceeding 61. should for the present b 3 charged to :he Guardians of the Runcorn Union on account of every child attending the Dutton Board School from the Workhouse. If, how- ever, the fee produced more than a fair share of the ex- penses of the maintenance of the School, the department would hereafter consider the question of modifying the terms of their consent. A copy of the letter had also been sent to the Dutton Shoot Board.</p>
        <p>_ In reply to the Chairmea, the Clerk said this was the first intimation they had receivel about the matter. Mr. Linaker said as Clerk to the Dutton School Board he wrote to the Department, at the suggestion of the Beard, agkieg that they should be allowed a fee of 61. for the Workhouse chil ken. The Board had 't appoint an additioial teacher at an additional cost, and they thought it was only fair that they should be re-imbursed the extra expenditure. The fee was subject to revision by the Department, and if they found they were making capital out of it they would cut down the fee. Nobody would desire that the Dutton ratepayers should furnish the children of the Workhouse with education 'free.of cost. Dr. Gastaldi : Is it a free school? "Mr. Linaker; 'Yes. Dr. Gaszaldi said he couli not see why they should be bound to pay the fee. The School Board received the fee grant fnr children who went to the school. Mr. Linaker said that was quite true, bat after taking into account the fee graut and the Government grant they would still have a deficit of £3O. Although the workhouse children had been tak an in the Board would not have decided to take them if they thought they were to bo ta aght at the expense of the Dutton ratepayers. He did not suppose there was a single pauper child out of Dutton in the school. Mr. Woodward said 61. seemed to him to be an ex- cessive fee. He thought before the matter was definitely settled secornmittee ought to take a careful view of the circumstances and get all the evidence peseilele,and come to a fair and equitable decision on the question. Mr. Linaker said the School Board had carefully worked the matter out, and it was only by suggesting a fee of 61 they could see their way to Coma anywhere near the additional cost. Half the additional grant would go to the master under the arrangement with him. If the D liertment found the Board were getting an ex- cessive amount they would soon cut it down. Mr. Gleave asked whether it was not an advantage to the school to have the children from the workhouse ? Mr. Linaker add there was no advantage whitever. He firmly believed when they came to reekon up their extra cost they would find the 61 a week would not quite cover it. Mr. Mclntyre said the arrangement enabled them to get over a difieulty, and he moved that the fee be be p Mr. Meadowcroft said he noticed there were 52 child- ren in the home. How was it that only 28 were attend- ing the school ?</p>
        <p>Linskar said part of the chiliren went t Preston Brook. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Meadowcroft : Shall wa ha ie an application from there. The Chairman: Possibly. Mr. Linsker did not think they would. In the cage of Dutton their school was qui:43 full, and had to be enlarged. Mr. Woodward thought the information was incom - plete, and insufficient. He moved that a committee be appointed to go into the question. Mr. Linalor eeload3d the motion of Mr Mclntyre. Dr. Gastaldi seconded the amendnient. On a vote being taken the original motion was carried.</p>
        <p>• THE ENAIVINEP. FRIDAY, JANUARY 20, 1899 RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>RITNOORN SAVINGS B A.NK.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the trustees and manageri of Runcorn Savings Bank was held at the High-street prem- ises on Wednesday afternoon. Mr. E. Liaison C.C. pre- sided and there were present Messrs. R. Norman, D Jones, W Hankinson, James Lea, William Sutton,Jaraes Pritcnard, S 0 Beckett, W Littler, R Wright, J Penning- ton, W Handley (actuary) C Handley (assistant actu- ary). The Actuary presented his report as follows :—ln sub- mitting a report of the Bank's business for the year end- ing 20:h November 1898 it is eminently satisfactory to be able to state that in respect to its volume both ia its monetary turnover and the number of separate transac- tions it has considerably exceeded the business of any former year, and the capital of the Bans, excluding the ameunt of consol stock held by the Bank for depositors stood at that date at £11,368 17s 21. Of this amount the sum of £40,822 193 101 was in- vested with the Coolafssioners for the Reduction of the National dent, in other words with the Government, and £545 173 41. was lodgsd in Parr's Bank, being the treasurer's balance to meet current requirements. The year's business shortly summarised is as follows : Ledger balances on November 20th 1897, £38,129 83 21; cash withdrawals £10,923 Sc Od •' transterred to other banks £232 193 ; withdrawn for investment in stock £219 193 6cl ; total withdrawn, £11,376 3s 103. ; leaving £26,753 43 41. Dapodted in cash during year , £13,393 5s 81 ; transferred from other banks £ll6 its 41 ; received as dividends on stock £46 is 11., total £13,560 18s 11 ; add thereto interests paid on accounts closed during the year £25 154 31 ; and interests added to current accounts £924 133 101, making a total owing to depositors on November 20do, 1893, of £41.264 is 61 ; an increase over the previous year of £3,144 133 41. Tate 1195 accounts making up this amount are set out in detail in the list of extracted balances certified by the auditors. In addition to the above total £2OO of consolidated stock has been pur- chased for two depositors, increasing this desirab e torm of investment from £1,650 to £1,853. The number of separate transactions has been as follewe deposit', 1,108 withdrawals, 4 transfers, 61 dividends on stock, 2 purchases of stock,tetal 3,752. The surplus in favour of the Bank over and above the amonnt req aired for the payment of every depositor is £lO4 153 8d; an increase on the year of £l3 124 4d. The removal of the bank to larger and more commo- dious premises has no doubt materially contributed to attaining these satisfactory results. Tne depositors are unanimous in their appreciation of the improved facili- ties thus afforded. Toe nature and volume of the year's business testifies to the steady,though it may be thought slow and very gradual, improvement in the trade of the town anl its immediate surroundings; an improvement which it is hoped will as steadily contin se and further develop those habits of thrift and providence which have hitherto le!en markedly characteristic of our town and district. The Inspector appointed under tee Sav- ings Banks Ac's has made his usual examinations ot the books and acciun's and also the system of conducting the business; his visit extent.ling over tyro days. Toe creditors have periodic fly exam- ined the whole of thew:counts, and their report is hereto appendel. The certificate of the National Debt Com- missioners certifying the amount bed by them on account of the Bank is also submitted, and the amount lodged in Parr's Bank as the Treasurer's balance is vouchel by him on the b dance sheet which with the other Goveanment returns will be sent at the close of the meeting to the National Debt Cemmisoioners. The Caairman moved tint the repot be adcpted and an extract advertised in the local papers an 1 that the auditors bi thanked for their services aid reappointed for the current year at the same remuneration. He said the report so adinir ably dra wn,sho wed them so exhaus• tively tee position of the bank an I the amount of busi- ness that the Bank had done during the year, that vefy litle was left for him or anybody else to say. Tee re- moval of the Bank's premises, had been a very desirable change, not only for the trustees and managers bnt for the customers waase ceav•lli, rice ought to no their first and chief c )nsideration. Tree appreciation of tilt Bank was shown by the increased amount of business, and though one swallow does not make a summer, he i thought that Mr. Handley and thee, who coined et with him, the auditors, were p srf ectly right in assnining that they had now turned the corner and that there were brighter days in store for the town. To have done £3,000 extra busine4s in 'he past twelve months clearly evidenced too, that tie Bank still occupied a position in the public mind that was no: filled by any other instituti m. Some people regarded hanks of this kind as antiquated and superannuated ; but he thought it was a very happy feature of Eeglish life that seine of thsaa had an otdfashioned affection fir iestitutiens practically of their own ferrisliee ; and in- A4:1110113 Such as that Bank founded by old Ru combos many of velem had passed away, managed by Renera- tion whom he sincerely hoped wouli tread in the feetsteps of their predecessors and do all they could to encourage habits of thrift and sobriety and all that helped people t brine SaVut,!S to an institution of that kind, an institution that ought to be encouraged and backed up oy einA.v person who bad the welfare of :he people at lira-s. It was encouraging to Mr. Handley and the Managers to know that the public shared that opinion, and t 'at the Bank was increasing year by year. He must also men- tion the services of the auditors. Au liens occupied an anxious and a responsible position, and the managers knew on the warrant of their c +r-ifieete that every depositor's book had beee under the r eyo and examined by them, which must entail a great amount of time and I trouble.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jinr.s Pritchard, who qemdel ths motion, said the fi4ures sutsrnitted ahowel th tt th, B Ink wag grow- ing iu appreciation and in the punlic estimation, and further that there was cc.nsiderabla improvement in the trade of the town.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wright, as a rep•evanta9v3 of the Frienily Svieties said the Bank was highly appreaiated by those bodie3.</p>
        <p>Tue Chairman acithd that tho suro'ul in favour of thn Bank, was an enantly sa iafac,tory foature. Ha p.)inted out that in 1885, th:neen veru ieo, tne total interest paid to dep micore was £603 153 Bcl.This year the amount paid in interest was £1,003, an increase of ne trly £4OO.</p>
        <p>Tne Actu3r7 remarked tat in the f )rm,r year the interest paid wai 21 per cent., now it was only 4 per cent.</p>
        <p>_ The motion was adopted unanimously. Mr. S. C.B.ckett moved a vote of thinks to mr.jameq Lea for his kindness in a:lowing the msnagers the room for their meeting. He cndorse.l the rctnark made as to the prosperity of the Bank. Mr. W. Hankinson seconded the motion.</p>
        <p>Mr. Littler shop uted and speaking for the friendly societies referred to the great convenience the Bask V 74,4 to them acid to the admirable manner in which the Actuary accommoiated their needs. The societies owed a debt of gratitude tc h7m. The Chairman said he knew what Mr. Littler said wag quite correo% On behalf of the trustees and managers he moved a formal vote of thanks to Mr. flandley bir his services dun ig the year and the considerations he bad shown to depositors.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Wright seconded the vote, remaTking that were it not for the manner in which Mr. Handley managed the Bank the friendly societies would often b 3 in a diffi• culty for money. The v )te was cordially accorded and Mr. Handley, in reply, said it was not often that thanks were rendered to paid servants, and he valued this vote very much in- deed. He did his utmost—it was a pleasura to do so— to forward the interests of the Bank and te convenience every uustomer. He felt that the best way to advance the interests of the Bank was to promote the interests of the customers.</p>
        <p>Mr. flankinson moved and Mr. Pennington seconded a vote of thanks to the Chairm AEI, the former gentleman remarking that Mr. Imitfoa was highly esteemed ani Lan; work for the we:fare of the p3opll appreciate .I aad valued. The vote was carried by acclamation.</p>
        <p>Th,. Chairman responding, said there was no public meeting he attended with greater pleasure than he did that of the Bank. Unlike many public meetings they met there without difference of opilion ; they were all of one mind and had a most desirable objict in view. The meetings were exceedingly peasant and anxieties were an unknown qpantitv.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>TSB APPOINTMIONT OF A NURSE .</head>
        <p>TSB APPOINTMIONT OF A NURSE .</p>
        <p>A letter from MissOel G. Lewudes, secretary of the Northern Workhouse Nursing Association, stated that she was sending Nurse Lowe to Dutton in place of Nurse Todi, as the latter ha.l obtained another appoint- ment. If the Board, hiwever, desired a three year's trained nurse, she could rec,,nrnand Nurse Katherine Marshall, ageld 40, who had 7± years experienco. and who was highly qualified. Her salary would be £53 and £ 2 \yearly for uniform. A postscript stated : As this nurse would have to give a month's notice, Nurse Lowe must go to Dutton in the meantime and can remain afterwards if the Board decide to go on with the present arrangements." Mr. A. R. Normin said the House Committee hal decided to keep Nurse Lowe and not go in for the experi- enced nurse referred to. The salary would be £25 and £2 for uniform. Me Chalig'3 WAS made so that the nurse received a part of the money quarterly, and if he left she would, not as had been the case previously, , walk off with the uniform as well. (Laughter.) ' Mr. S. Taylor seconded and the appointment of Nurse Lowe was unanimously serried.</p>
        <p>P3OR LAW ACTS AMENDMENT BILL.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from Mr. A. Rye, hon. sec., to the State Children's Aid Association calling the attention of Guardians to the Poor Law Acts Amendment Bill in- troduced in the House of Commons under the auspices of the ab)ve Association in 1897. The Runcorn Guar- dians were asked to give the bill careful consideration, and to communicate with their local M.P. asking him to Me his influence to get the measure carried into law during the coining session of 1599. The Bill seeks to give Guardians control over orphans and children of persons unfit to have control of them. Oa the motion of the Chairman the subject was re. tarred to the Parliamentary Committee with instructions to repsrt. Tag COST OF MAINTENANOR " HOXE AND ABROAD."</p>
        <p>A. letter was read from the Brothers of Charity of the Buckley Hall Orphanage, Roctriale, asking that the terms from the let January, 1899, should be 61. per head pr week as maintained in a ler,tnr of the Local GOVEMIIIISIIt Board, dated March 10th, 1898. The letter also states that the proposal had been favourably re- ceived by ether Boards of Guardians. It was observed that at present there was only one boy in that institution beloazinz to Dutton.</p>
        <p>_ In reply to questions the Cllrk said the cost at Pan- tasanh was 45., at Buckley Hall Orphanage 43. 61., and at the wlrkhouse 3s. 6i per head. Mr. Wright spoke in high terms of orphanage and moved that the additional is. 61. be pail. Mr. Wallimgton seconded. Mr. Woodward thought the incrnse ask ocl for was too , large. He thought ta whole question ough: to be con- sidered by a committee. At length Dr. Gastaldi moved that the question be re- ferred to a committeo for consideration. He thought the matter might stand over until they were sending -eome more boy.; there. This ,as carried and the committee WAS accordingly appnin'el. MR. MEADOWCROFT PROTESTS.</p>
        <p>_ The Board Itloal Committee recusnamended applica- tion be made to thl Loosl Government B nrd for con- sent to borrow an &amp; iditionel sum for the enlargement of the Board R3Olll etc, and for the repayment of the same in 20 years. The Clerk said thl amoun; was £325 23 21. Those ex- penses had been incurred. The Chairman proooied the adoption of the recom- mendation.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. Linaker seconded. Mr. Meadowcroft stid h i:hod his vote recorded in opposition to the recommendation as the sum was over and above the amount already sanctioned. The board had travelled beyond its legal sphere, and s3me question might arise as to how this expenditure was to be met. Not having endorsed that expenditure in the past he should vote against the recommendation. Mr. Woodward said the expenditure had already been sanctioned by the Board. The motion was curled, Mr. Meadoxcroft voting against.</p>
        <p>FINANCIAL IMPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. 8. Woodwardbinitted the financial renirt. Out relief was granted as follows :—Runcorn, £B5 ; Fred- sham, £25 ; Budwirh, £22. •Pwrrpnts that day £244 3s 81. ; balance remaining £1672 13s sd. Owing to. a number of cheques being unpresentel theactual balance in the treaiurei hands was larger. ' This was all the businoss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>lIALF-ItOUR LECTURES AT WIDNES.</head>
