      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>LITERARY AND PHILOSOPHICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>LITERARY AND PHILOSOPHICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The fourth meeting of the present session was held in the Museum Lecture Room, Museum-street, on Monday evening, the Presiuent(the Rev. Morley Stevenson) in the chair. There was only a small attendance. The President announced that the Hon. Secretary (Mr. Edelsten) was unable to be present, and he had asked Mr. J. Rymer Young to take his place. Those who were familiar with the proceed- ings of the society knew how often he had occupied that position. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Some formal business havicg been transacted, the Presi- dent said it was now his pleasant duty to ask Mr. Carruthers to read his paper. Mr. Carruthers was one of the members elected this season, andl he had not been amongst them a very long time, but those who had the pleasure of knowing him would look forward with pleasure to the reading of the paper. (Applause.) Mr. Carruthers, M.A.,one of the assist- ant masters at the Grammar School then read an able paper on "Jonathan Swif,," showirg that he had made a study of his subject and foraed. some distinct opinions on Swift. At the conclusion of the paper there was some discussion, and finally Mr. Carruthers was thanked for his services on the motion of the Rev. E. C. E Carleton, seconded by the Rev. W. 11. Drummond, and supported by Dr. White Etna Mr. T. May.</p>
