      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>ALA.RRIA.GE OF M. WILLIA.II COOX.E, A.R.C.0., RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>ALA.RRIA.GE OF M. WILLIA.II COOX.E, A.R.C.0., RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A very pretty wedding took place at the St. Mary's Parish Church, St. Helens, on Wednesday, between Mr. William Cooke, A.R.C.0., organist and choir master, St. Paul's Church, Widnes, and son of Mr. T. Cooke, registrar, Runcorn, and Miss Annie Smith, daughter of Mr. J. A. Smith, of Johannes- berg, South Africa, formally of Runcorn. The bride wore a white corded silk dress trimmed with white chiffon, white hat trimmed with large ostrich feathers and orange blossom, and was given away in the absence of her father by Mr. S. Coventry, of Runcorn. The bridesmaids were Miss Amy Webster and Miss Phylis Smith (sisters of the bride) and Miss Annie and Miss Alice Cooke (sisters of the bridegroom)who ware dresses of cream Sicil- han and large cream picture hats of chiffon. Each carried a pretty boquet the gift of the bridegroom. Mr. Arthur Savage of Runcorn acted as best man, and Mr. William Hough, of Mancuester, and Mr. Robert Webster,of St. Helens, as groomsmen. The Rev. Charles E. Stocks, MA„ vicar of Widnes, was the officiating clergyman. "The voice that breathed o'er Eden" was sung, and as the happy couple left the church Mr. William Hendey, organist of St, Ambrose Church, Widnes, played Mendelssohn's " Wedding March." Luncheon was partaken of at Denton's Green,the residence of the bride's mother. The newly welded couple afterwards lett for Lon- don, where the honeymoon will be spent, accom- panied by the good wishes of their numerous friends. The following is a list of Some of the wedding presents : Mr Smith. Johannesberg, bmk draft Mrs Smi.h, hand-painted mirror Mr and Mrs Cooke, Broadwood upright gran' piano and table 'Mr Jack Smith, Johalassberg, beak draft, Mr B.srt Smith mirror Miss Amy Webster, fir., brasses Mr Robert Webster, bu4t,ar io;er Master Hewitt niuu. d:naer bell TM as Pbv.lis. Cilia-lie a-ad Itsvriand Smith cruet staid Miss Alvah and Alice Cooke. hand-painted toiiet set Mr and Mrs F Cooke Manchester. tapestry table cloth. Mrs Hiss Nellie and Mr William Taylor, silver j am discs. Mr Fred Cooke jun. hall brushes Mr William Hough. biscuit barrel Mr and Mrs Robert Owen, Wigan, silver breakfast cruet Mrs Smith, Leigh. teapot Mrs Molyneux, glass ware Mrs Hilton, picture Mrs Knowles. picture Mr and Mrs Pemberton, sardine dish Miss Knivetm, Leigh pair of pictures Mrs Powell. St Hale is, slipper bag, and co:nb and brush bag</p>
        <p>Captain and Mrs Brockbank, Runcorn, china tea service Miss Annie and Ethel Brockbank, china trinket set. Mr and Sirs Robert Cooke, steel fender Mrs B Kniveton, vases Mr Ted Kniveton, pair of pictures Mrs Corns, hand painted placqnes Mr and Mrs Pearson, plate basket Mr and Virs S Coventry Oak coal vase Miss Katie and Barbara Nixon, silver tea pet Misses An lie, Melds and L say 0 tu4lts. alive • fish carvers Mr and Mrs B Bisbrown, bamboo flower stand Miss Bisbrown, knives and prongs Mr John D Bisbrown, silver pickle fork and silver jam spoon Mr and Mrs Wesley Cooke, white chine tea and breakfast service Mr and Mrs J Timpani, coal scuttle Mr and Mrs it Bien, bamboo table, tray, all cloth Miss Beatrice Bden, vases Miss B Nancollis, set of photo stands Mr and Mrs W Grim, china tea service Mrs Davies, placque Mr and Mrs 0 Butterworth, sugar basin and cream jug Mr and Mrs James Butterworth, ornaments Mr and Mrs R Moore, hand painted folding soreen. rdr T Cook, gentletnan's slippers Mr and Mrs Watson i sney tea pot' Mr Walter Millington, walnut coal vase Mr Arthur Savage. damask table cloth Mrs Cooke (Prodihatn),feaher pillows Mr and Mrs itzra Wen. Warrington, teapot and stand Mr and Mrs Hayton, Durham, Bohemian glos cake dish and cover Mr Jack Hayton, photo stands Mrs Moralise, fancy linen pillow slips Mr William Bentley. Widnes, silver dinner cruet Mr Herbert Stubbs. case of carvers Mr and Mrs Denton, Sherlock, hand painted mirror Mr and Mrs Turton set of kitchen ware Miss C Tartan, antimacassars Mrs Dyer, cake stand and glass dishes Mr William Mercer, rug Mrs Connally, set of jags Misses Carmichael. tea pot an I glass ware Mr and Mrs Spencer. vases and fancy cushion Misses Spencer, wall pocket and toilet m xts Master Spencer. Photo frames Mr and Mrs 0 W Owen. case of carvers Mr and Mrs Tebhutt and family, hannt p tinted vases</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>A. RA.ILWA.Y Ciitt-UAGE EPISODE.</head>
        <p>A. RA.ILWA.Y Ciitt-UAGE EPISODE.</p>
        <p>"3 17s. 4D. FOR A KISS."</p>
        <p>At the Runc)rn Petty Sessions on Monday, before M-sql. W. Timmins (presiding) and Satuubl Tay! another case of a remarkable nature, that adds one more to the list of expensive kisses was hear], in which William Henry Jaws, a venerablm looking old man, said to be a master tailor res'ding At 29, Bradley Greet, Congleton, was summonel on the information of Mr. James Henry Perkins. detective inspntor in the employ of the Landon and North-Western Railway Company, for unlawfully interfering with the comfort of a railway passenger whilst in a railway carriage at Runeora on the 15th of November,—Mr. J. Fenna, solicitor,appeared for the company.—Defendant plealed guilty. . _</p>
        <p>Mr. J. non% said it was one of those eases in which the Railway Company felt bounl to proiecut., how,.ver painful it might be for himself and the other railway officials. Mr. JOIIiS, as far as they were aware, bore a good character, and was respectable in other ways, but still if he so far forgot hirnseq he should be made to suffer. The railway company took up the c ise from a sense of duty to their passenger.. Their passengers w,re to be protected—lady passengers especially, for they be- came So frightened as to jump out of the trains and ran tremendous unknnwn risks in their endeavour to get out of the way of the threstened danger. M. Fauna then submitted the facts of the case.</p>
        <p>It appeared that a young lady (Mil; Beatrice Sarah Moreton) was travelling from Lime-street Station to Hartford by the 1140 train from Liverpool. As far as Edge Hill, there wes nothing of note ti record, but from that station the adventure commenced. Miss Moreton, who is only 22 years of aFf,e,and a yoeng lady of prepes- sessingappearaece noticel that four young lady scholars had got into the same compartment and from then to Runeorn the passengers in the carriage were Miss More- ton, the defendant Jones and the four young school girls. it Runcorn the girls got out, whereupon the defendant opened a conversation with his pretty companion by asking the name of the Station. Miss Moreton gave the desired informatioa. Defendant then asked" How far may you be going." To which I the young lady courteously responded "Hartford." Defendant then asked the young lady "If she would give him a kils." She indienaltly replied 'She would do nothing of the kind." Defendant evidently thought he would try a little persuasion and put his arm round the young lady's waist, and tried to help himself. The young lady resented that kind of treatment, and the train drawing up at Sutton Weaver she complained to, the stationmaster and porter there, with the result that she changed carriages and finished her journey in peace. Evidence having hben given by Miss Beatrice Sarah Mord in, Leftwich Grange, Northwich, in corroboration of her solicitor, she added she was very much fright- ened at the behaviour of the defendant.</p>
        <p>Defendant said that all th,, young lady said wa3 per- fectly true. He was sorry for the occurrence which wal due to him having been drinking previously and he was a little " elevated."</p>
        <p>The Chairman said it was a very s'riou3 offence. So seriona, th it they felt they ou.ght to put that kind of behaviour down. A. man of the defendant's age aid standing shnull have knlwn better. He would ha fined £2, and £1 17. 41. cos"s, or ane msnth in default.— The money was paid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>WESTON.</head>
        <p>WESTON.</p>
        <p>INQUEST ON A BOY.—On Tuesday at the Prospect Inn Weston, Mr J C Wi:miley held an inquest on the body of a boy named John Lewis, son of Margaret Walton, of Sswetman's-row, Weston, who died on Sunday morning umiee circumstances which it was thought rendered an inquest necessary. It was alleged that he had received injury while playing fo AWN. The evidence was fully sifted, that of Doctor Robinson going to prove that the boy had been in poor healthstor some time. Death was duet o perityphlitis, resu'ting in abscesses. But for the rumour that the boy had been kicked, the doctor would have given a certificate in the ordinary way.—A. verdict in accordance with the medical testimony was come to by the jury. A LECTITRE.—On Monday evening, at the Wesleyan schoolroom, Wes'on, a licture on "Jack and Jill" was delivered by the Rev. S. Wright, Runcorn, in connection with the mutual improvement class. The chair was oc- cupied by the Rev. J. A B. Malvern. There was a good attend trice. The lecturer dealt at length with the social and domestic relations of life. At the conclusion a vote of thanks was proposed to the lecturer on the motion of Mr. Edward Griffiths, seconded by Mr. S. Dodd, and unanimously carried. The lecture had been so thor- oughly enjoyed that a hope was expressed that Mr. lA/right would favour them aga'n at an early date.</p>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON PUPIL TEACHERS' DIY CESTRAL CLASSES.</p>
        <p>A meeting of pupil teachers was held in St. Clement's School Bank-street, on Saturday morn- ing, when over 70 were present. An address was given by the head master (Mr. Denison), in which he stated the requirements for the instruction to be given. The school opened on Monday, when 16 (arst year) and 20 candidates attended,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>BEDSTEAD MAKER'S SHOCKING DEATH.</head>
        <p>A BEDSTEAD MAKER'S SHOCKING DEATH.</p>
        <p>BURNT BY MOLTEN. METAL.</p>
        <p>In the Parade RO3lll at the Warrington Police Station on Tuesday morning, Mr. S. Brighouse con- ducted an inquiry concerning the death of William Fleming, a bedstead maker, who had recently come from Birmingham to Warrington. He died at the Infirmary on Sunday afternoon from the effects of burns received while employed at Messrs. Monks, Hall and Company's bedstead works, School Brow, en Thursday last week. Mr. Draper of Messrs. Robert Davies and Co., was present on behalf of the firm, and was accompanied by Mr. Petford, the manager. Mr. Smith, her Majesty's inspector of factories, also attended.</p>
        <p>The Coroner stated that on Thursday, about noon, the deceased was filling a corner stook mould with molten metal when the metal exploded in his face, severely burning him. His left eye was destroyed and his right eye partly so. He was removed to the Infirmary, and on Sunday had an at'ack of delirium tremens, dying a very violent death the same day. Mrs. Shaw, 16, Sntt-street, said deceased was a bed- stead maker, and was employed at the works of Messrs. Monks, Hall, and Company. He was fifty years of age. He left for work after breakfast in good health. In the course of the day witness heard of the accident. She saw him at the Infirmary, and he told her that if he had lost his sight in both eyes he hoped the Lord would take him: He said the accident occurred through pouring metal on to a rusty tube. Dr. Langdale, senior hause surgeon at the Infirmary, said the deceased was admitted •n Chursday, suffering from severe burns to both eyes. His left eye had been destroyed, and his right eye partially destroyed. There were also burns on the scalp. The injuries had evidently been caused by hot, metal. He died on Sunday morning at half-past four. The immediate cause of death was delirium # remens brought on by the injuries. The stock used by decease I and a couple of tubes, on one of which was the casting, were produced. John Charnell said he had been employed at the works of Messrs. Monks, Hall, and Company, as a rail-maker for about a year. Deceased had worked there about the same time. He was a pillar caster. Witness was pre- sent when the accident happened. He saw the deceased pouring the metal into the stock,and saw the metal ex- plode. Deceased said "Oh, my eyes." Witness called far assistance.</p>
        <p>By the Coroner: The workers had to b 3 very careful. Tkey had to take the precaution of warming the bt,ck in order to prevent the metal flying. The accident hap- pened between eleven and twelvo o'clock The Caroner Had any precautions been taken to warm this stock ? Yes; I saw him put some hot iron down.— Was it de- ceased's duty to see that the stock was right for casting purposes ?—Yes. It was his duty to warm it. He was beating the stock for about an hour before.</p>
        <p>By M. Smith : Is ic usual to cast in a rusty tube? It goes rusty.—Do you clean the tube ?—Yes. We would not put a rusty tube in. By Mr. Drapsr : If I had been working it I should have seen that it was clean. The Coroner: Is it a fact that this mould was being used be the first time this year ?—Witness: I believe so.</p>
        <p>Arthur Shaw, another workman, said that when the accident occurred he ran out of the shop and caught hold of deceassd. In his opinion, the molten metal coining in contact with the solder on the tube had causel the explosion.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith : If the stock had been heated it would have beeo all right ?—Wits : Yes. Too Coroner : Have you slen it fly before P—Witness; Yes. The last tims was Ant Friday, with another stock. It was a hatted stock wi'h some warm molten iron, and that flow.—When did you see it fly before Thursday ? It flies every day.—Accoriing to you, it is dangerous work ?—Five were blinded in six weeks it Birmingham. —Da you think the cleaning of the tub!, has anything to do wi:h it P—lf the tube h clean there is not so much ri4k.—What ia the remedy ?—Get the tube and stock red hot.</p>
        <p>By Mr. Drapn. : If the ordinary precautions are not taken the me-al wilt invariably fly? If they are taken it is all richt P—Witness : Yes.</p>
        <p>Toon:ifs Green ssid he ha 1 been emp'oyed at the w-Irks for two years. He was engaged in this clase of work. It wis neanssa.ry to thoroughly heat the stock and tube, and then there was nt much possibility of the molten metal ft ;leg. If he had been doing the work he would hive warm, d the eto3k and bar. Ic addition to warming, the rust ought to be taken off the tube. That c-mld be dons at to time of warming. By the Coroner: We know where the danger lies, and what preoautions to take. If the precautions are !a'rcAn_the danger d;s ippears.</p>
        <p>By Mr. Draper: The men kaow exactly what they have to do.</p>
        <p>The Coroner : His anyone been injured during the la,t two years ?—Witness : There has €nly been one light injury to the cr.—Are you able to say whether Fleming, on previous ocensions warmed both st)ek and t :be P—He did. I hP.ve workcd with him 35 years, and he thoroughly underatold his bu iness.</p>
        <p>John Ciarnell, recalled, was :,se iby the Coroner whether deceaged did anything wi h tho tube blfore he put it into the cAr..lll. Witness noun uot say. He might have done.</p>
        <p>Eimiincl Jones ad he nniarstool this work deceased did. The precaution was to warm the stock and tube. Deca.liod (did that. Fie heatel the tuba on both sides. He witnessed the explosion and rus'ied to deceased,who said "Oh my poor eys, my po3r eyes." He was taken to the lofirmary. Q iestioce i by the Coroner, w'tness said deceased took all the prcc ut,ion; he could be expected to take. Asked how he ac-.:ottni:ed for the accident, witness said they might p )or in 100 times and no explosion would occur. If the htock had been thoroughly heated it would not have occurred on thia occasion. The responsibility for all precaution; being taken rested on deceased. William Webb, another workman, said the stock or tube must have dean damp somewhere. fie saw de- ceased hat both.</p>
        <p>The foreman of tho jury (Mr. Web3ter) : Fleming took all the neclssary nrecautions ?—Witnesi : Yes.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Co-u!d he have done anything more.— Witness : I cannot say he could have done anything more.</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith said he had thought the matter over, and it appeared to him that the rust might have had some- thing to do with the accident. The rust was oxide of iron, and in the casting process it might have been heated up to liberate the oxygen, and then they got an escape of cas or air. There might have hien sufficient oxygen inside the mould to cauie an explosion. By the Foreman of the Jury (Mr. Webster) : The accident has haen cans ,e 1 by the metal poured in and not by what it has come in contact with ? Mr. Smith : That I cannot say. The Coroner said the question for the jury to decide was etritremely simple. Everyone recognized, both manager and m^n, the danger' and knew what steps should be taken to av,il it as far as possible. They had to heat both the stock and tube before the tube was put into the stock for the purpose of moulding. Fleming was an experienced man, having been 35 years at the work, and be recogeized tne danger, and the witnesses aereed that he took all the necessary steps to prevent it arising. Be warmed the stock and tuba, as he bad done many times before and thought everything was safe. He then procee(!ei, having itreerted the tube, to pour in the molten metal. It exploded arid caused his in- juries. Suppose he had no been injured, suppose someone else had been injure cl% and it was a question for the jury to say whether Fleming had done all be should have done. Would Fleming in that case be exempt from blame ? If he would, then, coming back to his own case, his iejories must have been the result of a pure accident. In these works people could only do what human knowledge and ingenuity suggested to them. The test precautions were not semetimes sufficient ; an accident happened and the case was not definitely known. He was perfectly salsfied in this case' and be did not see how any recommendation from the jury would assist. Both the men and the manager knew that the stock and tube should be sufficiently heated if dan- ger was to be avoided. The Foreman said the jury were unanimously of opinion that this had been a pure accident. Thy did not make any recommendation at all. They thoucht the firm sufficiently good and the men sufficiently intelligent to look alter their own interest.</p>
        <p>The Cmoner : I thoroughly agre3 with what you say.</p>
        <p>-A verdict of" Accidental death "was returned.</p>
        <p>WA.BRINGTON :.'7,UNDA; SCiloca. TRAM:MRS' ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the Warrington Sunday School Teacher,' Association took place on Monday evening in St. Ann's school. Tea was served to about 50 teachers and others, and amongst those present were the Rector of Warrington (the Rev. Canon Willis), the Revs. E. C. E. Carleton, J. J. Langham. and J. Davies. Subseqnently a very in- teresting IPcture, entitled "The Bible and the Monu- ments," illustrated by limelight views, was delivered by the Rev. S. Schor, of Liverpool.</p>
        <p>SAFETY OF A DISABLED LINER.</p>
        <p>Intelligence has been received at Qusenstown that the disabled steamer Alesia., belonging to the Hamburg. American Line, has put into Queenstown.</p>
        <p>LATEST LOCAL WEATHER FORECAST: The following foreca,t of the weather for toe ensuing twenty-four hours in the north-west of Eaglar.d, was issued at the Meteorological Office at three o'clock yea.. terdly afternoon :—South-westerly 'Anis, strong. Squally, showery. DE.t.TH OF A FAMOUS SWIMMER.</p>
        <p>John James Collier, the champion mile swimmer bc. fore the days of Joey Nuttall, died in the Salford Royal Hospital on Wednesday. Collier's death was due to throat disease.</p>
