      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>FATAL FIRE AT AN INN.</head>
        <p>FATAL FIRE AT AN INN.</p>
        <p>A public-hwn at Hull, called the Full Measure, was destroyed by fire early yesterday morning. The bodies of the landlady and a daughter were found amid the ruins. A servant and three children were rescued from windows under exciting circumstances, but not before they were slightly burnt. It is believed that the fire eriginated in the kitchen, where sticks were left drying on the hearth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>I STEAMER ABA.NDONED.</head>
        <p>I STEAMER ABA.NDONED.</p>
        <p>A New York te:egram reports till abandonment in the Atlantic, of .the Leith steamer, Glendower. The crew was saved.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF ADMIRAL JOHN HAY.</p>
        <p>Admiral John Hay died at Edinburgh on Wednesday at the age of ninety-five. BRIGANDAUE IN ITALY-ENGLISH LADY ROBBED.</p>
        <p>ROME, THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>A telegram from Naples to the " Messaieco " reportg that an English lady who was returning to Naples after driving in the neighbDurhood was attacked and robbed by bsndicti. Her carriage was stopped, and though she offered an energetic resistencs she was plundered of all her valuables.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Conseil was held in the Day Star la 4 stitute, Penketh, on TueAav evening. Mr. R. Garnett, pr,sidei, and there were also present :—Messrs. E W. Clarke, C. E. Park-.‘r,Dr. Murray,with Mr. Forster (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>MORTUARY QUESTION.</head>
        <p>THE MORTUARY QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Pal kn. mil the police cmstable of the parish had spoken to him about the tr-c ssity of a mortuary or some recognised place wh-re de 4d bodies found in the parish could be taken to. The officer had told him that under the present conditions he was able to leave the body at his (Mr. Parker's) house as he wia the overseer and res- ponsible for the mattee. He did not wish the officer to do that. (Laughttr.) He noticed that at Great Sankey they had provided for a stable at a small nntal. The Chairman said it would be wise if they could make atemperary arrangement with somebody. The building of a mortuary was out of the question. Dr. Murray thought some of the farmers would be in a position to allow their stables to be used for the purple. Mr. Parker asked the membars whether thne 'wed room in connection with the Day Star Institute to allot' of a body being placed there. Muir an inspection of tie prEmises, Dr. Murray moved a reaolu'ion suggesting to the overseers that they prom vide a rolim for the reception of dead persons found W the rorish, and the Council recommend the Day Ste Institute as a suitable place if arrangemews could PO • made for it. Mr. Clarke second.d, and the rAsolution was carrA4..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>FOOTPATEIS REQUI RING ATTENTION.</head>
        <p>FOOTPATEIS REQUI RING ATTENTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Clarke said he wished to call the attention of the Council to the condition of two footpaths in the pariah, one leading from flea'h-nad to Sankey Schools, and the other from Mr.J )hn Jackson's in Heath-r lad to Cemetery-road. For the past three months they had ben alm"st impassable, there having been three or four inches cf mud. The Chairman said he weat te sora Mr. White. the clerk to the Highway Beard, about the whole queation. The law was v-vy uniai4factory upon toe repair of footpath*. There were no instructions in the Act that the Parish Council, we bound to do anything at the footpaths. I sithpy skid that the Parish Council may undertake the repair and maintenance of footpaths within their parish except footpaths beside a public road, and they must not involve themselves in such ex.. pe,nses for the purpose as would exceed the amount thEy were au`horised to spend as a Parish Council. A definite pronouncement on the snhj.ct would be welcomed by Parish Counci!s and hiehway Boards. Dr. Murray said the Council discussed the subjact last year and received a note from the Local Government Board.</p>
        <p>Mr. Partzer ace( whether it was not the duty of the nwnr,r of the adj Aning property to keep the footpath ix</p>
        <p>repair. After some discussion ic was decided that the Cleric write to the tenants of the land adjoining the footpaths mentioned asking them to put the f )otpaths in order.</p>
        <p>HOUSES WITHOUT A WATER SUPPLY. Dr. Murray said the occupiers of the five cottages at the old locks, Fiddler's Ferry, were without a satisfac. tnry water supply. The people were drinking rain water as they were unable to get pump water. They were un. able te store any great quanrity of rain water and some. times they were entirely without water. Dr. Parker said if the tenants liked to take action they could force the owners to provide them with water. Dr. Murrarsain the tenants would not do that. Mr. Parker: Does it put the houses in an insanitary condition being without a water supply Dr. Murray: Want of water puts any dvrelling-heute into an ins ,nitary condition. It was ultimately decided that the Nuisance Inspeatot be informed of the matter.</p>
        <p>COUNTY RATE RETURN. The Clerk produced a return which had to be sub. ti the County Council every ten years showing the rateable value of all lands and properties in ,hs town-, ship for the purpose of arriving at a basis for the County Rate.</p>
        <p>The return was anproved of and oriered to b 3 sent to the County authorities.</p>
        <p>This cra:s al! the basinesq,</p>
      </div>
