        <p>Every person's future is to a eertlin extent in his own hand. Many a future wh'ch might have been bell- lent has been darkened by lack of timAy preparation to meet the exigencies and chanaeo which occur in every day life. Many a home which might have been glad- dened by life and health, and comfort,has been saddened by poverty, illness, and death, in many cases wholly preventable. There are, ind9ed, few that do not feel the system needs recruiting after the trying feeling of lassitude, with others, "not quite up to the mark," "a sort of all over sinking feeling," "too weak to do any- thing about the house," " these recurring headaches," "no appetite for my food," etc. These, and a dozen others, are common expre3Bions which all point to the urgent need of some good tonic miztare, a reliable me- dicine of established reputation and of proved virtue. There are several tonic mixtures to be cad, but none which have been so uniformly successful as Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters; it has never been known to fail. It's always the same, there can be no mistake about it. The careful preparation and a manipulation of manufacture under the most s'singent rules, assures</p>
        <p>the certainty that Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, the Vegetable Tonic, is always efficacious and genuinely successful in all cases of Indigestion,Weakness,Nervous- nes, Low Spirits, Sleeplessness, Dyspepsia, Liver Com- plaints, Neuralgia and Intbeen. We claim that Gwilym , Evans's Quinine Bitters is a General Benefactor of Suf- fering Humanity. Hundreds of testimonials prov 3 this, coming from all classes of the community. Pain is no respector of persons, be advised, try Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters,a sovereign remedy for various ailments, for rich and poor alike. When you buy Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, you get what you think you get, abso- lutely the Tonic of Natures own providing, comprised of the beneficient properties contained in Sars.warilla, Saffron, Gentian, Burdock, Lavender, and Dandelion in addition to Quinine. It is not so much what you pay for a thing, but what you get for what you pay. Don't listen to the "Just as good" arzument, there is no Tonic "Just as good" as Gwilyin Evans' Quinine Bitters, it stands supreme. To prevent disappointment, above all things see that you get the right ar:icie, with the name Gwaym Evans "on Label, Scamp, and Bottle, without which none is genuine. Sold everywhere in bottles 29. 9d. and 43.61 each, but should any difficulty be ex- perienced in procuring Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, it will be forwarded carriage free, for the above prices, by the Sole Proprietors: The Quinine Bitters Manufac- turing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. GWILYII EVANS' QUININE BITTERS.</p>
        <p>GWILINI EVANS' QUININE BITTERS.</p>
        <p>GWILYNE EVANS' QUININE BITTERS</p>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>NEGLECTED HOMES AT WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borou:„,,h Police Court, o a Friday, before Alderman Hemp Roberts (chairman), and ceh.r magistrates, Edward and Rose Ann Jeffers, 13,Bennett, street,were charged with neglecting their three children. Defendants pleaded not guilty.—lnspector McAteer who prosecuted on behalf of the N.S P.C.C.. said the 'defendant were the parents of three children. The man was a bricklayer and in constant occupation. On the 6th of the present Month he went with P.C. Clough to Abe house. The three children were huddled before a few cinders in the fire. The condition of the bed was indescribable—nothing but filth and rags. ,The was only a couple of chairs and table in the house. On Thursday he went to the house and there was no • improvement.—Evidence was given in corroboration by P.C. Clough and Sergeant Lewis.—The Chairman said it was a very bad case ' • the house seemed to be in an abominable condition. They would give them a month to improve matters, and if not done they woald be de- cidedly sent to prison.—Jane Mullen, 6, Ashton-stree, was charged with neglecting her five childran on the 6,:h January, and other dates.—She pleaded guilty.—lnswc- tor McAteer said the defendant was the wife of Thomas Mullen, who got about £2 93. per week as a bundler at Bewsey Forge. Witness visited the house in consequence of a complaint from her husband. The children were poorly clothed and in a filthy condition. The husband could do no good with her. She had pledged everything in the bows, to get drink; she hal even taken her hus- band's clothes whilst he had been in bed and washed and pledged them and he had been unable to go to work at night. Witness had been watching the case 'sine January 22nd lastyear,and bad advised her and warned her about her drinking habits.—Thomas Mullen gave corroborative evidence.—The Bench adjourned the case for a month, on the woman promising not to take (drink. OBSTRUCTION BY A FARMER .—FORTY SEVEN 'YEARS WITHOUT INTERFERENCE.</p>
        <p>i At the Warringt3n B nough Poltce Court on Monday mcbrning, before Mr. Monks (in the char) and other magistrates, Willi4m Handley, farm r, Ne'herton, near Frodsham, was charged with having on the 71. h January in Buttermarket-street, caused an obstruction to the traffic by allowing a cart to stand in the road longer than necessary for loading or unloading —Defendant who pleaded guilty, said he had been in the habit of coming to Warrington for 47 years, and he had never been charged with obstruction b3lore.—The Chief Con- stable: Warrington is different now to what it was 47 years ago.—The Chairman You must remember what a crowded thoroughfare it is.—P.C. Carter, who proved the ease, said the horse and cart stood opposite the en- trance to Bank-street from 10.5 to 10.30 a.m.(Saturday). There were several narrow esc ipes from accidents.—The Chairman said a fine of 53. would be inflicted, but in future the delta dant milA clear out of the streets as soon as possible.—Defendant I always do so.—The Chairman.—Not when you stand 25 minutes. If you come here again you will be more severely dealt with.</p>
        <p>At; ILL-USED WrFE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Monday before Mr. F. Monks (chairman), and other magistrates Martin Monaghan, 155, Knutsford-road, Latchford, was charged with committing an aggravated assault on his wife, Mary Ellen Monaghan on the 13% January.—D - fendant pleaded guilty.—Complainant said defendant Came home soon after midnight on the 13th January in a cab, and he asked her to pay the fare. She refused to do so because defendant had not given her any money for twelve months. Defendant then paid his own fare. Complainant was subs-vently taking a salt bag upstairs to her daughter, who was ill, when defendant pulled her down the stairs, and kicked her while on the floor. Her daughter got out of bed to assist- lier.—Defendant denied asking her for the cab fare. Be stated that his wife first lifted the poker to him, and be then struck ber.--rThe Chief Constable said defeniant had a very bad record. He had been summoned 15 times. The couple had lived a terrible life.—The Bench sent the defendant to ga3l for three months.—Mrs. Monaghan then applied for a Feparation order. She sad she had a family of eight. She considered it dangerous to live with her husband, who bad lately assaulted her five times.—The magis- trates made an order, defendant to pay lOs a week to- wards his wife'es maintenance. A TICKET-OF•LEAVE MAN COMMITTED TO THE ASSIZE 3.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Barough Police Court on Tuesday, before Mr. Garnett (chairman) and other magistrates Thomas Troy was charrfed with stealing from the shop of James Leslie,at 11.30 the previous day a pair of men's boots and trousers value 7s.—The Chief Constable said Mrs. Leslie was the wife of James Leslie, second hand clothes dealer, who had a shop at 39, Pinner's Brow. The prisoner came in the shop at 11 30 and offered her an old shirt for sale. She refused to buy it and privioner went out. A little later she heard a noise in the shop and went in but found nobody there. She however missal a pair of boors and trousers and on going out- side saw prisoner going awAy with something in his possession. Mrs. L3slie followed him into the "Lord Rodney" Inn and found him in the yard trying a boot on. Detective Roper arrested the prisoner. The prisoner was a ticket-of- leave man and had just returned from penal servitude. —Mrs. Charlotte L”lie bore out the Chief Constable's statement.—Detective Roper said he charged prisoner with the theft on arresting him and he rfipii=d " All I have to say is: I did not take them."—The b3nch coin- nutted him for trial to the Assizes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. SATURDXT,—Before Mr. Geddes (chairman). and Messrs Birchalt and Hayward. HRIRTLESS CASE OF DESERTION' —Patrick Kelly. of Glas- gow. was charged with running away from the borough leaving his wife and four children chargeable to the Union.— Mr. B. Shearer who prosecuted said the prisoner left War- rington on October 31',th, 1888 leaving his wife and four Children in the Union. The cost of their maintenance was £3OO and the prisoner had only contributed 30s during the eleven.years he had been away, and the Cost of the prosecu- tion amounted to £5. Prisoner had been earning good wages and he asked the Bench to make an example of the man.— The magistra es committed prisoner to gaol for three months with hard labour.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Monks (chairman), Aldermen Har- rison and Blackhurst, Councillors Shaw Green and Smethurst. and Mr. T. Birchall.</p>
        <p>ONE MONTH FOR A CORNER BOY.—James Leongrdy, 1, Welsh's-yard. was summoned for being drunk and disorderly in Winwick-street, at 11.20 p.m. on Saturday.—He pleaded guilty.—P.O.Leonard said he had received several complaints about prisoner begging and demanding money from people, at the same time using threats. He locked him up.---The Chief Constable said prisoner was one of their juvenile high rippers as they called them. Borne day they would have a Clear case against that class of men. He was a corner boy. A. gang of young men, to which prieoner belonged, on Saturday night " rushed a publican " in his own house. Prisoner had made 18 appearances.—The Bench sent him to hard labour for one month. A PERFECT TERROR."—Five boys, named William Shaw (13). 28. Howard-street. Pasil Dendy (13). 158, Liverpool- road, Ernest Jackson (13). 27, Howard-street. Michael Hayes</p>
        <p>(12), 17, Dale-street, and Samuel jones (13), 73. Howard-st., were summoned for playing footbtll in Na•elyn-street on the 7th January.—All pleaded not g iilty.—P.C. Woe is said he saw the defendants playing football in Evelyn-street, Sankey Bridges, at 12.30 on the 7th January. When they saw him they ran away. lie subsequently saw them playing in Evelyn-street. The bay Deady was a perfect terror to him. He was more blther than all the others together.—The Chairman: We shall have to put this nuisance down —The Chief Constable said he could not walk up the street without receiving a complaint from someone about bays—especially about the football craz s.—Defendants were each fine I ss. 0930 ENE LANGUAGE.—Oatherine Kelly, 60, Gaskell's- yard, Golborne-street, was fined 2s 61 for using obscene language on the 7th Janu try in Gaskell's-vard. DRONE AND DISORDERLY.—Arthur Gorst, 23. Napier- street, was fined Sc and costs for being drunk and disorderly in Scotland-road, at 2 40 on Sunday moral lg. -For a similar offence in Silver-street, at 7.35 oa Saturday night, James Thompson. labourer, 5, Morris's-yard, who did not appear, was fined lOs and costs or 14 days. CROWN FOR THE Papa Box.—Hannah Clare, shop- keeper, Nicholson-street, was charged with h tvi .g in her possession, on the 29th December, scales vflich were un• szamped.--Mr. Place market inspector, proved the case, and said the scales were broken, useless, and unstamped. There was no charge of fraud against her because tha scales were against herself —The decision of the Bench was that the defendant pay 23 63 to the poet b3x the scales to be given up to the Market Inspector to be broken up. A FRIENDLY ConvEasamox —Henry Wright, 71, Birken- head-cottages, Sutton, St. Helens. and Thomas ashardft, 11, Grocer's-row, Sutt m-rol3. St. Helens, were summoaed for bsing drunk in Irlam-street at 12-25 p m., on Saturday They pleaded guilty.—P.4. Bell proved the case —The Chief Constable said he stw defendants on Saturday under his window havittg a perfectly friendly conversation about football, but the conversation was garnished by most shocking expressions. They hid come to a football match in the town. -Defendants were each fined 55. BREACHES oF THE BrE-LAws —The following were fined for using vehicles with cut lights during prohibited hours : lidward Witaberley, 72, St. James'-st., 53 and costs ; John Thomas Poole. Cherry-lane. Gyrnm, 53 and costs ; Harry Challoner, general dealer, Knut-sfora-road. 53. and costs ; William Burns, hawk-r, 10, Mill-street, 5s ; and William Saunders, 61. Sankey-street. 51 and costs "ELEVEN GLASSES or EU3l"—Jtmes Doyle, Friar's- green, was sum:lamed for being drunk in Bank-street. at 11-30 p.m. on Saturd ty.— :i.e. Gibbs proved the case and said defendant had a cut on the site of his head, the result of a fall against 801115 rails. Defendant told witness he had had eleven glasses of rum.—The Bench fined him 53 and costs. _</p>
        <p>TUE3DAY.—Before Mr. Garnett (chairtnan). Councillor Shaw Green and Mr. Bir..;hall.</p>
        <p>HER 45TI{ APPEARANCE. —Julia Ann Shields was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Bridge street, at twenty minutes to eleven the previous night.—Sergeant Hollings- worth said the prisoner was making use of very filthy lang- uage and she went into the crowd coming from the Royal Court Theatre. They would get the benefit of the language. —The Chief Constable said she had been beFore the curt 45 times. He mig,et have brought a different charge against her but he wished her to complete her four terms for the new Act if she was so disposed.—The Bench committed her to prison for one month. WOULD Sid ASH HER SKIT LI. IN." —PATIO% Wald 9r, Percival's Mold, WAS charged on a warrant with making • use of threats towards his wife Mary Welder, on January 16th.— Defendant pleaded guilty.—Complainant sail she asked her husband to get up to go to wo-k, and he said he would smas.h her skull in. The defendant was bowed over fn• six month., in a nicety of Rao, and ordered to pay the costs, or go to gaol for a month. W ECIN EBDIY.—B sfore Alderman James P tirclough (chairman) and Messrs. Geddes and Birehall.</p>
        <p>JUST OUT OF 1.114 WORKEIOUBE.-31ary Farrell WAS charged with being drunk in Kendrick-street at 8 o'clock the previous night.—Defendant pleaded gut ty.—P.O. Healey who proved the case said defendant was lyin4 ac-oss the path opposite the Infirmary.—The Chief Constable said the woman had beenin the workhouse for some time and had co ne out for the purpose of seeking work. She WAS On hot way back when some friends gam her drink which overcame her.— Defendant said she got work for Friday —The m tgistratas discharged her on her promise to return to the workhouse. Atf ANNOYANCS IX BOLD.STREET —John Thompson Was charged with being drunk and disorderly in B3td-street at 5 minutes past 12 the same morning and w:th being a deserter from H.M.'s Kings Liverpool Regiment stationed at Orford Barracks.—P.O. Gibbs said prisoner was drunk aimut 12 o'clock that morning and goint from door to door in Bold-st. shouting " let me In"- He had a pair of regimental trousers on with the stripes torn out.—Provost Sergeant Kelly said at 11.15 on Tuesday he found a cap beloneng to prisoner at the side of the wall. Prisoner muit have got over the wall.— Prisoner was fined 5s and costs or seven days for the S at offence, and Was remanded to await escort for the second The magistrates recommended P.C. Gibbs for the usual re- ward.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUN FY PE a SESSIONS</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAT.—Before Captain Withinzton .(ritnirman) Messrs</p>
        <p>Joshua 8411 and James Tin delr</p>
        <p>OCCASIONAL LICENSZ.—A W. Bishop, applied for an occa sional license to sell at the " Pied Bull " Newton—GrantAd. MiNonCAsEs —Thomas K 41y was fined 2s. 63 and costs for being drunk in charge at Sankey on January 11th. Edwin Cork was fined Is and cos's for riding a bicycle on the foot- path at Cuerdley on December 33th, Martin Sands. of St Helens. was fined 2s 61 and costs for not carrying a lighted lamp attached to his vehicle on tlO 11th January at Burton- wood. John Bennett was fined 5s for haying a dog at large on January 13th at POIII2OII with F .arnhead. James William Griffiths, Edward Howarth. John Blakely, and Edward Mit- lington were fined ls,and csts fo, gaming by playing "pitch and toss " on January let at Burton syoad.—P.C. Berry proved the ease.</p>
        <p>A HU3BIND SUMMONED FOR CRUELTY.-11iWird Boylin, of Sankey Bridges, was summoned for persistent cruelty to hh wire on the 13th January and other dates wise-eby she had to leave him.-11efendant pleaded not guilty —lra. Bovlin said on the 13th January she came downstairs, after leaving been in bed through illness since the previous Sunlay, when defendant began to ill-use her. He used but language and strunk her a violent bl )w on he temple. He also st,..sck her son. She had t Ikea out tkreo summonses ag dust him for illmeing her beore. Defendant was a " bolter- down" at Bewsey Forge, ant earned abou 21 weekly. Defendant denied striking his wife first. The case was adjourned for 14 days, as there were no witnesses present for either the complainant or defendant.</p>
