        <p>VACCINATI &gt;if Horo.srP nom'. —Thmoaa Wilson. of 97, Shaw- street, applied for an order of exemption troll yccination In respect of his son Sydney Wilson. He believed that vaceination did not contribute to the heal,h of humanity. He had hal three other chit iren vaccinstel and one hid suffered slightly.—The application was 4r,int,34. DRUNK AND DISIRDERLY.—R3,Iph Bi rows, 11 %trout, 15 Hgerton-street, plesded ietility to being druak and disorderly in Meriey-street on- the 11th January. —P C Srai'M proved the case and a fine of 5s and 8s 3d costs WAS imposed. PROFANITY IN KiGR-sragore.—Frecl,ries Smith, Nelson- sti-eet admitted offending against the pesce by using obscene language towar is his wife in High-street on the 10th January.—Both had been haring a tiff nest-the Nelson Hotel, so Police-Constable Smith *dd.—Defendant who had been previously convicted in 18/: was fined 2s 6 1 and 8s 61 costs.</p>
        <p>.PAY UP !—Williarn Ellis, a 1 bourer, was sornmoned by the police. on behalf of the Inspector of Reformatories, in respect of £2 14s 6d arrears of meintenance order, in respect of his boy who was being meinteined at AI tccleafield Indus- trial School—Defendant's wire apeetred.—From the state- ment of P.S.Gill it appeared that the order was m ,de by the magistrates at a previous sitting for Is 61 a week. Proceed- ings had ben instituted against the defenden; for 22 91 64 arrears up to the end of 14th November last, bat beyond the payment of a fey/ instalments nothing lied bee-s done.—The Bench made an order for the payment of 2" I.le 61 fo:th- with, or in default the defendant .Ellis to go to prison for 14 days with hard 1 +bout. dTREET NUVANCES—LOITERING AND FoDTBA.LL Bentham, 13rickelay Houses, and Henry Forth tw, Windmlll- street. pleaded no; guilty to a cherzn of obitructmg the public footwey in Bridge-street.—P C. Thomas H laverds stated that about 7 o m.on the Bth of January,the de'endents were obstructing the footway in Bridoe-street. One was leaning and the otOer sitting on the stone wall near the stone stile ther?. There had been nneneraus compleints of the nuisance. - B)th defendants deemed that they were caus- ing ob,truction.—The Chairman said he practice was a very disagreeable one. As it was their fir.t off ,no they would be let off with the payment of costs. 5s 61 each —foaeon John- son. and Peter Radcliffe, two young men, were arraigned for playing football in the streets—High-street and Top Locks. P.C. Bencroft, of Weston Point. proved the offences to which the defendants had p'ead .d guilty. —The Ch airman hearing characterised the habit as another greet nuisan es. ordered the payment of costs (43 6cl). DISORDERLY CONDI:Tr.—For disorderly conduct in the streets, Annie Collins (an old character) and Annie Have a (a young girl about 19) w -re sent to prison for one month hard labo ar.—P.C. G-regory proved the cases.—The younger girl, who had evidently been led astray, burst out sobbing,</p>
