        <p>Holroyd and Sam Lees. At half-time Oldham led by 6 points to nil. In the second half, Oldham had matters all their own way, Sam Lees Jan, Holroyd (2),SamLees son, and Pierce scoring. Sam Lees ticked 2 goals. Final —Oldham, 2 goals? tries!(2s points); Tyldesley, nil.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE JUNIOR LEAGUE. FLETCHER RUSSELL'S GAS ENGINEERS v. LEIGH SITABIR' CRS.</p>
        <p>Leigh Shamrocks were unabie-to fulfil their engage ment ,with the Engineers on Satuscray and sent Bedford St. Joseph's as substitutes. The home team kicked off against a strong wind, and the ball was quickly taken to the home quarters, where Foden was prominent for a grand clearance. The visi6rs attacked again, but this time Scot; was responsible for comm-ncing a fine passing movement whizio to3k the bill to the visitors' 25 flag. A series of scrimmages ensued, and a clearance was affected. Fine passing saw Lawless open the scoring for the Edginoers T Antrobus failing to add the major points. After the drop out the visitors could mike no headway, and shortly after- wards Scott made a good attempt to score, hut was grassed a yard from the line. The visitors could do nothing with the splendid combination of the nome backs, and the outcome was that Radford crossed ail line again, J Antrobus this time kicking the gat. AG the interval the score read :—Fletcher Russell's Gas Edgineers, one goal and two tries ; Bedford, Sr. Josely,',l nil. Shortly after the restart, Lawless dropped a fine goal. The Engineers attacked throughout this half, and two goals and five tries were added the scorers being T Antrobus (two), Lawieso, Radford, and Warbur- ton. Final score : Fletcher Russell's Grs Engineers, four goals and seven tries (29 points) ; Bedford St. Joseph's, nil. WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V. WIDNES WRITE STAR.</p>
        <p>' This match was pla)ed at Widnes ^xi S tturday. Tha Saints were without Boscnw and Povey while Widr.et had their full team. The vigitors won the test and played wit a strong wind at their backs. Warrington went away with a rush and were soon pressing. The home forwards relieved. The" Stars" full back kicktAl well but Burton got possession and raced round his op- ponents in fine style and scored, the kick for goal just going outside. The St. Mary's again had th't best of matters, the forwards ge'Aing possession foontinu Illy and J. Corless got over twice,but both tries were disallowed. Watrington continutd to have the best of matters and at the interval were leading by 1 try to nil. On restart- ing play as kesnly fought at half way. The vizitors forwards continued to play a fine game and Clarke took care that no chit:vs were lost, but the Saints failed to score. The hotnesters were alowed many free kicks but god defensive pl ty by the visitor's backs prevented any other tc,re, the final being—Warrington Si. Mary's, 1 try (3 points) ; Widnes White:Star, nil.</p>
      </div>
