      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION, LA.NCASTERE SENIOit CaItPETITIO.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION, LA.NCASTERE SENIOit CaItPETITIO.</p>
        <p>LEIGH WIN At WA.LIRINGTON.</p>
        <p>There was a lot co? interest malifestei locally in the encounter botween W4rrintri and 1,,q111 at Willers- pool. B th team; of late. nave hown improve I form, especially Leigh who have defeat:d several of the crack clubs in succession. And, moreover they generally give Warrington a tight game at Wilderepool. Toe last four marches with Leigh, previous to Sa.turday,have resulted in three wins for Laigh' ani a draw—last year. Cons'- quently the game on Saturday was looked upon as likely to produce an exiting strugee. Speculation was rife, and while many favoare 1 Warrington as they had choice of ground, there were a good number ready to lay olds on the visitors. Both teams turned out as selected, in the presence of 6,090 spectators. For a wonder the day was fine and admirably suited for foot- all, while no complaint could be urgal a 4ainst the con- dition of the turf. There was a good followinE; io.c. spec- tators from Leigh. Mr. G. Eagles was the referee, and the tears i lined out in the following order : WarrinVon : Hallam, full back ; Fiah, Cross, Isher- wood, and 1:1 irris. thres-q airtirs ;J T Bate. and As- burst hat backs ; Naylor, Taylor, Boardman, Fairhurst, Towhead, Swift, Scholtze, and Sudlow, forwards.</p>
        <p>Leigh :J Coleman, full back; T Colon n, Pearson. Anderton, and T Davies, three qaartere ; Molyneux aed Danbavin, half backs ; Smitn, Mecmisters, Taylor, Arrowsnerth, J Reberts, W Roberts, Boardman, and Ramsdale, forwards. 'rae Warring•lon captain was fortunate with the spin of the coin, and the homesters took up their positions with both sun aed wind behind them. Molyneux star- ted operations for Leigh, and. Sudlow returning, no advantage fell to either side. L'sigh were early pena- lised for being off sile.but assumed the aggressive with a dribble. I R oberts menaced the Warrington line before he was pulled down by Taylor. Another penalty aeainst Leigh forced them to fall back, and Warrington entered the visit era' half by the aid of Cross and Bate. Fast, open play followed, Bate making a clever mark in the thick of his opponents. Fellowine his kick up well, the Warrington skipper grassed Anderton in fine style, J.Colernan coming to the rescue with a run and punt which changed the venue. Warringten were penalised, and Danbevin tried a drop at goal which went very near. The Leigh fortwards raced to the line' and from a scrimmage here Hallam saved pluckily. Neat combination among the visiting backs put T. Davies in possession, and the Welshmen took a cool drop at goal from near touch but was un- successful. Molyneux threw wide to Coleman, but the tatter was kaocked in teuch near toe hue. So tar Leigh had had the best of matters, but at lest Warring- tare took up the offensive. Cross and Fairhurst carrie7l the ball into the Leigh half, and Isherwood attempted to lower the Leigh goal with a drop, but registered only a deed ball. Warrington pressed vigorously, Isherwood and Fish promptly nipping in the bud a movement hetweea Pearson and T. Coleman. J. Coleman was tackled with the ball, and Leigh were hard pressed when Danbavin secured and made a wonder- ful dodgy run to centre. He remised to Pearson, and the latter spoiled a fair opening with a knock on. Bate and Asiourst carried play back to the Leigh line, Ashurst marking in front of the posts twice, bet both the kicks were charged down. Harris made a dash dcwn touch —but was hemmed in. Leigh were penalissd when de- fen ling, and Isherwood bad a shot nearly in front of goal but the ball went under the cross-bar. Coleman made some good saves for Leigh. N.ylor put in a short kick Which Towneed followed up and was over the line with the ball when the whistle sounded for off-side. Smart passing along the home three-varter line sent the ball F.sh who made a gallant attempt to get thrnu rh but was unable to round Coleman. Play of an exciting nature fallowed.Lei,gla having some wry narrow escapes A-hurst tried to get Barrie in at the c orner aced would have succeeded but T. Davies jumped and intercepted the ball. Half time arrived with Warrington still attack- ing and no score had been registered. Taylor opened the second moiety, and after give and I take play the advantage rested with the 111,nt ssers. Dunb tvan was the first man to do anything distinctive, and he put in a h Igo punt which Davies. made use of. the Warrington lino only being saved throueh the quickness of Fish in intercepting a pass. Hallam male a foolish run and was carried ire touch,Warrineten again having to defend. Scrummaging followed at the War- ringten quarter-line, from which place M3lyneux dropped a splendid goal amid the cheers of the Leigh partisans. Warrington wer 3 so -n driven back again after kicking off, the splendid punting of Molyneux and Dunbavin giving the homesters a lot of trouble. Dun- bevin passed, b trek to his partner, and the latter tried to repeat his performance of a few minuses earlier, but failed by the merest trifle. The homesters struggled hard, and at las; got to the other en&amp; Fish tried to drop at goal and the ball went dead, but War- rington ma.intained the upper hand. Bate got posses- , sioa from the pack and kicking across the field he was 1 rewarded by seeing Cross pick up smartly and hand to Fish who raced in cleverly. The fact that Warrington had obtained the lead was duly recognised. Hallam failed at goal. Both sides now played up desperately, Leigh slowly driving the homesteri back. Ilverwood made a grand burst into tee Loiett half, hut passed a If tle too late to Harris and the latter was bowled over. Davies intercepted another pass and went off down touch at a greet rate.onlv to be grandly upended by the I Warrington full back. Play settle I near the home god and some bungling b shin i the scrurrinuge resulted in the Leigh forwards kicking the ball up, and Arrowsmith scored a soft.' try. There was no goal. bat Warriaeton I were disheartened. They made a final effort to score and Fish tackled Coleman near his line. Tee •Leie4h detence was excellent however, and time arrived a m.niit. 1 it er with the score five poiutsti three,in favo ir of L ieta. It transpired after the mat ehe that Flab received serious injuries to his shou'der in scoring the try, though he centinued_playine._</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v: WIGAN.</p>
        <p>THE " LINNETS " IN A. CHIRPING HUVOUR</p>
        <p>Saturday afternoon saw th, " Linnets " arrays against Wigan, on the Irwell enclosure. at Runcorn, and the weather being favourable a crowd of about 2,500 turned out to view the encounter. Runcorn, who were beaten last week Ly S warp -bout in the same boat as Wigan (who were a 1.,) to ~t .a I at weck by War- rington) and both felt the need of " two points." A minute or two after time the ball was started,the follow- ing b.ing the constitution of the teams Ronc.4rn Holghtnn, back; T. Warder, Wilkinson, .1. Buterworth, D. Jones, threr-quarters ; J. Jolley, J. Faulkver. halve;; Taylor, Darlington. Walker, Light- foot, Graytor, Bqtley, Reece, and Tomlinson, fnrwar is, Wigan : Crossley, baek ; Ball, Lewis, S,d lon, Webster, thre.-quarers ; Pannington. Low-, halves ; Rackburn. Yate.,Turner, Paste, Prescott, Brry, D vies, Brown, forwards.</p>
        <p>Wigan (pone] and a scrimmago resulted from a weak ret arn. Rencorn made a capital movement and secure I a scrimmage within tee Wigan 25. Houghton made it fair mark but his drop was ineffective. Jolley pante well and an exciting time was experienced. Better- wo th scor.d a brilliant try, from a pass from Jolley. Houehton'4 kick felled. Then Runcorn dribbled over and ()myth-1, caved a near thing by kicking dead. Off- side against Ruocotn gays Wigan an opening. Wa..- der'a gooi play, however, averted danger. Seddon tried a penalty, hut 'sir kick failed to rise in the least. Warder, Wilkinsoe, Jones, and Butterworth got in some excellent passing. A fine openine was last by Warder knoekiog en. Getting possession out of the serum Dar- lineton and W.lker dribbled towarde Crossley. A fi mark b 7 Buttermnrth was responded to be Croaeley. Jolley was then conspicuctts 141' good punting. Wilkinson made a good mark and Houghton tried at goal. The attempt was a paer one. E7en play vaulted. A nicely put punt by Butterworth, was well- followed up, but although Runcorn got over they were penalised for forward pessing. Seddon relieved by marking, A wep placed kick in from touch was spoilt by knocking on. Off-side against Wigan gave Jolley a chance, and he dropped a beautiful goal. Tiongetrin responded to the kick 6ff and made good ground. J. Faulkn-r gained possession, passed to Butterworth, who gave J.)neq a splendid oeportenity, and ha scored a try. Jolley ag;in kicked finely and a goal was added. Wigan came away from the restart, Yates, Peete,and Berry be- ing conspieuous. Preacott took n ponoltv egalnst Ran- corn, bet • he b,at went wi ie Jones got an opening and gained ground with grind running. Wigan returned the play to m'd field by wane of good foot work. Faulkner and Jolley Ly judicious fieldina gave their th-eequsrters ample opportueity and it w-s only the smart tackling of Webster and Bill that averted heavy sc irieg. Warder kieked finely; Walker received and the resell of a pay- ing bout found Liealloet over. A line go.i scrimmage resulted. .Tolley marked without knowing it, tut nr- thing tangible rrisult,d. Half-'ime, Runcorn 2 goals, 2 tries (10 points); Wigan nil. Taylor restarted the eeeond hajf and Runcorn at once prrssel Houghton follewe 1 ue'we.l,end Jimmy Butter- worth improved with good kicking. From a scrimmage close up Eloughton received and dodged, Jon's acsist,ad him, and a heaetiful piece of work restalted. Finally Joees passed to Walker and he scored a superb try. which Eft:tighten fAilel to improve. Seddon relieved with a mark. A penalty improved things for thehorne</p>
        <p>team, which was shortly followel by another. Jolley tried at goal and Lewis returned. Butterwor,h received in a wool position, but his attsmpl at a drop failed to rite. Wigan wails now hard pressed. ki result of the pressure Taylo: founl an opening and slor id. Jolley's pot at the sti.iks was a good ene hut miss-id. Liv ly play followed and time after time the Wigan line was in danger. Joneß, Butterworth and Warder being contin- ually on the attack. J. Faulkner received oil.; of a scrimmage, and coolly rasied to Riley. and how that player failed at his drop was bard to tell. So far Runcorn had been having their own way. Hough- ton in negotia•ing a running catch knocked-on. This afforded a happy release to Wigan and Turner and Pres- c At; improved things. J Butterworth then showed good form, but retained posse,sion too long and lost the hag in attempting a low piss. Anoth sr good run by Hougn- ton was ay .11 followei up. and Crossley, miscallulating the reblund, let Re.cein, and another try was registered f r Runcorn. Jolley failed to convert. "Cr aisle,/ exhibi- tel remarkable coolness when hard pressed. Wilkinson imnroved things for Runcorn, and soon Butterworth ee- tablished a strong position on the extreme left, Wigan being compelled to kick dead. Getting possession out of the ccrumniage Warder, passed to Wilkinson, Wilkinson to Butter worth and he gave to Jones who sailed in glori- ou,ly. Efouzbton missed at goal. Wigan made an effort to respond, bit Sadden was brought to grass Excite- ment followed and play got somewhat rough! A sin.- prising piece of passinz resulted in Butterworth getting over and placing the ball right between the stick Jolley jest failed by inches and the whistle then sounded " no-side." Final result : Runcorn, 2 goals 7 tries, (25 points) ; Wigan, nil.</p>
