      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>WIDNES v. STOCKPORT.</head>
        <p>WIDNES v. STOCKPORT.</p>
        <p>At Widnes. .:1:n-e-i;rn- ;.cli-ria-,:v;;;;iiliOut the services of B Lty, Ha lopson. Murphy, aid Dauglaa. whilst Stock- port w,rre well represented. Stockpert startei, and Johnson was tsekled before he could teply. The visitors were penalised for off-side play, and a moment later Brown pick .d smartly up and, after a sensational ran, passed to Rispaa, who reuassed to Peacock, whet' the latter grounded the boll bene WI the crossbar, fleJlop converting. Ronsel by this reverse, Stockoort attackyl vigorously,hut the defence of the home team was sound. O'Bri.n Mewl out of touch, and Peacock ranging up seored a sole d try, Heslop failing at goal. Shortly .4frerwards Risoan made a mark aed dropped a goal. Wirings caatinuel to press and Halligan dropped another whilst O'Brien got a try from a scrummage.Heslep failinsf at goal. Wormley got away and passed to S tville, who sco-ed an UIIC/13,7 Tted try for Steckporc. Handley also added a try for Widnes, half t'mle score :—Widnes 20 points; Stockport, 3 pouts. Niiines restarted, and S-ockpott gradually worked their way to the home half. Widnes were penalised and Saville placed a splendid goal. On restarin; Widnes attacked with vigour,and receiving the ball paseei it to Rispa.n who scored a spiew- did try, Peacaelc failing at goal. Saville was applauded for a smart run, Heaton bringing him dowa. Brown re- lieved the pressure, hut Stenkport, aided by the wind, were soon bank to the home quarter, Peacock Raving with a neat kick. 'the home forwards male a splendid run up the field, but the ball was thrown forward near the Hoe. Saville raced away. but Brennan tackled him smartly, and a splendid combined rush by the Wines for marl; almost ended in Handley .scoring. Robinson sending tie ball dead. O'Brien dashed over from a scrummage, Peacock failing at goal. G:b on also added a try for Stockport, Saville converting. Final score : Wiines four goals and six tri .9 (26 points); Sto,:kport, two g; A's and two tries (10 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>ST. RELEZIS v. 3WINTON.</head>
        <p>ST. RELEZIS v. 3WINTON.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens, Swinton were fully represented, but S. Helens were short of Traynor. The home te !In star!ei with 14 men, owing to the ats‘nce of Siddall. Swinton won the toss, and played with a strong wind in their favour during the first hail. Captaia Michael Hughes, presiclent of till S. Helens Club, kicked off for till home team. Toe visitors hid the best of the play a•-• the start, and, aided by the wind pressed. The home det-ndors vitro in grand form, howaver, anl repeated'? saved from the oopoing f nwards. Hampson. of Swin- ton, broke through the home forwards, but Fculkos col- !wed him and brought him down close to the goal line. The St. Helens forwards got to Swinton 25. but we-e drwen hack-, and Valentine with a penalty kirk sent the hall againd one of the pasts, whence it rebounded into the field of nay. St. Helens were rarely dangerous during the first half. Gladwin filled the vacancy in the home ranks after the game had bent in operation for 25 m,nutis. Swinton tried hard to score just before the interval. but failed to do FO, and ends were changed without any snoring. After ends had been changed the home forwards made some strong rustle*, and for the first fif-een minut,s Swinton were very hardly pr-seed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL,</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL,</p>
        <p>NEWTON-LE- 6'ITJLOW V. PEASLEY CROSS.</p>
        <p>The return fixture between the above teams uridcr the auspices of be LVerpool and District Combination was Played at Nulsery Park' Newton, on Saturday. The initill mseting between the teams at Peasley Cross re- sulted in a win for Peasley Cross by 2 goals to nil. Great interest was centred in this match as a win for visitors would eve them a very good chance of the championship. The ground was in splendid conditio s.ll tt though atr fib heavy in places. The homesters won time toss and fle::ted to play against a slight incline, Peasley Cross kicking off in the presence of a fair concourse of spectators. The opening exchanges were very even and were chiefly conte..ted in mid-field. The visitors, however. playiag with the wind got into clns 3 qu trters. and Saxon sent a shot in whicn Wyatt save i and then let fall ain. The visitors strenuously appesled for a goal and the referee blew his whistle, hut for a moment or two he seemed undecided whit to do. After a lot of talking and c insultation with the lintismen he decided to throw the hall up might on the goal line from where Ashton cleared and the Nwton forwards race away withlthe ball. The visitors' forwards by the aid of a couple of fouls got into the Newton goal and S mxon eventually beat Wyatt with a high dropping shot, t iii coming after hail-an-hour's pay. The hornest irs made strenu us efforts to draw level after this but all to no purpose. Ha'f time : Pasley Cross. 1 ; Ndwton, 0 Upon renaming the hotnestirs showed that they meant business, and they commenced a hot fusi'ade on the visitors' goal but theftestodiert-played a splendid game and dealt with all the shots in the coolest possible fashion. Newton still kept up the pressure and Ward raced down the home left and pissed to Thompson who in turn passed to Frankland, this player banging the ball past Frodsham with a lightning shot ten minutes after the restart. Encouraged by this euccess,the New- ton forwards again took up the attack and Hill driW)led the ball past the visitors' tacks and gave the custodian no chance with a splendid shot thus giving the home- sters the lead. The same player eventually scored a third goal before the close. Final : Nowton 3. Peasley Cross 1. EARLESTOWN ROVERS v. WARRINGTON S r: MARY'S.</p>
        <p>The above match, which was looked forward to with interest, by both clubs owing to the result having an important bearing on the championship of the League was played at Barlestown on Saturday. Both teams were fully represented, and the game was fiercely con- tested right thrcugh, the Rovers just scoring a minute b-fore the close and winning by one goal to none.</p>
        <p>NEWTON-LE-WILLOWS. v-.-STU BSHAW ORO9S.</p>
        <p>WIGAN SHIELD.</p>
        <p>Newton had to send two teams out on Saturday, the first team playing off the combination match with Peasiev Cross at NEwt Ai and the Athletics going to Stubshaw to play till above match. The game which was clogely contested right through ended in a win for Stubshaw Cross by two goals to nil.</p>
