        <p>WARRINGTON CHAMBER OF COMMERCE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Chamber was held on Nionday, Mr. John Fairclough presiding.—The Secretary(Mr. A. Bennett) reported the result of certain corres.- pondence with the Board of Trade with reference to the improvement in the returns of the imports of tanning materials, which in the opinion of several members of the Chamber were not as clear and satisfactory as was desirable. It was decided to consider the matter further at a subsequent meet- ing. On the initiative of the Peace Society a resc- lution was unanimously passfid expressing the pro- found .atisfacti Du of the local commercial community of ths acceptance by Her Majesty's Government of the invitation of the Czar too Euro- pean Conference to consider the maintenance of general peace and the possible reduction of exces- - armaments. Communications were read from the Edinburgh and Liverpool Chambers in favour of the nationalization of telephones, and after an interesting discussion it was unanimously resolved 'That having regard to the disadvantages arising out of the present telephone service underthe dual control of the Government and the National Tele- phone Company, Limited, it is expedient in the opinion of this Connell that the Government should take immediate steps to acquire the undertaking of the National Telephone Company, Limited, upon just and reasonable terms as between the nation and company's shareholders." Two new members were elected, and the annual meeting was fired for Monday, January 30th.</p>
      </div>
