        <p>WARRINGTON ASSOCIATION OF B INDS.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>This association held is annual tn-ering at the Town Hail on Ttosday oveaing. Mr. r. J. Dowd (president) occupied the hiir and there were al3o pre-elt—C cilor aeatun, M ssrs. Jos,on Taylor (hon. seer Wright, York, Di'.edfizlt.l, T. Szni;h, Armstrong, R.Snaildi and B. Wadsworth. THE BALANCE SHEET.</p>
        <p>The PaEsinewr mid toe palt;eu:.r, of t trance shePt whlch showed thAt en DecNnb..rl4th, 1897, t-le e waq a h.:l4mei h 3 ere tit of the AsioeiatiJa ot --‘212 4;81 ; 15 13 101 tiaa nevn handenl over a 3 to. orr,needi of Ott+ c:)ncert field in Hill3nd'4 neneli, and £56 lOs had born paid to bancl; playing in th rk,Ltvizg a ualance in the bw.ilt of £l5 7551. . _ _</p>
        <p>The PuERIDRNT ~id ts who4t aff sirs of toe 4.tStr:iia• tioa ape.i.treci to b. io a thorough y - tion. Fmn the statement supplied to him by Mr. Taylor it aponPred there were 45 punt io p,rfortn,:c last Tar end 35 of the e w,,ra by four barith ir, 9 ,unteer St. Paul's, Br and L?. ^atra of the Cross t;aad4. .1:' it had not lxicti for the bands natnid w ild not have b,en ,:nai.L-A to give tue pubiic the namuer of perfoemanc s taey had s right to exp .c-., az! was atrail it indic.ted a tailing off in ninsical itogiams ia 'Na - rMgt,o). They were no ditibt; well aware that he enter- [idled a high opini•ro indeed of the beLefitS of good mu •ic to the. town. Ile tho Light present extraordinary crbz! !or loo:hal!, eni bicycling wou!d am-mut for it He had nlie t. say agars; those pas'irnes ia rnodera!Tifi, but hA was gaits c,.rtain t in time the peop:e wool I re;:ret the time devcoert t those pursiiit-s. Of clurse that state of things was not con- fuel to Warrington alo-sr. Be was p!eased to 6ee that the Cotp :ration were going to taae powei in their Act for providing muQic performance in the park. That was as it shoal be T-e beticrt o' Warrington were a mast g,nerous and on e:fiih cof men and he hoped the Town Council wouid be sb e to give the bandsmen a is., for each week-day re7formauce. P-_,rsenally, he practiced what he pr.achtd, and when his bandsmen practised in the park he supplemented their money by giving them 6 t., each. Be was ex7.eedingly sorry to hear their worthy sfcrtary had decided to reeigtVhig, pot. They would al ackrow:eige that their success had been en'irely doe to Mr. Taylor. Be (Mr. Down) was personal!; inne: ted ti Mr. Taylar foe many things, and he Moat may how much they regre:ied his dec;stan, and how higlq relay appreciated his strvie.e to the sociation. Councillor BEATON remarkod that he was very pleased at the performances given by the !Darius in the park. rhe rrrformarcris gave much satisfaction to th 3 people. Be had no doubt; when the T.iwn Hall, Park and Baths Committee were in a posiriou to ly.lp them t i question of givin2 bandsmen a Is fur each performance would 131 fairly and honestly considered. ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>On the propasition of Mr. HReSSTRONO, secmded by Mr. T. Sams, Mr. Down was unanimously re-slec.ei president.—The following were re-electtd dents :—The Mayor (Alderman J E Wright), Mr A II Crosfield, Mr R A Naylor, Aldermea Kober te aud Bolton. Councillors Heaton, Cannel!, Mouks, and tie members, of the Town Hall, Park and Baths COMO2iCtO.3. The President proposed that Mr B Wadswoita he electen secretary to the association, in place of Mr Taylor, v:hr was retiring. Mr. cirmstrong seconded and reindrsed that Mr. Wadsworth was a thorough musician. They could not get a better man to fulfil the position. The proposition was carried. VOTES OF THANKS. • , Mr. TAYLOR propr,vd a vote of thinks he passed to the Town Hall, Park and Baths Committee and the Parr Hall Committee.—Mr. York seconded and t in motion was carried. Councillc.r HeiTON, in reply, said as a member of the Town Ball, Park and Baths Committee lie was very thankful for the hearty vote accorded. He was perhaps one of the oldest members of that Commitee. lie could Promise that the Commitee woald do their best for the bands in the future as in the past. Votes of thanks were also a'..corlel to the treasurer (Mr.Fairhurst), and the retiring secretary (Mr. Taylor). In acknowledgment of the vote, Mr. Taiman said it had been a pleasure to him to work for a body of men like the Warrington bandsmen. Unfortunately during the last year they hai lost four bands—one of wnich was Fleteher's and the otheri were Sunday school bands. It had been very difficult io procure bands to take the place of others, but Nwing to Mr. R. Smith, Mr. Armstrong, and the assistance rendeted by The Borough Band and League of the Cross Band the difficulty had been overcome. Be thought the perform- ances in the past year had been very creditable ones. It was with regret that he relinquished the post of secre- tary: it was not because he had not the same love for it but because he had not tbe time. He well knew Mr. Wadsworth would carry on the work in the same man— ner he had done. (Applause.) The meeting concluded with a vote of thanks to the- Chairman for his services.</p>
