      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>PEOPLE YOU KNOW.</head>
        <p>PEOPLE YOU KNOW.</p>
        <p>No. 2—THE POSTMAN.</p>
        <p>A rural postman (Mr. R. Parry, 12, John-street,Wins-. ford) writes: " I desire to add this uhinlicited testi— mony to the sterling qualities of Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa lam a rural postman, and expesed to all kinds of weather. I have used Dr. Tibbles' Vi-CoNla daily this last eleven months, and I believe thit I can do my work with less fatigue since I have used it. I can honestly recommend to my fellow pestmes Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Coc:aat for its sustaining qualities." Of late years many new manufacture's, rejo'cing.in a multiplicity of names, have beers brought before the public notice, but there are none which have bounded into favour so readily as has Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa. Throughout the country its salts have increased, with great rapidity, and an art;cle which only a few Year&amp; back was unheard of has now become one of the mot popular beverages of fhe people. Merle, and merit alone, is what is claimed for Dr. Tibbles' VI-Cocos, and the Proprietors are prepared to, send to any reader who names the Warrington, " Examiner " (a postcard will do) a dainty sample tin of Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa free and post-paid. There is magic in all th's. It is.a plain, honest, straiehtforward off-T. It is thne to :ntroduce the merits of Vi-Cocoa into every home. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, 83 a concen- trated form of nourishment and vitality, is invaluable ; nay, more than this, for to all who wish to face the strife and battle of life with greater endurance and more stritainei exertion, it is absolutely indispentahle. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa can be obtained from elt Chemists. Grocers and Stores, or from 60, 61 and 62. Bunbill Row,London,E.C.</p>
        <p>To AND FRO WITH MY CYCLR! AND CAM.ERI.'</p>
        <p>This was the title of a highly interesting lant•rn lecture delivered in connection with the Wycliff, Young People's Society, on Monday evening by Mr. Knowles, the hon secretary of the Warrington photographic Society. Mr. George Wallington in- troduced the lecturer, and spoke of the pleasure he had himself derivei from cycling. Mr.. Knowles prefaced his lecture with a few remarks on the additional pleasure to be derived by combining photography with cycling, and alleed to the vantages of of amateur photographers being connected with the Photographic Society. A number of beau- tiful slides were then shown, many of them de- picting scenes in the town and diqr7ct taken by the lecturer during his cycling tours. In addition some picturesque scenes in Wales and Cumberland, were thrown on the screen which were interesting memereces of holidays spent there. The lecturer - pleasantly described the views, and at the close bis services were acknowledged on the proposition of Mr. O'Brien, seconded by Mr. Jolley, and supported by Mr. R. Wyllie. There was a good attendance, the upper schoolroom being well filled.</p>
      </div>
