      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Ketterg to the Mittor.</head>
        <p>Ketterg to the Mittor.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Author; of letters intended tor publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor. Matter intend(d f3r publication must be written on one side only of the paper. Correspindents are strongly advised to wrlts briefly. A short letter has much greater chance of insertion than a long one.</p>
        <p>SIR J. T. BRUNNER AND THE EDUCATION OF NONCONFORMIST CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Sir,—four issue of the Bth inst. contained a long extract from a letter by the Rev. 7Canon Maitland Wood, published in the "Runcorn Parish Magazine." Canon Wood considers that the charges to which I referred in my letter of the 10th of December were effectually disproved at the inquiry over which he presided. " It was made as clear as the day that Nonconformists' children were not and never had been subjected to any unkindness or unfairness." If your readers reflect that the children who wete examined were confronted with the august tribunal of the managers of the school, knowing very well the partisan sympathies of that tribunal, and that the Nonconformists were entirely unrepresented on the court of inquiry, they will appreciate the reasons which prevent Nonconformists from shar- ing the Vicar's satisfaction with the procedure or implicit confidence in the verdict. There is not sufficient security for fair play to the children of Nonconformists at the hands of a governing body, from which Nonconformists are excluded, and the profound dissatisfaction with which Nonconformists regard the investigation in this particular case, is extended to the administra- tion of Voluntary schools in general. Foray part I hope that the Nonconformist min- isters of Runcorn will constitute themselves a com- mittee of vigilance o afford to'the children of Non- conformist parents such protection as can be secured by giving publicity to particular grievances and to net as a check upon an otherwise irresponsible ex- ercise of power.</p>
        <p>--- They may rely upon receiving any assistance which it may be in my power to give them.—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>JOHN T. BRUNNER.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON DIST RICT HIGHWAY BOARD.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of this Board was held at the Bewsey-street Chambers on Wednesday afternoon. Captain Withington presid-,d and there were also pro- sent: Messrs. Joshua Ball,Fairchugh,Eienry Thornton, T Halton, Clare, Rigby, A Garnett, with Mr. E A Whits (clerk), and Mr. J Thompson (surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>WATER WORKS EETEN-SIOR.</head>
        <p>WATER WORKS EETEN-SIOR.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this Committee the SURVEYOR (Mr. Thompson) said that no ices had been handed to him by the Clerk that application was intencbd to be made to Parliament in the ensuing ses3ion for an Act for the con- Btruction of new waterworks and other purposes in the County Palatine of Lancaster and the County of Chester. Be had therefore examined the deposited plansotad found that it was proposed to lay a main from the present Winwick Waterworks, across Wit Tworks-lane thence across the fi-lds to centre of Midilaton-lane near the Winwick and Middleton bound try, from thence to near Middleton lodge and from thence along Middleton Delph- lane to proposed new pumping station in field opposite Spa Well House-lane end. A main was also proposed to be lied from the Winwick Waterworks along Waterworks- 'Jane to Middleton-lane, thence along Middletm-lane to Winwick Post Office, thence from Winwick Post Office along Winwick Brow, Winwick-road to Longford Bridge. The laying of those mains would involve the cutting of deep and wide trenches ahng the aforesaid roads, and some provision should be made to ensureiproper restora- tion or trenches, and for that purpose he would recom- mend that the work be done under a specification,to be prepared by the Board and that the Board reserve the right to do the work themselves for and at the cost of the Warrington Corporation. Of course the Winwick- road was a main road and would be dealt with by the County Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>STATE 01 TEE ROADS IN SANKEY.</head>
        <p>STATE 01 TEE ROADS IN SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Mr. HALTON said he thought it was about time they had a roller in Sankey. The roads were in a fearful ttate. He had noticed that in other cases, with the ex- ception of Burtonwood,the roads had be?n repaired and rolled. In Sankey the roads hal been repaired but not rolled. With reference to Vacs dangerous place by Mrs. Crosby's in Back-lane, were they not, in duty, bound to keep that in order. Mr. Mormon Ido not think so. Mr. HALTON : There is scarcely room for a trap to pass. Mr. THOMPSON : It used to be much worse than it is at present. Mr. HALTON : I have been reqwsted by the 'Parish Council to bring the matter before the Board. The part of the road in a bad state extends from Mr. Cooke's farm as far as Mrs. Crosby's. Two traps could not pass together; they had to wait at certain sections. Mr. THOMPSON suggested that the pit be filled up from the time being and this was agreed to. HOOD LANE, GREAT SANKEY. At a meeting of the Finance and General Purposes Committee the Surveyor submitted a plan showing a strip of land containing 379 square yards, which he recommended the Board to endeavour to acquire trom Captain Hughes for widening the road. The Committee authorised the Surveyor to negotiate with Captain Hughes' agent. This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>WiiRRINGTON WOMMN'S LIBERA.L</head>
        <p>WiiRRINGTON WOMMN'S LIBERA.L</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>SOIREE AND BALL.</p>
        <p>The fourth annual soiree and ball under the aus- pices of the Warrington Women's Liberal Associa- tion was given in Parr Hall. on Tuesday evening. Though only of recent inception, this event has come to be regarded as one of the most popular social functions held in Warriegton during the winter season, and the modest price of the tickets generally ensures the presence of a goodly company. Two years ago the number present exceeded all an- ticipations, and some discomfort was the result,but like other people the Liberal wo.nen learn by ex- perience,and care is now taken to prevent a recurr- ence of the crush on that occasion&amp; is a somewhat difficult matter to so limit the number of tickets as to have an enjoyable gathering and at .the same time keep the financial balance on the right side, but this feat has been accomplished under the able directorship of the Ball Committee, with Mr. C. Jackson Holmes (as chair- man), Mr. W. Roberts (secretary), and Councillor Tinnion (treasurer). The dance on Tuesday even- ing was a great success from every point of view, the mixed company present all contriving to enjoy themselves immensely. Dancing commenced at 8 p.m., and was continued, with one interval, until 2 a.m., during which time a suitably arranged pr- gramme of 22 dances was gone through. Mr, R. H. Taarson's bind supplied the music, end it was a distinct feature of the evening's entertainment. The hall had been tastefully decorated by Mr. Ralph Johnson, who is to be congratulated on the success which his efforts realised.. Round the front of the gallery were hung prominently the well-known names of Morley, Rosebery, Harcourt,and Campbell- Bannermsn—suggestive of the vacant Liberal leadership. Mr. Arthur Browne undertook the arduous duties of M.C., and carried them out with an ability which effectively prevented the dances Ragging in the slightest. In the hands of Mrs. J. W. Hayward the c3tering was eminently eatisfac- tory, anti we should think all who desired it were able to obtain an elegant sufficiency. Altogether there were about 300 people present—a number which the hall comfortably accomodates. Mr. A. Crosfield was present dining part of the evening. The officials connected with the soiree and ball,besiobs tnose already mentioned, were :—Ball Committee: Mrs William Pierpoint, Mrs Henry Roberts, Mrs Clay), Mrs J Hill, and Mrs Cooke, and Messrs 3 J Beaman, Will am ilayes, Councillor Peacock, Councillor F R Robert 4, and Mar John Griffiths. Stewards :—Mr C J Holmes, Coun. cillor Tinnion, Mr Georze E Clay, Mr W Bibby, Mr J E Warren, Mr R Woods, Mr W Mounfield, Mr Gonlou Roberts, Mr J Griffiths, Mr Hill, and Mr 8 J Beaman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CEIIJRCFIES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CEIIJRCFIES.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Mr.lYonge presided at the service in connec- tion with th 3 above society, on Sunday. Be stated that he had received a reply from Lord Salisbury acknowlede- ing the receipt of the resolution sent to him on behalf of that society in favour of the Czar of RuFsia's rescript. Mr. Yonge r...lerred to the new publicatian "War against War" edited by Mr. Stead,fand advised those present to buy and read it. He also suggested that the working 'llea of Warrington should take up the qu3stion ot uni- versal disarmament and if possible arrange for a town's meeting to discuss the matter. The Rev. Toomas Willis of Manchester, was the sneaker, and delivered an excell- ent address ,full of good advice as to how th? year which bad just been ent,red upon stwuld be uttliied. Men should not aaow themselves to drift,but evc ryone should have a purpcse in life. Mr. Kent and Mr. 13:..swick also took part in the service.</p>
        <p>" LINSEED COMPOUND" (Trade Mark) for Coughs and Colds. of proven efficacy. 134 d. Sold by Chemists only.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>EA.RLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EA.RLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLARY.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting of the Hope of Earlestown lodge of Good Templars W. 13 held in the C. -opera- tive Hall on Monday evening. Bro. Cutler pre- sided. One candidate wai initiated. The pro- gramme which afterwards followed was supported by the Tranmere,lodge, Bro. F. Miller presiding. Songs, recitations, &amp;c., were given by the visitors and an interesting address was given by the chair- man. The monthly C3nvention was held at Ather- ton on Saturday. There was a good muster of representatives and a pleasant afternoon was spent, with the usual ceremony in the evening. EARLESTOWN WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIA.TION...</p>
        <p>Toe usual monthly meeting of the members of this Association was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, on Monday afternoon. Miss Allatt pre- sided, and there was a very good attendance of members. A paper was read by Miss Greenall on the higher education of women, with special refer- ence to an American lady, Miss Lucy Clarke, whose scruggles for an entry into Harvard College, and her trials whilst there were very lucidly brought out by the essayist. Her career as a lecturer and newspaper editor was also touched upon, in the view of showingthe dilibulties attending the search after learning on the part of women. The paper was c!everly written, and an interesting discussion fol- lowed. Refreshments were afterwards handed round, and an enjoyable meeting was spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>POLICE CASES-.</head>
        <p>POLICE CASES-.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall, Newton, oa Saturday morning before Mr. T. J. Gillespie, Mary Ann Lawlar of War- rington, and Mary earthy of Manchester, were each charged with soliciting in Earlestawn the previous night, and were sent to gaol for 14 days. Mr. Gil- lespie remarked that this offence was becoming too prevalent, and the next time anyone, who had been previously convicted was brought before him, he would send them to poll for a month with hard labour. P.C.'s Benson and Schofield proved the cases.—Bridget Murray of no fixed abode, for being drank and disord3rly was fined 53. and costs and committed to gaol for 14 days in defaalt.—At Earlestown on Monday before Mr. J. W. Emmett, William Langan of Alfred-street, Newton, was charged with being drunk in Newton on Saturday night and was fined 236 d and costs.—P.C. Robson proved the case. EA.RLESTOWN CONSERVATIVE CLUB.</p>
        <p>A special smoking concert was held in the Con- servative Club, Earlestovvn, on Saturday under the presidency of Mr. C. B. F. Borron. With one or two exceptions the artistes hailed from Warrington and the prngramme was very successfully sustained. The Warrington artistes included Messrs. R. H. Pearson (solo violin),ll. Bond 'cello) W. H. Payton (accompanist), and A. Griffiths (tenor). The fol- lowing was the programme: Trio (a)" Andantino" (b) "Serenade de Mandolines,"Messrs Pearson,Bond, and Payton; song,Mr. G. Wright ;violin solo "Scenes de Ballet," Mr. R. H. 'Pearson; song, "Good Com- pany," Mr. A. Griffiths; trio (a) "La Colotnbe," (b) "Gipsy Rondo," Messrs. Pearson, Bond, and Payton; humorous song, Mr. J. Huddleston; trie "Adigio and Allegretto" (Hymn of Praise) Messrs. Pearson, Bond, and Payton; song Mr. G. Wrigit ; violin solo Mr. R. H. Peari.on; song "Come into the garien Maud." Mr. A. Griffiths; humorous song, Mr. J. Huddleston. VIADUCT WORKS OFFICE STIFF DINNER. •</p>
        <p>The office staff engaged at the Wagon Department of the London and North-Western Railway Com- pany at Earlestown, held their ninth annual dinner on Saturday afternoon, at the Bee Hotel, Liverpool. Leaving Earlestown by 134 in a special train, the party numbering over 20, arrived in Liverpool shortly before 3 o'clock. The time preceding the hour of dinner was spent in a variety of ways, and at six o'clock they assembled at the Bee, and sat down to an ex:;ellently served dinner, which was thoroughly enjoyed. After the cloth had been re- moved, Mr. Thomas Ormsnd presided over the sub- sequent proceedings, Mr. C. W. Emmett occupying the vice-chair. The usual loyal toasts were proposed and duly hotioured, and the other toasts given and responded to were "The London and North-Western Railway I.,ompany," by the chairman ; " The fore- man," Mr. R. Riade ; "The °faze," Mr. Smalley; "The Visitors," Mr. J. H. Alderson. The following programme of songs was given during the evening "The Young Brigade,"" Oh, Oh, bear the wild wind blow," and "The B and el aro," Nine. [Use; "S tiling," "The Sentry," and "The Warrior Bold," Mr. S. Swann; "Lite is a river," and "They all love Jack," Mr. J. Owen; "The Midshipmite," Mr. C. W. Emmett; and a song by Mr. Joseph Swann. Mr. T. F. Roberts ably accompanied and a most pleasant evening was spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH IN THE STREET AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>James Boariman, 24 years of age, a labourer' of Platt-street, Parr, St. Helens, while walking towards his borne on Tumday night was obaeryel to fall to the ground. A man named Elam wentto his assistance,but found that Boardman was dead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>NEGRO'S HALLUCINATION.</head>
        <p>A NEGRO'S HALLUCINATION.</p>
        <p>Stephen N tthaliel Smith, a negro, whase add -els was given as " Jamaica " was charged at Si. He!ens on Monday as a lunatic. Detective Hughes said he found the man in Riven-street on Sunday afternoon acting in an extraor.lieary manner. He seemed to be under the impression that he was walleng about under the glowing sun of the tropics, and was undressing himself coin. pletely.—Dr. Chtllenor said he bad formed the opinion that Smith was a very hard-working and temperate man. He appeared to have ha I a severe attack of loft+. enzs, which had dis)rganised his mind. He should be sent ta Whiston Workhouse hospital for a fortnight. The magistr ttes md tha necessary order.—Dr. Challenor then anplied to the benc't for leave to give evidence, and get an order in another case of lunacy. The patient had been sent to the workhouse for fourteen days, but that had not proved sufficient, and he wanted the order evtended.—The Clerk : It would be unlawful to make such an order if the marl h not here.—Dr. Challenor asked if he could not give evidence even though the man was not there.—The bench said it was quite impossible for them t ) make an order in the absence of the prisoner elnennli. ATTEMPTED SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens Police Court on Monday,Pater Forshaw, of Frederick-itreet, was charged wit 1 attempting to commit suicide. Coief Constable Wood said that prisoner was a da3, wageman, and had worked at the Wnit 'cross Colliery, and had worked there up to DicemOer 24. He then commenced drinking heavily. He and his wife had been living apart since N ivirnber. and prisoner hai kipt his three children at his house in Frederick-strest. Mrs Forshaw went to live in the same streer,. On New Year's Day m irning prisoner's son James aged three years. went ups'airs and faund his father in a pool of blood on the bsdroorn fl )or. Mrs. Forshaw was cilled,and found her husband had cat his thro it with 3 razor. Dr. Unsworth was summoned, and sent the min to the Pr3vilenci Hospital. Prisoner had told him that morning that he had no recollection whatever of cutting his throat. He was in drink.but hi was very sorry for what he ha 1 done.—Mr H. L. Riley appoared for Forshaw, and sad that the cause of his appearing there was un-loub.;edly dompiitic trouble. Prisoner would tilo3; solemnly under- take that he would not again indulge in excessive drink- ing. Hi belonged to a most respectable f tinily, and his friends were in court and prepare i to take care of him and his chil Ireo until his family affairs were siittlel. The bennh agreed to diseherr. prisoner.</p>
        <p>~, .......••••=llll CHRISTA.DELPIIIAN LECTURE,</p>
        <p>Mr. Lee,of Warrington. -occupied the platform, in the Bold-street rooms on Sunday, his subject being "The eternal city : Rome or. Jerusalem, which ?" The lecture was appropriately preceded by the singing of a hymn commencing " For Zion's sake I will not rest," and the r iading of 60th Chapter o Isaiah. Toe lecturer that the term eternal city conveyed to most people the idea that Rome was meant which was correct—so far as current expressions were concerned—but an examine- tion of the title showed that that city was to be destroyed, while Jerusalem as was shown would be made by God a praise in the earth. After giving a brief outlima of the history of the two cities, Mr. Lee obs3rved that the power of Rome or Romanism was distinctl y foretold by the prophets as terminable at a given period and that the tem- poral power did cease at the time expected,in 1870. The same prophets, however, had distinctly intim- ated that the potentates of the earth would give their power into the hands of Rome for a brief period which would be employed in fighting against Christ and the acceptei Saints. The result will be most disastrous to the enemies of Christ, and Russia who had and did oppose and persecute the Jews more than any other nation,would be the head of the confederencv which would then be overthrown. Rome and her daughters would afterwards he destroyed. Je-u4a- lem on the other hand would be made the metro- polis of the earth, with Christ a king. They were commanded to give the Lord no rest till he estab- lish, and till he make Jerusalem a praise in the end, and if his hearers were desirous of having a participation in the gaol time coming on the earth they would have to comply with the conditions which God had Rivini. in His work.</p>
        <p>--AK fci CUNNINII--H—A.3l'B HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATICRS.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, JANUARY 20, ISO 9</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, JANUARY 20, ISO 9</p>
        <p>- DEATH OF MR. JOHN BILL. A well-known and familiar figure in Earlestown ' and Newton, in the psrson of Mr. John Ball was claimed by the hand of death on Sunday morning last. For some time past Mr. Ball has been in fail- ing health, and since the death of his wife nearly 12 months ago, he has appeared to be very despondent. Ile has been subjected to a series of attacks of epilepsy, the last being of a serious character, which left him in a prostrate condition. The disease as- sumed a violent forns.and Mr. Ball relapsed into such a condition that he had to be placed under restraint. He graduatly sank, however, and died, as stated, at the age of 60 years. The deceased has resided in the Newton township for about half a century and during that period has had a very varied career. He served his apprenticeship with Messrs. McCorquodale and Co., at Newton, as a compositor, and was for many years employed by the firm. In his younger days he was a noted cricketer, and as a bowler was always viewed with a certain amount of alarm by opposing teams. He was intimately connected with the Newton club, and for many years acted as secretary. From a commentary pub • lished some time ago, we find it stated that the Newton Cricket Club was formed about 1859 or 1860 and owed its existence mainly to the late Captain Birley, the Fairclough family and Mr. John Ball. The Warrington club were rivals of the Newton club and many time i Warring- ton had to go down before Newton. The main cause of their defeat in many of the matches was the insidious bowling of "Old John Ball" as every- one called him then. Probably no greater cricket enthusiast than this veteran ever lived, and at 60 years of age he was still playing cricket. He bowled fast under hand" grubs," with a diabolical twist, and with a splendid bail ball occasionally thrown in. If this bowling was not of the most approved and scientific style, it was certainly most destruc- tive, and the wickets of the Warrington and other clubs went down before it like nine pins. He has not only figured in Newton'matches, but has played with first class clubs, and has scored numerous suc- cesses. So much for John Ball as a cricketer. In his spare moments he 20 years ago acted as the Newton correspondent for several local nem- papers, and on severing his comtection with Mc- Corquodale and Co., he received the appointment of school attendance officer, under the old Newton Improvement Commissioners, and hp occupied the position up to the time of his death. He was also at one time 4 hay and straw dealer, and officiated as distributoeof the Haydock Colliery explosion fund which was instituted in 1878. The funeral took place on Tuesday. and the body was interred in the cemetery attached to St. Peter's Church, Newton. The body was followed to the grave by Mr Herbert and Mr Joseph Ball (sons), Mr Walsh (son-in-law), Mr T Ormand, Mr W W Shirley, Mr H Houghton, Mr J Crouchley, Mr Dick, Mr W H Hindley, Mr Gregory, Mr T Martindale and others. The service was performed by the tiev W Rowlands (curate of St. Peter's) and the Rev T R Matthews (vicar of Earlestown).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>NEWTON DIVISION ELECTION. COLONEL BILKING fON RETURNED ITNOPeOSED.</head>
        <p>NEWTON DIVISION ELECTION. COLONEL BILKING fON RETURNED ITNOPeOSED.</p>
        <p>On Mmday morning, Mr. E. T. Wilson, acting under sheriff sat as Retorning Officer to receive nominations for the election of a member of Parliament for the l‘lsv- ton Division. Altogether 14 nomination papers were handed in by Mr. W. Holland-Owen, agent for Colonel Pilkington, who was described as Richard Pilkington, Glass Manufacturer, Riinford Hall, Near S. Helens They were as follows :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Sr, HELENS.</head>
        <p>Sr, HELENS.</p>
        <p>Proposer. Joseph Massey; seconder, Henry Forster. Proposer, William Pilkington ; seconder, William Lee Pilkington.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Proposer, Robert Davies • sec mder. James Tinsley. Assen- tors, Ralph Johnson, Charles Holland, James Wood, Richard Whitlock Rowson. Edward Kirkconnel, William Dooley, James Hepherd, Robert Heaton, Joseph Webb, William John Forshaw, John Hayes. BILLINGE (CHAPEL EvD). Proposer, Thomas Heald; seconded, William Melling. ASHION.</p>
        <p>Proposer, William Valiant ; seconder, Thomas Crompton. Assentors, Charles Henry Astle, WilliAm Painter, Andrew Molynewc, Thomas Crompton, jun., William Lowe. Samuel Brook Coburn, Joseph Stevenson, John Taylor, James George Jones, William Swift, Daniel Baldwin Marsh, William Thornley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>WINWICIC.</head>
        <p>WINWICIC.</p>
        <p>Proposer, Oswald Henry Leyeester Penrhyn (Bev.Canon) ; seconder, Rabert Stone. Assentors. Henry Isaac Wadeson, Reginald Owen. Joshua Ball, William Massey, Joseph Swin- ton, John Wright Knowles, Thomas Warburton, William Williams.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>SANKEY.</head>
        <p>SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Proposer. William Dickson Houghton; seconded, William Dimpier Jeans. Assentors. William Dickson Houghton, John Ho,-.ghton William Houghton, William Henry Leather- land Gorse, Peter Pownall. Harry Cartwright, Thomas Halton, Peter Boardman, Frederick Joynrm, Benjamin Hobson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>If AYDOCK</head>
        <p>If AYDOCK</p>
        <p>Proposer, Theodore Drayton Grimk6-Drayton ; seconder, Joha Robinson. Assentors, John jAckson. Francis Ireland (Rev.), Pr±ter Rainford, Robert Kenyon, Thomas Greiory, Albert Rimmer, Henry Wiswell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>GOLBORNE.</head>
        <p>GOLBORNE.</p>
        <p>Proposer, Simon Stubbs Brown ; seconder. Henry Worsley. Assentors, Thomas ktolyaeux, Nathan Hurst, Robert Davies Thomas Watson, John Rutter, John Abbot, Thomas Shaw, John Lowe.</p>
        <p>ECCLESTON.</p>
        <p>Proposer, Duncan ISlcKeehnie ; seconder, Thomas W.lker•</p>
        <p>NEWTON (TOWN HALL).</p>
        <p>Proposer, Thomas John Gillespie ; seconder, Joseph Long- sill,* Woad. Assentors, Richard Barton: Samuel Leather, Frederick Arthur Frost, John Simpson, John 'Ulmer, William Charles Horrobin, William Rimmer. James Liptrot. BALNH ILL. Proposer, George Bashby ; seconder, William Gamble. EABLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Proposer. Charles Bell Ford B3rron ; seconder, Arthur Handyside. Assentors. Rich ird Walton, Orlando Bridgman, Thomas Haselden. Rdward Fairclough, William Thompson. John White, Toomas Tunstall, Alfrel Wrigley.</p>
        <p>RIINFORD.</p>
        <p>Proposer, William Richardson, seconder, John Boardman. At a quarter to two o'clock Mr. Wilson declared Colonel Pilkingt-m, of Ralnford Hall, Rainfori, St. Helens, duly elected member of Parliament for the New- ton Diviaion of Lancashire. Tne announcement was re- ceived with applause. Colonel Pilkington, M.P., moved a vote of thanks to the Returning Officer for the duties he had performed. Mr. T. D. Grimke-Drayton seconded, and athie i that he hoped future returning officers would have a similarly easy taAk te perform. _(Applause.)</p>
        <p>The motion was carried: A nualbsr of rockets were scut up after the declara- tion of the Returning Officlr. CONSERVATIVE MEETING AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The last of the series of meetings which Colonel Pilkington has been holding througheut the Division Was held at Newton on Mend ay night. There was a fair attendance and Mr. T. J. Gillespie presided. There were also present Messrs. R. Stone, T. D. Grimke-Drayton, W. Holland Owen, R. Barton, J. Massey, &amp;a. The Chairman said his first duty would be to read a couple of telegrams he hal received. One from L 3rd Newton read :—" Congratulate you upon unopposed re- turn." and another " Heartiest congratulations from Atherton Bros on your parliamentary return."He had to remind them that this bye-election had been caused by the removal of their late member to the Upper House, owing to the sad and sudden death of his father, Tney all knew the respect and esteem their late member was held in, and how sadly they mused him. They had to submit to the ways of providence and try to fill up the vacancy in the best way they could. This, he thought, they had been particularly fortun- ate in doing, and they bad secured the services of an able politician in Colonel Pilkingeon. (Applause.) A more able man, he thought, they could not hive. The greatest pleasure he had in ocauoying the chair that night was the opportunity it gave him of congratulatin2 their new member, and it also gave him the opportunity of congratulating them that they had been able to find such an able member to occupy the position which was held by Mr. Leen. (Cheers.) Colonel Pilkington had the advantage of being a local man. He Was also a thorough good business man, being a very large em- ployer of labour, a partner in one of the largest eon- cerns in the country,and therefore they might take it for granted that their interests in the way of business would be well Looked after. He had taken a keen interest in polities. He had made a careful study of them, and he (the chairman) asked them to wish him many years of health and happiness,and for many years represent them in Parliament. (Cheers.) li,at the general election.their opponents attempted t 3 run a man, if they could find one, he could promise they would fight shoulder to shoulder and carry their candidate at the bead of the poll by a larger majority than they did Mr. Legh. (Cheers.) Co'onel Pilkineton said he had to thank alrthe electors in the Newton Division. That was the first thing he had to do because nobody had voted aeainst him, and it appeared to him he was there as the representative of the whole ot the Newton Division. He b +Revel it was true he was their M.P., and as such seeing he had not come direct from Westminster, he had no report to give and as to promisee he had none to make. He referred to the very strong post'on of the Government, which during his candidature he had continually told them he should most loyally support. (Hear, bear.) He gave a brief exposition of the condition of the Cabinet, and said if it was backed up solidly, it could settle the three serious questions before the country, viz: The Chinese, the Transvaal and the French difficulty. In coeclus'oe he said it was not much to say that when he was de- clared the member for the Newton Division by the Re- turning Officer that it was the proudest moment of his life. (Cheers.) Mr. It. Stone moved a reseolulion of congratulation upon the unopposed return of 0 11 once Pilkington as their representative in the House Ov Cemm nu, assuring him of their hearty support in the future. Mr. T. Grimlas-Drayton seconded, and said he had re- ceived, as Cnairman of the association, the following letter from Lord Newton. " I regret that for private reasons I am unable to attend your meeting, but I wish to offer my congratulations upon the unopposed return of Colo lel Pilkington. This most satisfactory result is no doubt due to the present demoralised condition of radicalism, but it is also due to the excellent organisation of our party in the Newton Division, which has been so carefully maintained by yourself and other gentlemen, and to the efficiency of which Mr. Holland Owen has so greatly contributed. In nay opinion the Constituency can have no better representative in Parlia- ment than Colonel Pilkington. He has for a long time taken a prominent and honourable pert in local and municipal affairs. He is universally respected for his public spirit, energy, and generosity. He is intimately acquainted with the Industrial needs of the district, and finally be resides in the Division. I cannot conceive any better qualificatien than he pessesses and I sincerely hope that he may long continue to be the member for the Newton Division, being firmly convinc,d that the electors could have found none better fitted to reprssent their interests. I dare say that on some future o'm melon I shall have an opportunity of thanking you and other friends for their support of me in the past. I remain Yours very truly, NEWTON." Mr. C. B Burton sepperted the resolution and said that was what be would call a maiden election, as in that division they were not accustomed to such and they felt somewhat diappointed that it had been such a quiet walk over, but they couli le,ep their powder dry and be ready on another occasion if necessary. (Applause.) The resolution was unanimously adopted. Votes of thanks to Mr. Holland-Owen and the Chair- man brought the meeting to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>SANKEY HARRIERS.</head>
        <p>SANKEY HARRIERS.</p>
        <p>The above harriers held their ordinary run from headquvrtorsi, the, 1319 up B.)tel, on Saturday. The weather being favourable for cross-country run- ning, 25 runners took advantage of it. Trail was hid by G. Basketter and F. Whittaker, over seven miles of heavy country, in the direction of the Bold estate. The slow pack went out at 330, fol- lowed by the fast pack ten minutes later. The first man to arrive home was E. Davies, G. Perdue being second, and G. Dwerryhotm third, all of the slow pack; whilst of the fast pack.G. W. Eastwood arrived first, T. Devlin second, and J. Potts third. The fixture for to-day Saturday is an inter-club run with Ashton-in-Nfakerfield Harriers.</p>
