      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>CH4RG,4I OF-EMBEZZLEHENT AGAINST A WARRINGTON TRAVELLER.</head>
        <p>THE CH4RG,4I OF-EMBEZZLEHENT AGAINST A WARRINGTON TRAVELLER.</p>
        <p>TRIAL AT THE LIVERPOOL QUARTER SESSIONS. George Parr. fornserly a traveller in the employ of Messrs. noydel Brothers, tailors, Sankey-street, War- ringion, where he is well-known,was brought up at the Liverpool C Linty Quarter S3slions, on 'Tueiday before Sir W. B. Forwood (chairman) and other magistrates, charged as follows :—(1) A; the b'rouge of Wa,rington, being then a servant in the employment of Boydell Brothers, unlawfully, wilfully, and with intent to de- fraud, dii, on the 251 n Juae, 1897, falsify a certain book, to wit, a day b.ok journal : on the 25 h July, 1898, a certain book, to wit, a ledger : and on the 17th January, 1895. did omit to enter a material par- tic liar in a daily takings book which said b)oks then belonged to the said Boylell Brothers. (2) At War- rington, on the 21ch March, 1898 then being servant to Boyden Brotlyri, felonionily and fraudulently did em- oezzle and steal the sum of £3 is. in money; and within six calendar months thereafter, to wit, on the sth May, 1893. the further sum of £3 3s. in money; and on tic 6th June. 1898, the farther sum of £3 10s. in mow, re- ceived by him on the account of his said masters. Mr. Rigby Swift (instructed by Mr. Arthur Brawne) applared for the prosecution,and Mr. S3gar (instrty.:ted by Messrs Jenkins and C).) for the prisoaer, who looked very ill and careworn as he sepped into the dock. The prisoner, who is 45 years of age, was described in the calendar as "well educated." Re pleaded guilty in a firm voim to the in lictments. In opening the case, _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift—eaid that the prisoner in 1889 entered the employment of Messrs. Boedell Brothers, who carried on bu iiness as clothiers and tailors, and had shop, in vari- on; parts of the country, and at Warrington, where prisener was appointed their manager. From 1889 to 1897 he went on all right, as far as anyone could tell, and he acquired a good reputation and obtained the confidence of his employere4 but in December 1896, he bec sine, for a time at any rate, dissipated, and upon one occasion he gave himself up at the Police Station, stating that he hid been robbing tee firm. Me. Boydell went down to the Police Station, and had an interview with prisoner. Negotiations were carried on for some days, and Mr. Boydeel arranged to give him another chine°, took him back into his employment, but in an inferior position, and instead of manager he went back as sales- man. By that means no money passed through his bands, and he was kept from that temetation. But. although he had been taken back again, and had been given another chance, it was discovered that be was still robbing his employers. ' He went to people who had known him before, and obtained from them cash to pay to his employers. His system was to collect accounts, to give a receipt in his own name, and then t o falsify the ace ,unt books. Upon one oceasioa be go t £8 4e from Mr. Greavea, and in order to hide what he had done on the bill again going out te Greaves he wrote to him telling him that it had been sent in mis- take, and that be would call round and 830 him about it and by that means he prevented the customers going to Boydell's shop to complain that he had received a second account for a paid bill. Whilst Parr was with Messrs Beydell as manager he had the sole charge of the books, and he conducted a most ingenious system of falsifying them, by which altogether he was enabled to obth.in without discevery something like £250. Mr. Seger said there were several points in this sad case which the Bench would consider it their duty to weigh carefully. The prisoner himself had admitted that he bad gone wrong in his accounts ; and up te that time there was no suspicion at all that anything was the matter. He bad become dissipated, and, as counsel put it, he found be had been spending his master's money, but he bad spent 80013 of it in treating people who were customers. The history of this case was very peculiar. Parr started the business in Warringten with great suc- cess ten years ago. Beydelle were then unknown in Warrington, and he as traveller and cutter out started the len4ness in the small house in whi h he lived. There the customers came to be tried on, and for a couple of years Boydells were represented by a man who increased the number of customers so consider- ably that at length they opened a shop in one of the leading thoroughfares, and the prisoner was made manager. He was paid a certain wage and commission on the business ; he was in fact master of the situecion in every way. He collected the accounts, got the cuseomere, measured them, tried the goods on, cave the firm satisfaction, and brought in large profits. That went on fur years, and it was not till January of the last year that it was discovered that prisoner had been going wrong. He (Mr. Seear) suggested that the prisoner bad been in the habit of treating customers with drink and then got into a habit of drinking himself, ' which was extremely difficult for him to resist. His wire had all the money, and as he had none himself he was under a strong temptation to put some in his own pocket, on the occasions referred to in the indictment. In conclusion, Mr. Seger said he hoped the bench would treat this as a first offence. Let the prisoner go out on his own recognizences to be called soon if necessary for judgment. He would be glad to call the wife who would give the Bench a history of the case; Mr. Seger then called Mrs. Parr.</p>
        <p>Sir William B. F3rwood.—Wa don't require her evi- denca.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Regar.—l couH call the solicitor in the case. who krows thlt the prisoner was a very abll and popular man.</p>
        <p>Sir Wil'iam Forwood (to the prisoner) : George Parr, you have pleaded guilty to having for a long series of years systematically defrauded your employers. Your learned coun.el has made an appeal to us to deal with you under the First Offenders Aet,but this is a very serious off 3ncs. You have not yielded to the sudden im- pu'se of the moment, but have carried on these frauds tor s-me three os, four years, and on one occasion your employers condonel what you had done and gave you another chan-e. But you did not avail yourself of the chanee, and went on with the robberies. Therefore the case is a very s!rilus one, and we feel that we cannot lis'en to the appeal of your counsel. At the same time we shall take into consideration the fact that this is your first appeara ce in a court of law, and also that you have been in /2.a0l three or four weeks. The sen- tence of the CoOt is that you will have to be imprisoned for four calen Jar months with hard labour. _</p>
        <p>WIRRAL AND BIRKENHEAD AGRICULTURAL SOCIETY'S SHOW.—At a meeting of the Executive Committee held at Birkenhnad on Tuesday it was unanimously relolved to hold the Annual Show on the 6th and 7th Septemher. The Royal Lancashire (late Manchester and Liverpool) :4110W is 1-eing held at Liverpool, in August, and several other leading societies have already fixed their dat-s, therefore rather than clash with them the committee de- cided to revert to the old dat?s for this year's show. On and aFter the 16th instant the society's offices will be at 6, Hamilton Square, Birkenhead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-01-20_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>MARRIAGE OF COLONEL J. L. WOOD.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF COLONEL J. L. WOOD.</p>
        <p>There was a large gathering at St George's Church, Hanover Square, London, on Wednesday to witness the marriage of Colonel Joseph Langshaw Wood, of The Priory, Newton-le-Willows, Lancashire and Miss Ellen Hargrave Simpson, daughter of the late Robert Walker Simpson, of Armley Grove, Armley, Leeds, and niece of the late Miss Hargrave of Willaston House, Harrogate. The officiating clergy were the Rev. Alfred Thornton, of St. Peters Church, Eaton-square, and the Rev. T. G. Crosse, Rector of S. Alphege with All S lints', Canterbury. The church was beautifully decorated with white flowers and palms, and the service. fully choral. • The bride who was given away by her broiter Mr. James Simpson wore a lovely dress of white satin It had a frill of lace down the front, and carried round the edge of the skirt headed with two bouillonees of clafon. The bodice was covered with guipure. with a soft vest of chiffon, spangled with silver and embroidered panne revers. Elbow sleeves of guipure with broidered cuffs of panne. Full Court train in the richest duchesse satin lined with silk, and trimmed with chiffon. She wore a beautiful veil of old Limerick lace, the same which had been worn -by her mother on the occasion of her marl Lige; a diamond necklace and crescent the gift of the bridegroom,and diamond stars and brace- let, and carried a white shower bouquet of orchids, roses, lilies of the valley, with white heather and .orange blossoms. The brides- maids, Miss Edith E. Leach (cousin of the bride) and Miss M. Clarke (cousin of the bride- groom), ware dresses of pink corded silk, pink moire sasties and paste buckles, the bodices of the dresses having a soft chiffon vest, and trimmed with string coloured lace, and black velvet picture hats lined with pink. The bride's court train was borne by the Miss Verona and Master Ardern Wood (niece and nephew of the bridegroom and Geoffrey Simpson (nephew of the bride) their costumes being of white satin of the period of Charles I. The three bridesmaids, who carried shower bouquets of pink carnations and lilies of the valley, were presented with diamond brooches by the bridegroom, and the two pages with pearl pins by the bride. Mr. Stewart McCorqudale acted as best man. At the reception afterwards held at Limmers Hotel, Mrs. Leach, cousin of the bride, wore a black satin dress, embroidered with steil and bonnet to match, Russian sable cape and bou- quet of mauve orchids. Mrs. Fred Wood, sister-in- law of t.te bridegroom, wore a lovely heliotrope silk gown trimmed with silver lace and Russian sables, velvet picture hat to match and bouquet of lilies of the valley and violets.</p>
        <p>Liter in the afternoon the bride and bridegroom left for Folkestone en route for the Riviera. The bridb's going away dress was of dark green cloth, with cream satin bodice trimmed with cream lace and paste buckles. dark green coat trimmed with chinchilla fur with muff to match and large black velvet picture hat turned up with pink.</p>
      </div>
