        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. RUGBY - FOO I' BALL . Another two points dropped at home ! It is really deplorable. Warrington =not beat Leigh on the Wild- rspool ground. That was evidenced oi Saturday when the visitors ob:ained the verdict by 5 points to three-the same mergin as in the Runcorn match. Of course there is a lot f hard luck about being beaten by a goal kick,and this has been Warrington's unhappy fate on several occasions. It is rarely indeed that Warring- ton win by the goal kick. Ae a matter of fact there is not a man in the team, or in the two teams, who can be reliel upon to land a goal from an easy position. This undoubted weakness has cost Warrington dear on many an occesion in the past, and will do so again in the future. Take all risme Leiga were just ab iut two points the better team, and impressed the Warrington crowd with the vigorous and clever game they played all through. They might have been in the running for the championship to jadge by their de:ermination to win. Se far Warrington have gone under on their own turf to Oldham, Broughthn Rangers, Runcorn, and Leigh, and up to Saturday the record in this respect was the same as lest season's. Leieh hive notched on addi- tional point hovvever,and so have Swinton, as compared with their performances last season. This is far from being eatiefetory. It is progress we want, not retro- eresjon. From a spectator's point of view the game on Satur- day was a treat. The teams were evenly balenc d,and right up to the close the issue of the contest was in doubt. From end to end the struggle went, and the for- tunes of the game fluctuated every minute. The fine day too, made open play inevitable, and there was not five minutes of (dulness throughout the match. Tile first hall of the game produced no scoring,so that the advan- tage lay with Leigh when they crossed over with a clean sheet, and got the wind at their banks. Warrington really ought to have secured a point of Some sort before the interval, and worked hard for it, but the defence of the visitors was superior to the home attack. It was well on in the second half when the first score came, awl Leigh were the sc)rers. Molyneue seized a grand opportunity atid taking a cool drop near the War- riugton 25 line sent the oval over the cross-bar with a grand shot. The cheering showed p'ainly that there was a goodly number of Leignites present. Stung by this reverse the homesters made a terrific effort and drove the invaders to their own citadel. Trick after trick was tried, only to be foiled, but at last "Snack's " invaluable head-piece proved too much for the Leighires. Securing the ball well in the visitors' half he punted s'raieht to the left wing where Cross and Fish took up the ball, and the last named raced in amid wild enthu- siasm. The pent-up excitement of the crowd was vented in hurrahs and the waving of hats and caps galore. Fish's brilliant dash cost him dear,for he sustained a con- tusion of the shoulder which will keep him out of the field for a fortnight. After being doctored by the amoulance men be resumed play and was of considerable use. The od is now seemed in favour of Warrington, for with only tee minutes to play they hell a teed of one point. Leigh, however, did n)t despair and being fresher than the home lot they obtained the upper hand again. Date gay them their chance by making a bad pass to Harris which was intercepted at ceatre by T. Davies, who rustled to within 20 yards of the line. Once there and the visitors made a desperate effort. Fortune favoured them for the ball was fumbled behind the home forwards, and Arrowsmith scooped it up and rushed over. To say Warrington were oishearteuel does nut convey any adequate description of their feelings. They were simply crushed, aed baked it. Yet Leigh had to defend again b lore the close, but they did it su- perbly. --- - There is no doubt that Waningt on should have won after getting the lead, and they were greatly at fault in diving Leigh such a soft opportunity. But it is easy to find excuses ! Toe fact remains that Leigh were slightly the best team. They had an unmistakable pull at half back,where Melyneux and Dunbavio were nearly irresis- tible at times. Their reputatien as goal-droppers WAS justified, and after Dunhavin had just failed, Mo'yneux neatly accomplished the trick. Bate did rather the much dodging at times, but he deserves all credit for the try wnich Fish completed,and nearly as much blame for the try Leigh got. Ashurst played a sound, dete mined game and watched his two opponents closely. Forward Leigh held a slight advantage at the close, the men last- ing better than Warrington. In punting too the visitors beat Warrington severely, but that is nothing new. I must give the home team credit for playing a great game. Every man was on his mettle, and all did well. The positions of the clubs in the Lancashire Senior Competition and the results of matches played are as follows : For. Against. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P P. Salford 20...15... 3... 2 17...45-169...19 13- 77...32 BroughtonHang. 19...15.. 4... 0 32...52-221... 9 13- 57...30 Oldham 18...14... 4... 0...24 75-273... 9 10- 48...28 Widnes 17...12 .. 3 .. 2 ..‘?3 29 -133...13 13- 65...26 Leigh 20...13... 7... 0...29 25-136...11 25- 97...26 *S wintou „„ 1e..i2... 4... 2...23 41-169 ~10 8- 44...24 Runcorn 17... 8... 7... 2...10 18 - 64...13 22- 92...18 Warrington 17... 8... 8... 1...10 23- 89...13 27-107...17 St. Helens lB. 7.. 8.- 3-20 ac--lia..lT Rochdale HornetslB... 5...10... 3...16 12- 68...26 41-175...13 Tyldesley 29... 2...14... 4...11 14- 64...23 54-208... 8 Wigan 19... 3...15... 1...14 11- 61...32 34-7166.- 7 Stockport 17... 2...14... 1...12 14- 65...35 50-220... 5 Morecambe lB. 1...16... 1... 4 7- 29...29 50-208... 3 *Swinton have had two points deducted for breach of professional rules. The League championship is now in a very interest- ing state, as the four top clubs are practically all in the running. Broughton Rangers seem to have been throw- ing their chances away of late, and on Saturday last their third match in succession, succembing te Rochdale Hornets on the Broughton ground. As matters stand Salford, Oldham, and the Rangers all possess a chance of victory. The Rangers have to , play one more game than the New Barns lot, and are I I tied to win it to be on perfectly level terms with Salford, I whilst 011 ham have two extra games to make up, and also are compelled to win them to be en an even footing with the New Barns lot. Where Sal- ford's superiority of pesition comes in force the most is that games already won are of more value than those in prospect, as accidents will happen even to teams Lao strone as that representing Spindledom. Another point very favourable to the Borough club is that three of their most difficult tasks, the games with Widnes, Broughton Rangers, and Swinton are all on the ground which adjoins the racecourse. As a set-off thee meet Oldham at Watershed- dings, and the team hailing from Spindlenom have something to fear when they invade the dominions of Widnes, Runcorn, and Swinton, all of whom have to be marked dangerous. The task set before the Ringers is also of a more difficult character than those of their near and dear opponents, as they have to face the music of Widaes, Salford, and Swinson, all away. Taking all these things into consideration, Sal- ford stana the best chance of winning the championship. --- Another away match is on the fixture card for this Saturday, Warrington having to make the journey to Tyldesley. Here, as at 'Wigan, I think they should win, having already beaten the " Bongers " by about 20 points. Only two wine so far stand to the credit of Tyldesley, bat they are the champion drawists of the League. Four times have they finished up level with their opponents. They are, at all events, a sturdy lot, and Warrington will have to play for all they are worth to get the better of the encounter. ----- The vacancy caused by Fish's injury has teen filled by Duckworth, who is playing a dashing game for the "A" team. In my opinion he is spoiled by having to fill so many positions. Still his ability to play either half back or three-quarter makes him a player of great usefulness. This will be his first appearance on the wing. The " A "team found themselves in rather a warm shop at Leigh on Saturday and it was only in the last ten minutee that they won the game. Leigh were the first to score, and held their lead until near the close, when R Bate and Nelson scored tries for Warrington. Leigh had their bast team up and played a new half- back named Robinson, late of Barton, who naade a most satisfactory debut. He bids fair to be an excellent understudy for Molyneux and Dunbavio. The results of matches played and the positions of clubs in the Lanceeshire " A " team league are a 3 fel- low :- For. Against. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. P Salford 15...13... 2... 0... 26 58...216... 3 12... 42...26 Warrington 12...10... 1... 1... 9 26... 95... 6 6... 30...21 Oldham ...... 12... 9... 3... 0... 21 42...168... 9 8... 42...18 Broughton 11ang12... 7... 4... 1... 9 36...136...10 17.- 71...15 Whines .. 11.- 7... 4... 0... 18 33...135... 4 8... 35...14 Swinton 12 6.. 4.. 2.. 17 31...127... 7 21... 77...14 Rochdale Has 15... 6... 9... 0... 15 23... 99...23 45...181...12 Stockport .. . 12... 5... 7... 0... 6 18... 66... 7 24... 92...10 Runcorn 11.. 2.. 7. 2... 5 12... 46...14 35...133... 6 Leigh .. 12. 1.11... 0... 5 10... 40...26 56...220... 2 Tyldesley --- Leigh Shamrocks were unable to fulfil the return fix- ture with Fletcher Russells en Saturday lact and sent Bedford St. Josephs as substitutes. The Engineers appear to have wreaked vengeance for their disappoint- ment by beating the visitors by 29 points to nil. The home team played a good game throughout, the passing of the backs being very pod. They seem to have cap- tured a good youth in Sutton who hails from Runcorn, and we think he will make his presence felt to a good many teams as aeon as he settles down to the Engineers style of play. Radford and Lawless seem to play better than ever they did with the premier team and the half backs work well together.</p>
